(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4a.38f64d0d0a.js"]={"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":1,"stem":"A psychiatrist is preparing to interview a 4-year-old child about recent behavioral changes at home. Which interviewing modality is most appropriate for this age?","choices":{"A":"Play-based assessment with puppets or dolls, questioning them rather than the child","B":"A fully structured, closed yes-or-no format, borrowed directly from adult interviewing","C":"A chronologic narrative interview, relying on the child's verbal self-report","D":"An entirely open-ended interview, offered without structure or any play props","E":"Direct confidential engagement, conducted without any parental presence involved"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The use of puppets and dolls with children younger than 6 years of age is often an effective way to elicit information, particularly if questions are directed to the dolls rather than to the child, consistent with symbolic play being the appropriate modality at this developmental stage. A fully structured, closed yes-or-no format is not developmentally appropriate for a 4-year-old, who typically cannot construct a chronologic narrative. Relying solely on verbal self-report ignores that young children reveal more through symbolic play than conversation. An entirely open-ended interview with no structure or props would overwhelm a young child rather than engage them. Direct confidential engagement with no parental involvement is the adolescent-appropriate approach, not the young-child approach, and confidentiality for a young child does not work this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Symbolic play with puppets/dolls, directed away from the child, is the developmentally matched interviewing technique for children younger than 6. COMLEX: Age-graded interviewing technique (play-based young child, structured school-age, direct confidential adolescent) is a core pediatric patient-centered communication competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into age-graded pediatric confidentiality and mandatory reporting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is interviewing an 8-year-old about a recent stressful family event. The child gives only shoulder shrugs to open-ended questions. Which technique is best suited to this school-age child?","choices":{"A":"Entirely open-ended questions, offered with no structure at all for this age","B":"Simple closed yes-or-no questions, used exclusively for the entire interview","C":"Partially open-ended, multiple-choice questions, paired with drawing or the squiggle game","D":"Puppets and dolls, directed away from the child, with symbolic play as the medium","E":"A fully structured respondent-based interview, allowing no clinical interpretive flexibility"},"correct":"C","explanation":"School-age children respond best to partially open-ended questions with some multiple-choice structure; entirely open-ended questions can overwhelm a school-age child who cannot construct a chronologic narrative, often resulting in a shoulder shrug, exactly as described here. Drawing tasks and games like Winnicott's squiggle can facilitate disclosure at this age. Entirely open-ended questioning is the described cause of the child's shoulder-shrug response, not the fix for it. Simple closed yes-or-no questions may not elicit sufficient information from a school-age child. Puppets and dolls directed away from the child are the younger-child (under 6) technique, not the school-age approach. A fully structured respondent-based interview format is a diagnostic-instrument concept, not the described school-age interviewing technique.","boardPrep":"Step 1: School-age children need partially structured, multiple-choice-flavored questions and props like drawing/squiggle games rather than fully open-ended or fully closed formats. COMLEX: Recognizing when a child's response pattern (shoulder shrug) signals a developmentally mismatched interview technique is a patient-centered communication skill.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into age-graded pediatric confidentiality and mandatory reporting","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Entirely open-ended questionin"}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":1,"stem":"A 15-year-old is being interviewed individually and expresses concern about whether the psychiatrist will tell her parents what she says. How should the psychiatrist respond regarding confidentiality?","choices":{"A":"Assure her everything is entirely confidential, including disclosures of danger","B":"Tell her that everything will be automatically shared with her parents, regardless of content","C":"Decline to discuss confidentiality at all, and proceed directly into questioning","D":"Tell her that confidentiality applies to school matters, not to her home life","E":"Assure her that permission is sought before sharing specifics with parents, except for danger"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adolescents may be concerned about confidentiality, and clinicians can assure them that permission will be requested from them before specific information is shared with parents, except in situations involving danger to the adolescent or others, in which case confidentiality must be sacrificed. Assuring her that everything is confidential under all circumstances with no exceptions is inaccurate and could leave a genuinely dangerous situation unaddressed. Telling her that all information will be automatically shared regardless of content ignores the age-appropriate confidentiality afforded to adolescents and would likely undermine rapport and disclosure. Declining to discuss confidentiality at all fails to address the adolescent's explicitly stated concern before proceeding. Limiting confidentiality only to school-related information is not the described framework, which is about danger to self or others, not topic area.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adolescent confidentiality is conditional, not absolute — permission is sought before sharing with parents, except when danger to self or others requires disclosure. COMLEX: This age-graded confidentiality principle is a direct pediatric extension of the general duty-to-warn/confidentiality framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 1 — extends the confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into age-graded pediatric confidentiality and mandatory reporting","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Limiting confidentiality only "}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician wants to screen a school-age child for ADHD using a family of assessment instruments with teacher, parent, and adolescent self-report versions. Which instrument is best suited to this purpose?","choices":{"A":"The Conners Rating Scales","B":"The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist","C":"The Rorschach test","D":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test","E":"The Children's Apperception Test"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Conners Rating Scales are a family of instruments most commonly used in the assessment of ADHD, with teacher, parent, and self-report (for adolescents) versions in both short and long forms, and excellent validity for discriminating ADHD patients from normal controls. The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist covers a broad range of symptoms and positive attributes rather than being ADHD-specific in this way. The Rorschach test is a projective personality test using inkblots, unrelated to ADHD screening. The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test assesses visual-motor perceptual performance, not ADHD symptoms. The Children's Apperception Test is a projective test using animal-scene picture cards, unrelated to ADHD screening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Conners Rating Scales are the described family of teacher/parent/self-report instruments specifically validated for ADHD screening and monitoring treatment response. COMLEX: Multirater ADHD screening (teacher + parent + self-report) reflects the systematic multirater data-gathering principle in pediatric diagnostic workups.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — extends the validated cognitive-screening-tool framework (MMSE/SLUMS/Mini-Cog) into pediatric structured interviews and rating scales","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician wants a broad-range parent and teacher questionnaire covering mood, frustration tolerance, hyperactivity, oppositional behavior, and anxiety, with normative profiles based on three childhood age groups. Which instrument fits this description?","choices":{"A":"The Conners Rating Scales","B":"The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist","C":"The Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia","D":"The Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised","E":"The Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The parent and teacher versions of the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist cover a broad range of symptoms (mood, frustration tolerance, hyperactivity, oppositional behavior, anxiety) and several positive attributes, with profiles developed for three age groups (4 to 5, 6 to 11, and 12 to 16). The Conners Rating Scales are specifically ADHD-focused, not this broad-range checklist. The K-SADS is a semistructured diagnostic interview for mood disorders and other conditions in children 6 to 18, not a broad symptom checklist with normative age-group profiles. The Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised is specifically for autism assessment, unrelated to this broad checklist. The Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test measures receptive vocabulary, unrelated to this behavioral checklist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist is the broad-range parent/teacher symptom questionnaire with age-group-specific normative profiles, distinct from the ADHD-specific Conners scales. COMLEX: Broad symptom checklists identify areas deviating from age-matched norms without themselves making a diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — extends the validated cognitive-screening-tool framework (MMSE/SLUMS/Mini-Cog) into pediatric structured interviews and rating scales","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The K-SADS is a semistructured"}},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher needs a highly structured interview that can be administered by trained laypersons without any child-psychiatry background, assessing more than 30 DSM diagnostic entities over the past 4 weeks and past year. Which instrument fits this need?","choices":{"A":"The Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Assessment","B":"The Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia","C":"The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist","D":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test","E":"The NIMH Diagnostic Interview Schedule for Children"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The NIMH DISC-IV is a highly structured interview designed to assess more than 30 DSM diagnostic entities, administered by trained laypersons, with parallel child and parent forms, assessing diagnoses present within the last 4 weeks and within the last year; because it is fully structured, the examiner need not have any knowledge of child psychiatry. The CAPA is an \"interviewer-based,\" semistructured instrument requiring training and clinical judgment in interpreting symptoms, not a fully structured layperson tool. The K-SADS is also semistructured and requires some training in child psychiatry, unlike a fully structured layperson interview. The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist is a questionnaire, not a diagnostic interview assessing 30+ DSM entities. The Rorschach test is a projective personality test, unrelated to structured DSM diagnostic assessment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fully structured \"respondent-based\" interviews like the DISC-IV require no clinical interpretation and can be administered by trained laypersons, unlike semistructured \"interviewer-based\" tools like the K-SADS or CAPA. COMLEX: Distinguishing structured from semistructured interview formats clarifies who can administer each and what training is required.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 2 — extends the validated cognitive-screening-tool framework (MMSE/SLUMS/Mini-Cog) into pediatric structured interviews and rating scales","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The CAPA is an \"interviewer-ba","B":"The K-SADS is also semistructu"}},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"During a pediatric neuropsychiatric exam, a clinician notes mild incoordination, poor balance, contralateral overflow movements, and mild choreiform movements. What is the correct interpretation of these findings?","choices":{"A":"These findings indicate a focal neurologic lesion, requiring urgent neurologic localization","B":"These are neurologic soft signs, nonfocal findings not requiring urgent localization","C":"These findings are minor physical anomalies, structural features of head and face","D":"These findings are a normal variant needing no further clinical consideration","E":"These findings establish a specific psychiatric diagnosis without further workup"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neurologic soft signs, a term coined by Loretta Bender, are nondiagnostic abnormalities including physical findings like contralateral overflow movements and nonfocal signs like mild choreiform movements, poor balance, and mild incoordination; they do not indicate focal neurologic disorders but are associated with developmental disabilities, learning disabilities, and behavioral disturbances. These findings specifically do not indicate a focal lesion requiring urgent localization; that is the key distinguishing feature of a soft sign versus a hard focal deficit. These are neurologic soft signs (behavioral/physical/nonfocal findings), not minor physical anomalies, which refer instead to dysmorphic features like epicanthal folds. Some soft signs are normal at a young age but become abnormal if they persist in an older child, so they are not always normal at any age. Soft signs are useful to note but are not useful in making a specific psychiatric diagnosis on their own.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neurologic soft signs are nonfocal, nondiagnostic findings associated with developmental disability, distinct from hard focal neurologic deficits requiring localization. COMLEX: The Physical and Neurological Examination for Soft Signs (PANESS) is a standardized instrument for children up to age 15 documenting these findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into pediatric neurologic soft signs and physical exam findings","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"These are neurologic soft sign","D":"Some soft signs are normal at "}},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with a learning disability is noted on exam to have a high-arched palate, epicanthal folds, low-set ears, and multiple hair whorls. How should these findings be characterized and interpreted?","choices":{"A":"Neurologic soft signs, nonfocal findings of coordination and motor persistence","B":"Definitive diagnostic evidence of a specific, identifiable chromosomal syndrome","C":"Findings that reliably predict a poor long-term developmental and academic prognosis","D":"Minor physical anomalies, rarely diagnostically helpful and carrying no prognostic value","E":"Findings occurring at equal rates in typically developing school-age children"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Minor physical anomalies or dysmorphic features, including a high-arched palate, epicanthal folds, low-set ears, and multiple hair whorls, occur with higher-than-usual frequency in children with developmental disabilities, learning disabilities, speech and language disorders, and hyperactivity; documentation is part of the neuropsychiatric assessment, but these findings are rarely helpful in the diagnostic process and do not imply a good or bad prognosis. These are minor physical anomalies (dysmorphic features), not neurologic soft signs, which refer instead to behavioral/physical/nonfocal neurologic findings. They are not definitive diagnostic evidence of any specific chromosomal syndrome on their own, since they are rarely diagnostically helpful. They do not reliably predict prognosis, good or bad. They are specifically described as occurring with higher-than-usual frequency in developmental/learning disabilities, not as unrelated to them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minor physical anomalies (dysmorphic features) are associated with developmental/learning disabilities but are rarely diagnostically specific and carry no prognostic implication on their own. COMLEX: Distinguishing minor physical anomalies from neurologic soft signs clarifies which exam findings belong to which category during a pediatric MSE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into pediatric neurologic soft signs and physical exam findings","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"They are specifically describe"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is deciding whether a 10-year-old's mild motor incoordination noted on exam represents a nonfocal soft sign or warrants further neurologic workup for a focal deficit. What distinguishes a hard focal neurologic deficit from a soft sign in this differentiation?","choices":{"A":"A hard focal deficit is any finding present in a child younger than 15 years","B":"A soft sign always requires immediate EEG, CT, or MRI regardless of clinical context","C":"A hard focal deficit localizes to a lesion, while a soft sign lacks localizing value","D":"There is no meaningful distinction between a hard focal deficit and a soft sign","E":"A soft sign is defined exclusively by a child's intelligence quotient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The defining distinction is that neurologic soft signs are nondiagnostic and do not indicate focal neurologic disorders, whereas a hard focal deficit would localize to a specific neuroanatomic lesion; when a seizure disorder or structural brain abnormality is considered in the differential, EEG, CT, or MRI may be indicated specifically for that concern, not for soft signs themselves. Age younger than 15 is simply the PANESS instrument's applicable age range, not the definition of a hard focal deficit. Soft signs themselves do not always require immediate EEG, CT, or MRI; that workup is reserved for when a seizure disorder or structural abnormality is specifically suspected. There is a meaningful, clinically important distinction between the two, contrary to this option. A soft sign is not defined by IQ; IQ is a separate developmental measure captured through standardized testing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The soft-sign-versus-hard-focal-deficit distinction hinges on whether findings localize to a specific neuroanatomic lesion, guiding whether EEG/CT/MRI is warranted. COMLEX: This distinction directly parallels the primary-versus-secondary/organic differentiation principle taught in the broader psychiatric MSE framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into pediatric neurologic soft signs and physical exam findings","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"A soft sign is not defined by "}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old is administered the WISC-III-R and scores an average full-scale IQ of 100, but achievement testing shows a specific, isolated deficit in one academic area despite otherwise average-to-high performance. What does this pattern most likely suggest?","choices":{"A":"Global intellectual disability, with uniformly low functioning across all domains","B":"A normal developmental variant, requiring no further evaluation or intervention","C":"A neurologic soft sign, requiring formal PANESS testing to further characterize it","D":"A learning disorder, confined to that one affected academic area","E":"An unreliable test result, that should simply be disregarded entirely as invalid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Achievement tests (such as the Woodcock-Johnson or WRAT) measure specific academic skills, and an average full-scale IQ (100) with an isolated deficit in one academic area points toward a domain-specific learning disorder rather than global intellectual disability, which would show a pervasively low full-scale IQ across domains. Global intellectual disability would not present as an average overall IQ with one isolated deficit; it requires pervasively low functioning across adaptive domains. This isolated-domain pattern with an otherwise average-to-high IQ is not a normal variant requiring no evaluation; it specifically flags a possible remedial-education need. A neurologic soft sign is a distinct physical/behavioral exam finding, not a test-score pattern. This is not an unreliable result to disregard; an isolated deficit against an otherwise average profile is exactly what intelligence and achievement testing together are meant to reveal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An average full-scale IQ with an isolated deficit on achievement testing in one academic area is the classic pattern distinguishing a specific learning disorder from global intellectual disability. COMLEX: This IQ-plus-achievement interpretation directly guides academic/remedial recommendations, a named pediatric assessment competency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into standardized IQ/adaptive testing for learning disorder versus intellectual disability","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Global intellectual disability"}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician needs to assess a child's everyday functioning across communication, daily living skills, socialization, and motor skills domains to help determine the level of intellectual disability. Which instrument is specifically designed for this purpose?","choices":{"A":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales","B":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children","C":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale","D":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test","E":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A measure of adaptive functioning, including competence in communication, daily living skills, socialization, and motor skills, is the most definitive way to determine the level of intellectual disability, and the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales specifically covers these four domains (Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialization, Motor Skills) for infants through youth 18 years of age. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC-III-R) yields verbal, performance, and full-scale IQ scores, not adaptive-functioning domain scores. The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale similarly yields an IQ and mental age score, not adaptive-functioning domains. The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test assesses visual-motor perceptual performance via figure copying, not adaptive functioning. The Rorschach test is a projective personality test using inkblots, unrelated to adaptive functioning assessment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales is the named instrument for adaptive-functioning assessment (Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialization, Motor Skills), the most definitive way to grade intellectual disability severity. COMLEX: Adaptive functioning, not IQ score alone, determines the clinical level of intellectual disability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into standardized IQ/adaptive testing for learning disorder versus intellectual disability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician needs to select an intelligence test for a 3-year-old child, an age below the typical starting range of the WISC-III-R. Which instrument can be administered starting at this younger age?","choices":{"A":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children","B":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale","C":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale","D":"The Kaufman Adolescent and Adult Intelligence Test","E":"The Raven Progressive Matrices"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale covers an age range from 2 to 24 years, relying on pictures, drawings, and objects for very young children and verbal performance for older children and adolescents, making it appropriate for a 3-year-old. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC-III-R) is given to children from 6 to 16 years of age (Kaplan ch. 1), too old a starting range for a 3-year-old. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale covers ages 16 to adult, far too old for a 3-year-old. The Kaufman Adolescent and Adult Intelligence Test covers ages 11 to 85+, also far too old. The Raven Progressive Matrices, a nonverbal reasoning test, is normed from about age 5, still older than this 3-year-old.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Stanford-Binet's 2-to-24-year range makes it the appropriate intelligence test choice for children too young for the WISC-III-R's 6-to-16-year range. COMLEX: Selecting the age-appropriate standardized instrument is essential before intelligence/educational test results can be validly interpreted.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into standardized IQ/adaptive testing for learning disorder versus intellectual disability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old is brought in by her parents for evaluation of irritability and poor engagement in play. The clinician plans the initial assessment approach. Which of the following best reflects appropriate technique for this age?","choices":{"A":"Separate the child from the parents immediately, to obtain an individual confidential interview","B":"Begin with the parents present, observing the parent-infant interaction and play","C":"Rely on a fully structured, respondent-based interview administered to the toddler","D":"Administer the WISC-III-R, to establish a baseline IQ before any behavioral observation","E":"Proceed directly to a squiggle-game drawing task as the primary assessment tool"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Assessments of infants and very young children usually begin with the parents present, because very young children may be frightened by the interview situation, and this also allows assessment of the parent-infant interaction; observations of play reveal a child's developmental level and reflect the child's emotional state. Separating a 2-year-old from parents for an individual confidential interview is developmentally inappropriate and contrary to the described approach; confidentiality concerns apply to older children and adolescents, not toddlers. A fully structured respondent-based interview administered directly to a toddler is not developmentally feasible; young children have limited ability to report symptoms verbally. The WISC-III-R is designed for ages 6 to 16, not a 2-year-old, and IQ testing is not the described first step. The squiggle game is a school-age technique requiring drawing and turn-taking skills beyond a 2-year-old's developmental level.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Infant and toddler assessment begins with parents present to observe parent-child interaction, since very young children may be frightened alone and cannot yet self-report reliably. COMLEX: This age-appropriate technique selection reflects the broader principle that interview modality must match developmental stage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — extends age-appropriate assessment technique into interpreting the physical/neurologic exam findings gathered during the same visit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old male is referred for evaluation after a school disciplinary incident. He is apprehensive and initially hostile during the interview. He asks directly whether his parents will find out everything he says. Which approach best reflects appropriate technique with this patient?","choices":{"A":"Explore his perspective openly and nonjudgmentally, answering his question honestly","B":"Confront him immediately about his hostility, assigning blame for the disciplinary incident","C":"Inform him that details will automatically be relayed to his parents and school","D":"Terminate the interview immediately, due to his initial apprehension and hostility","E":"Avoid any discussion of confidentiality, since he asked just the one direct question"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With adolescents, clinicians should communicate the value of hearing the story from the adolescent's point of view, reserve judgment, and not assign blame; adolescents may be concerned about confidentiality, and clinicians can assure them that permission will be requested before specific information is shared with parents, except in situations of danger to the adolescent or others. Confronting him about hostility and assigning blame directly contradicts the described nonjudgmental, non-punitive stance needed to build rapport with a defiant or difficult teenager. Informing him that everything will automatically be relayed regardless of content misrepresents the actual age-graded confidentiality framework and would likely shut down disclosure. Terminating the interview solely due to initial hostility is not warranted; interviews should be postponed or discontinued only if the clinician feels threatened or the patient becomes destructive or self-injurious, not simply due to apprehension or initial hostility. His direct question about confidentiality is exactly the moment the clinician should address it, not avoid it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An open-ended, nonjudgmental stance with explicit, accurate confidentiality reassurance (permission sought except for danger) is the correct technique for an apprehensive or hostile adolescent. COMLEX: Managing countertransference toward a defiant teenager while maintaining appropriate limits is part of pediatric patient-centered communication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference awareness framework into managing a hostile or defiant adolescent interview","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply age-appropriate clinical interviewing modalities and legal confidentiality parameters when evaluating pediatric patients—utilizing play-based assessment/symbolic dolls for young children, structured questioning for school-age children, and direct confidential engagement (with mandatory reporting limits for abuse, neglect, or imminent self/harm) for adolescents.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician is evaluating a 7-year-old with a suspicious bruising pattern inconsistent with the parents' explanation. What is the clinician's legal and ethical obligation regarding confidentiality in this situation?","choices":{"A":"Confidentiality must be preserved, with no outside agency notified","B":"The finding should be documented in the chart, with no further action","C":"Confidentiality must be limited, and child protective services must be notified","D":"The parents should be confronted directly before any further evaluation","E":"The clinician should wait for the child to disclose abuse explicitly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As part of pediatric assessment, clinicians must develop an index of suspicion about whether a child is a victim of abuse or neglect, and whenever there is a suspicion of child maltreatment, clinicians are required to inform the local child protective service agency; this mandatory reporting limit overrides ordinary confidentiality. Preserving confidentiality absolutely with no outside reporting under any circumstance directly contradicts the described mandatory reporting requirement for suspected maltreatment. Documenting without further action fails to meet the described obligation to actively inform child protective services. Directly confronting and accusing the parents before further evaluation is not the described appropriate response and could compromise both the clinical relationship and any subsequent investigation. Waiting for explicit disclosure from the child is not required; suspicion alone, based on clinical findings like an inconsistent bruising pattern, triggers the reporting obligation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Suspected child maltreatment triggers a mandatory report to child protective services, overriding ordinary confidentiality — a hard legal-ethical limit distinct from standard adolescent confidentiality. COMLEX: This mandatory-reporting obligation is the pediatric-specific application of the broader duty-to-warn/limits-of-confidentiality framework.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — extends the general confidentiality/duty-to-warn legal-ethical framework into the specific mandatory-reporting obligation for suspected child maltreatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Documenting without further ac"}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A child psychiatrist evaluating a 12-year-old wants a semistructured, interviewer-based instrument that can be given in modules so she can focus on selected diagnoses, that rates the presence and the severity of symptoms separately, and that asks about the 3 months before the interview. Interviewers must be trained and use clinical judgment in rating answers. Which instrument fits these needs?","choices":{"A":"The Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia","B":"The NIMH Diagnostic Interview Schedule for Children","C":"The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist","D":"The Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Assessment","E":"The Conners Parent and Teacher Rating Scales"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock describe the Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Assessment (CAPA) as an interviewer-based instrument for ages 9 to 17 that comes in modular form, covers the 3 months before the interview (the primary period), gives separate ratings for presence and severity, takes about 1 hour, and requires training and clinical judgment. The K-SADS is also semistructured but covers current symptoms over the previous year and is not described as modular. The NIMH DISC-IV is a fully structured, respondent-based interview that trained laypersons can give without clinical judgment, covering the last 4 weeks and the last year. The Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist and the Conners scales are questionnaires completed by parents, teachers, or the child, not diagnostic interviews.","boardPrep":"Step 1: semistructured interviewer-based interviews (K-SADS, CAPA) need clinical judgment; fully structured respondent-based interviews (DISC-IV, ChIPS, DICA) follow a script; questionnaires cover broad (Achenbach CBCL) or narrow (Conners for ADHD) symptom areas. COMLEX: diagnostic instruments supplement but never replace the clinical interview.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — age-appropriate interviewing of children and adolescents","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The K-SADS is also semistructu"}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinic is building a comprehensive pediatric ADHD diagnostic workup and wants to gather data from the child's parents, teachers, and the child directly, systematically comparing perspectives across settings. Which of the following instrument pairings best supports this multirater goal?","choices":{"A":"The Rorschach test with the Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test","B":"The Vineland caregiver interview with a Conners parent form","C":"The Conners self-report form with Achenbach parent and teacher forms","D":"An unstructured child interview with the WISC intelligence scale","E":"The K-SADS child interview with an Achenbach teacher form"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both the Conners Rating Scales and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist have separate teacher, parent, and (for Conners) self-report versions, so pairing the Conners self-report with the Achenbach parent and teacher forms captures all three informants, directly matching the multirater diagnostic-workup goal described. The Rorschach test and the Bender Gestalt are both performance tasks completed by the child alone, giving no parent or teacher perspective. A Vineland caregiver interview paired with a Conners parent form draws on the parents twice and leaves out both the teacher and the child. An unstructured child interview with the WISC gives only the child's perspective, and the WISC measures intelligence rather than ADHD symptoms. A K-SADS child interview with an Achenbach teacher form covers the child and the teacher but omits the parents, so it cannot compare all three settings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combining ADHD-specific (Conners) and broad-symptom (Achenbach) rating scales, each with parent/teacher/self-report versions, is how systematic multirater pediatric diagnostic data is gathered. COMLEX: Multirater data-gathering directly addresses the well-documented poor agreement between teacher and parent ratings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — extends the social-determinants/multi-informant contextual-assessment framework into structured multirater pediatric rating-scale data gathering","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Select appropriate structured diagnostic interview tools and standardized rating scales (such as Connors Rating Scales for ADHD, and the Achenbach Child Behavior Checklist) to systematically gather multirater clinical data from parents, teachers, and pediatric patients during a diagnostic workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician notes that a parent and teacher completing separate Conners Rating Scales forms for the same child report substantially different symptom severity. How should this discrepancy be interpreted?","choices":{"A":"As evidence that the Conners Rating Scales lack established reliability as instruments","B":"As an expected limitation given the poor agreement between informant versions","C":"As proof that the child is deliberately exaggerating symptoms in one of the settings","D":"As an indication that the parent-completed form should be weighted more heavily","E":"As evidence that the child's behavior has changed markedly between the two ratings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reliability data are excellent for the Conners Rating Scales individually, but the teacher and parent versions specifically tend to show poor agreement with each other — a described, expected limitation reflecting that behavior can genuinely differ across settings, not a flaw invalidating the instrument. This discrepancy does not mean the scales are unreliable and should be discarded; individual reliability remains excellent, only cross-informant agreement is poor. It is not proof the child is fabricating symptoms in either setting; differing behavior across contexts (home versus school) is a recognized, genuine phenomenon in pediatric psychiatry. Neither the parent-only nor teacher-only form should be treated as the sole trustworthy source; the described rationale for using multiple raters is precisely to capture this setting-dependent variability, not to discard one perspective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Poor parent-teacher agreement on the Conners Rating Scales is a described, expected limitation, not evidence of unreliability or fabrication — genuine cross-setting behavioral differences are real. COMLEX: This reinforces why multirater data gathering, not any single informant, is the systematic diagnostic-workup standard.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — extends the primary-vs-secondary/contextual differentiation framework into interpreting genuine cross-setting behavioral variability rather than assuming fabrication","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This discrepancy does not mean","D":"Neither the parent-only nor te"}},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old with a learning disability and mild hyperactivity is noted on exam to have poor balance, mild incoordination, and the persistence of an infantile reflex that would normally have disappeared by this age. The neurologic exam is otherwise nonfocal and no seizure activity or structural lesion is suspected. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Order emergent EEG, CT, and MRI as the standard response to soft signs","B":"Conclude the child has a definitive focal neurologic lesion requiring neurosurgical referral","C":"Disregard the findings entirely as clinically meaningless and omit them from documentation","D":"Use these findings to establish a specific psychiatric diagnosis directly","E":"Document the soft signs without pursuing EEG, CT, or MRI"},"correct":"E","explanation":"These findings (poor balance, mild incoordination, persistence of an infantile reflex) are classic neurologic soft signs, which are associated with learning disabilities and hyperactivity but do not indicate focal neurologic disorders; EEG, CT, or MRI are specifically indicated only when a seizure disorder or structural brain abnormality is considered in the differential, which is not the case in this otherwise nonfocal exam. Ordering emergent imaging regardless of the nonfocal exam is not warranted by soft signs alone and contradicts the described indication threshold for imaging. Concluding there is a definitive focal lesion requiring neurosurgical referral directly contradicts the defining feature of soft signs, which is that they do not indicate focal pathology. Disregarding the findings entirely and omitting them from documentation is incorrect; documentation of soft signs is part of the standard neuropsychiatric assessment even though they are not individually diagnostic. Soft signs alone are not useful in making a specific psychiatric diagnosis, so they should not be used this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Soft signs are documented as part of the neuropsychiatric exam but do not by themselves trigger neuroimaging unless a seizure disorder or structural lesion is independently suspected. COMLEX: This reflects the broader principle of matching workup intensity to the actual clinical suspicion rather than reflexive escalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 1 — connects the age-appropriate interview/exam framework to correctly interpreting and documenting the physical findings gathered during the same evaluation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old with speech and language delay is found on exam to have a high-arched palate, epicanthal folds, and low-set ears, along with mild motor overflow movements. The clinician is documenting these findings for the neuropsychiatric assessment. How should these two categories of findings be distinguished from each other in the documentation?","choices":{"A":"The palate, folds, and ears are minor physical anomalies; the overflow movements are soft signs","B":"All of these findings are neurologic soft signs, forming a single, undifferentiated exam category","C":"All of these findings are minor physical anomalies, forming a single, undifferentiated category","D":"The palate and folds are neurologic soft signs, while the overflow movements are physical anomalies","E":"None of these findings belong anywhere within the neuropsychiatric assessment documentation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Minor physical anomalies or dysmorphic features specifically include structural features like a high-arched palate, epicanthal folds, and low-set ears, while neurologic soft signs specifically include physical/behavioral/nonfocal findings like overflow movements; both categories occur with higher frequency in developmental and learning disabilities and are documented as part of the neuropsychiatric assessment, but they are distinct categories. Classifying all these findings as neurologic soft signs incorrectly merges the two distinct categories; the palate/fold/ear findings are structural dysmorphic features, not soft signs. Classifying all as minor physical anomalies similarly incorrectly merges the categories; overflow movements are a soft sign, not a structural anomaly. Reversing the pairing (palate/folds as soft signs, movements as anomalies) exactly inverts the correct classification. Both categories are explicitly part of the neuropsychiatric assessment and should be documented, not omitted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Minor physical anomalies are structural/dysmorphic features (palate, folds, ears), while neurologic soft signs are physical/behavioral/nonfocal findings (overflow movements) — two distinct documented categories in the pediatric neuropsychiatric exam. COMLEX: Correctly categorizing exam findings supports accurate, standardized documentation across evaluators.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — connects the structured multirater assessment framework to correctly categorizing the physical/neurologic exam findings gathered during the same comprehensive workup","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Classifying all as minor physi","D":"Reversing the pairing (palate/","E":"Both categories are explicitly"}},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate physical findings during a pediatric mental status examination by identifying neurological soft signs (e.g., mild incoordination, motor impersistence, overflow movements) and minor physical anomalies (e.g., epicanthal folds, high-arched palate, low-set ears) to distinguish non-focal developmental aberrations from hard focal neurological deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old presents with new-onset staring spells with brief loss of awareness, in addition to several minor physical anomalies noted incidentally on exam. How should the clinician approach the staring spells differently from the incidental anomalies?","choices":{"A":"Both the staring spells and the anomalies warrant an urgent EEG equally","B":"Neither the staring spells nor the anomalies warrant further workup","C":"The incidental anomalies, rather than the staring spells, warrant an urgent EEG","D":"The staring spells, rather than the incidental anomalies, warrant an urgent EEG","E":"Both findings are equally low priority and can be deferred indefinitely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When a seizure disorder is considered in the differential diagnosis, EEG, CT, or MRI may be indicated; new-onset staring spells with loss of awareness are a classic seizure-concerning presentation warranting this specific workup, whereas minor physical anomalies alone are described as rarely helpful in the diagnostic process and do not themselves trigger imaging or EEG. Both findings do not equally warrant urgent EEG; imaging/EEG indication is driven by genuine seizure concern (the staring spells), not by incidental anomalies. Neither finding warranting further workup ignores the clear seizure-concerning nature of new-onset staring spells with loss of awareness. It is the staring spells, not the incidental anomalies, that specifically warrant EEG; this option reverses the correct clinical prioritization. Treating both as equally low-priority and deferring indefinitely inappropriately delays a genuinely time-sensitive seizure workup.","boardPrep":"Step 1: New-onset staring spells with loss of awareness are a seizure red flag warranting EEG, clinically distinct from incidental minor physical anomalies, which do not themselves prompt imaging. COMLEX: This exemplifies matching diagnostic urgency to the specific finding rather than applying uniform workup to all exam observations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — extends the true-hallucination-versus-organic-cause differentiation framework into recognizing which pediatric exam findings specifically warrant organic/neurologic workup","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Both findings do not equally w","C":"It is the staring spells, not "}},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old is referred for school readiness concerns. The psychologist wants one standardized battery, normed for children from about 2.5 to 12.5 years of age, that yields a mental processing composite equivalent to IQ along with achievement scores, so that intellectual capacity can be compared directly with acquired knowledge. Which instrument best meets this need?","choices":{"A":"The Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children","B":"The Woodcock-Johnson Psycho-Educational Battery","C":"The Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test","D":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales","E":"The Bayley Scales of Infant Development"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock's table of psychological tests lists the Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children (K-ABC) for ages 2.6 to 12.6 years and notes that it allows immediate comparison of intellectual capacity with acquired knowledge, scoring a Mental Processing Composite (an IQ equivalent) alongside achievement standard scores; that comparison is the core of separating a specific learning disorder from global intellectual disability. The Woodcock-Johnson battery measures academic achievement in reading, mathematics, and written language but gives no IQ measure. The Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test measures receptive vocabulary only. The Vineland measures adaptive functioning in communication, daily living, socialization, and motor domains, not intelligence or achievement. The Bayley Scales assess infant development from 16 days to 42 months, so a 5-year-old is outside its range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: specific learning disorder = achievement well below expectation despite average IQ; intellectual disability = low IQ plus adaptive deficits, with severity graded by adaptive functioning. COMLEX: pair an intelligence measure with achievement testing before labeling a struggling student.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 3 — assessment and management of specific learning disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old is being evaluated for possible developmental delay. Standardized infant/toddler developmental assessment scores are borderline low. The family asks whether this score reliably predicts the child's future IQ. What is the most accurate response?","choices":{"A":"It reliably and precisely predicts, on its own, the child's exact future adult IQ score","B":"Infant and toddler developmental assessments have no clinical value and are not used","C":"It guarantees the child will not later develop a specific learning disorder","D":"It should be interpreted identically to a WISC-III-R full-scale IQ score","E":"Not a reliable predictor of later intelligence, but useful for detecting deviation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An infant's score on a developmental assessment is not a reliable way to predict a child's future IQ in most cases, but these assessments remain valuable in detecting developmental deviation and mental retardation and in raising suspicion of a developmental disorder; infant assessments rely heavily on sensorimotor function, while later intelligence testing includes verbal, social, and abstract cognitive abilities that have not yet emerged in infancy. The score does not reliably and precisely predict exact future IQ; this overstates its predictive value. Infant/toddler developmental assessments are not clinically valueless; they are valuable specifically for detecting deviation and raising suspicion, even without precise IQ prediction. A borderline-low score does not guarantee anything about future learning disorder status, since prediction from these assessments is limited. The score should not be interpreted identically to a WISC-III-R full-scale IQ score, since infant assessments measure different, earlier-developing functions (sensorimotor) than school-age intelligence tests (verbal, social, abstract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Infant developmental assessment scores have limited predictive value for future IQ but remain useful for detecting developmental deviation and prompting further evaluation. COMLEX: Communicating this limited-but-real predictive value accurately to families is part of guiding appropriate academic/remedial planning discussions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary presentation differentiation framework into correctly interpreting the limited but genuine predictive value of early developmental screening","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret standardized intelligence and educational test results (including the WISC-III-R, Stanford-Binet, and Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales) to distinguish specific learning disorders from global intellectual disability, and guide academic/remedial recommendations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old with a documented average WISC-III-R full-scale IQ has a persistent, isolated deficit specifically in reading despite six months of targeted intervention, with no deficits in math, written expression, or any adaptive-functioning domain. Educators are deciding on remedial recommendations. What is the most appropriate interpretation guiding those recommendations?","choices":{"A":"Global intellectual disability, requiring comprehensive adaptive-skills remediation across all domains","B":"A learning disorder limited to that one academic skill, informing focused remediation there","C":"No remedial recommendations, with the average full-scale IQ taken as sufficient","D":"A referral for neurologic soft-sign evaluation, with formal PANESS testing","E":"An invalid WISC-III-R result, requiring the full test to be repeated immediately"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Achievement testing identifies deficits in specific academic skills; an isolated, persistent reading deficit with an otherwise average IQ and preserved adaptive functioning (no deficits in math, written expression, or adaptive domains) is the classic pattern supporting a specific, domain-limited learning disorder, which should guide targeted, domain-specific remedial recommendations rather than broad intervention. Global intellectual disability requires adaptive-functioning deficits and a pervasively low IQ across domains, neither of which is present here; comprehensive adaptive-skills remediation is not indicated. An average full-scale IQ does not mean no remedial recommendations are needed; the isolated reading deficit itself specifically warrants targeted educational intervention. This isolated academic pattern does not itself prompt an evaluation for neurologic soft signs, which is a separate physical/behavioral exam category unrelated to this described academic testing pattern. There is no indication the WISC-III-R itself is invalid; an isolated domain deficit against an otherwise average and internally consistent profile is precisely the kind of meaningful result the test is designed to reveal, not evidence of test failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An isolated, persistent academic-domain deficit with average IQ and preserved adaptive functioning is the classic specific learning disorder pattern, guiding domain-targeted (not global) remedial recommendations. COMLEX: This synthesis question ties together IQ testing, adaptive-functioning assessment, and academic/remedial guidance for school-age children.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 4 — synthesizes the full IQ-testing/adaptive-functioning interpretive framework taught across this objective into a single domain-targeted remedial-recommendation vignette","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"An average full-scale IQ does "}}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":1,"stem":"A child has intellectual disability along with slanted eyes, epicanthal folds, a flat nasal bridge, a single palmar transverse crease, and a congenital heart defect. Karyotyping reveals 47 chromosomes with an extra chromosome 21, and the mother's karyotype is normal. Which mechanism produced this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Meiotic nondisjunction","B":"Robertsonian translocation","C":"Mitotic nondisjunction after fertilization","D":"Uniparental disomy","E":"Trinucleotide repeat expansion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patients with trisomy 21, representing the overwhelming majority of Down syndrome cases, have 47 chromosomes with an extra chromosome 21 and a normal maternal karyotype, caused by a meiotic nondisjunction occurring for unknown reasons. A Robertsonian translocation produces a total of 46 chromosomes despite an extra chromosome 21 and is often inherited from an unaffected carrier parent, unlike this patient's 47-chromosome count and normal maternal karyotype. Mitotic nondisjunction after fertilization produces mosaicism, with both normal and trisomic cell lines, not a uniform 47-chromosome karyotype. Uniparental disomy, in which both copies of a chromosome come from one parent, leaves the chromosome count normal and underlies some cases of Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes. A trinucleotide repeat expansion is the mechanism of fragile X syndrome and does not change chromosome number.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trisomy 21 nondisjunction, the majority mechanism of Down syndrome, produces 47 chromosomes with a normal maternal karyotype, distinct from the inherited translocation and mosaic-nondisjunction subtypes. COMLEX: Down syndrome is a named genetic disorder directly tested for its inheritance pattern and characteristic physical stigmata.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning determination framework into the specific genetic/metabolic syndromes that underlie many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":1,"stem":"A male child has intellectual disability, a long face and large ears, macroorchidism after puberty, hyperextensible joints, and rapid perseverative speech. Genetic testing shows a mutation at the fragile site Xq27.3. What is the inheritance pattern and relative severity expected in a female carrier compared with this affected male?","choices":{"A":"Autosomal dominant, with female carriers typically as severely affected as males","B":"Autosomal recessive, with female carriers remaining asymptomatic obligate carriers","C":"X-linked, with female carriers typically less severely affected than males","D":"Mitochondrial, with severity determined by the degree of heteroplasmy present","E":"Y-linked, occurring exclusively in males with no possible female carrier state"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fragile X syndrome results from a mutation on the X chromosome at the fragile site (Xq27.3); female carriers are often less impaired than affected males, though females can also manifest the typical physical characteristics and may have mild intellectual disability. It is not autosomal dominant; the mutation is specifically X-linked, at Xq27.3. It is not simply autosomal recessive with asymptomatic female carriers; female carriers can manifest physical characteristics and mild intellectual disability, unlike an asymptomatic carrier state. It is not mitochondrial; the described mutation is a fragile site on the X chromosome, not mitochondrial DNA. It is not Y-linked; the mutation occurs on the X chromosome, and females, who lack a Y chromosome, can still be affected carriers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fragile X syndrome's X-linked inheritance at Xq27.3 explains why female carriers are typically less severely affected than males, though they can still show physical stigmata and mild intellectual disability. COMLEX: Fragile X is a named genetic syndrome with a distinct behavioral phenotype (ADHD, learning disorders, autism spectrum features).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning determination framework into the specific genetic/metabolic syndromes that underlie many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"It is not mitochondrial; the d","E":"It is not Y-linked; the mutati"}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn undergoes a screening test that detects an inability to convert phenylalanine to tyrosine due to absent or inactive phenylalanine hydroxylase. Untreated, this condition is expected to cause severe intellectual disability, hyperactivity, temper tantrums, and bizarre hand-twisting movements. Which of the following best describes this disorder's inheritance and primary preventive management?","choices":{"A":"X-linked recessive, mostly preventable with strict dietary phosphate restriction","B":"Autosomal recessive, mostly preventable with an amino-acid-restricted diet","C":"Autosomal dominant, managed with enzyme cofactor supplementation","D":"Sporadic deletion, requiring lifelong enzyme replacement therapy","E":"Mitochondrial, managed symptomatically with supportive care"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PKU is transmitted as a recessive autosomal Mendelian trait, caused by the absence or inactivity of phenylalanine hydroxylase preventing conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine; PKU is mostly preventable with screening, followed by a low-phenylalanine diet if positive. It is not X-linked recessive, and dietary phosphate restriction is not the described intervention; phenylalanine restriction is. It is not autosomal dominant, and a preventive dietary intervention (low-phenylalanine diet) is specifically described as effective when started early. It is not a sporadic deletion requiring enzyme replacement; it is an inherited recessive enzymatic defect managed primarily through diet. It is not mitochondrial, and dietary management is specifically effective, contrary to this option's claim of no effective intervention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PKU's autosomal recessive phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency is preventable through newborn screening followed by a low-phenylalanine diet, a classic Step 1 metabolic-disorder teaching point. COMLEX: Phenylketonuria is a named genetic/metabolic disorder directly linked to a preventable behavioral phenotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning determination framework into the specific genetic/metabolic syndromes that underlie many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":1,"stem":"A 5-year-old has a limited vocabulary, speaks in short, ungrammatical sentences, and struggles with word retrieval and storage, despite normal hearing and no autism spectrum features. Which DSM-5 diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Speech sound disorder","B":"Childhood-onset fluency disorder","C":"Language disorder","D":"Social pragmatic communication disorder","E":"Childhood dysarthria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Language disorder consists of difficulties in the acquisition and use of language due to deficits in comprehension or production, including reduced vocabulary, limited abilities forming grammatically correct sentences, and difficulties with semantics (word retrieval, storage, and organization) — exactly this child's presentation, with normal hearing and no autism spectrum features specified. Speech sound disorder involves difficulty pronouncing speech sounds correctly (omissions, distortions, substitutions), not this child's vocabulary/grammar/word-retrieval profile. Childhood-onset fluency disorder (stuttering) involves disruptions in the fluency and rhythm of speech, not vocabulary or grammar deficits. Social pragmatic communication disorder involves difficulty with the social use of language, such as adjusting to context or following conversational rules, while vocabulary and grammar are intact — the reverse of this child's profile. Dysarthria is a structural/neurologic speech disorder with slurred speech from muscle incoordination, not this vocabulary/grammar profile, and is excluded here by normal findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Language disorder specifically involves comprehension/production deficits (vocabulary, grammar, semantics), distinct from the articulation-focused speech sound disorder. COMLEX: Distinguishing DSM-5 communication disorder subtypes by their specific deficit domain guides accurate diagnosis and referral.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing organic dysarthria/hearing impairment from primary communication disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":1,"stem":"A 4-year-old omits the last sounds of words (saying \"mou\" for \"mouse\") and substitutes sounds (saying \"tup\" for \"cup\"), with normal vocabulary, grammar, and social language use, and no known neurologic condition. Which DSM-5 diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Speech sound disorder","B":"Language disorder","C":"Social pragmatic communication disorder","D":"Childhood dysarthria","E":"Childhood-onset fluency disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Speech sound disorder involves difficulty pronouncing speech sounds correctly due to omissions, distortions, or substitutions, such as omitting the last sounds of a word or substituting one sound for another — exactly this child's pattern, with intact vocabulary, grammar, and social language ruling out other communication disorders, and no neurologic condition ruling out dysarthria. Language disorder involves vocabulary, grammar, and semantic deficits, not present in this child with normal vocabulary and grammar. Social pragmatic communication disorder involves difficulty with the social use of language, not present here with normal social language use specified. Dysarthria is a speech sound disturbance with a known neurologic basis, explicitly excluded here by \"no known neurologic condition.\" Childhood-onset fluency disorder (stuttering) disrupts the rhythm and flow of speech with repetitions and prolongations, not the production of individual sounds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Speech sound disorder is isolated articulation errors (omissions, distortions, substitutions) without an underlying neurologic cause, distinguishing it from dysarthria and from the broader language disorder. COMLEX: Speech sound disorder is a named DSM-5 communication disorder subtype requiring exclusion of structural/neurologic causes before diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing organic dysarthria/hearing impairment from primary communication disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Language disorder involves voc","D":"Dysarthria is a speech sound d"}},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":1,"stem":"A child's speech contains frequent sound and syllable repetitions and prolonged pauses between sounds, with normal expressive and receptive language competence otherwise. This has persisted since preschool age and causes visible frustration. Which DSM-5 diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Speech sound disorder, involving omissions, distortions, and substitutions","B":"Language disorder, centering on vocabulary, grammar, and semantic deficits","C":"Social, pragmatic, communication disorder, involving the social use of language","D":"Childhood-onset fluency disorder, the DSM-5 term for developmental stuttering","E":"Selective mutism, involving a failure to speak in specific social situations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Childhood-onset fluency disorder, or stuttering, involves involuntary speech motor events including repetitions and prolonged pauses between sounds and syllables, occurring despite normal expressive and receptive language competence — exactly this presentation. Speech sound disorder involves articulation errors like omissions, distortions, and substitutions, not repetition/prolongation fluency disruptions. Language disorder involves vocabulary, grammar, and semantic deficits, explicitly not present here given normal language competence. Social, pragmatic, communication disorder involves difficulty using language socially, not a fluency disruption. Selective mutism involves a failure to speak in specific social situations despite speaking elsewhere, not a fluency disturbance affecting all speech.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Childhood-onset fluency disorder (stuttering) is a fluency/rhythm disruption occurring despite intact language competence, distinguishing it from language disorder and speech sound disorder. COMLEX: Fluency disorder is a named DSM-5 speech disorder distinct from articulation-based speech sound disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 -> Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing organic dysarthria/hearing impairment from primary communication disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old has persistent, significant difficulty with reading single words and sentences fluently, comprehension, and spelling, present for over a year despite targeted reading intervention. Her overall intellectual functioning is average, and there is no sensory impairment. Which term does DSM-5 use as equivalent to describe this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Dyscalculia","B":"Dysgraphia","C":"Hyperlexia","D":"Dyspraxia","E":"Dyslexia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"DSM-5 notes that the term dyslexia is an equivalent term describing a pattern of learning difficulties including deficits in accurate or fluent word recognition, poor decoding, and poor spelling skills — exactly this child's reading-specific presentation with average overall intellectual functioning and no sensory impairment. Dyscalculia is an alternative term referring to deficits in learning arithmetic facts and numerical processing, not reading. Dysgraphia refers to written expression deficits, not this reading-specific pattern. Hyperlexia is precocious word decoding, often seen with autism spectrum disorder, the reverse of this child's poor decoding. Dyspraxia, a common name for developmental coordination disorder, involves motor planning and coordination deficits, not reading difficulties.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dyslexia is DSM-5's equivalent term for the specific learning disorder subtype affecting word recognition, decoding, and spelling — a named, high-yield Step 1 term. COMLEX: Specific learning disorder diagnosis requires ruling out intellectual disability and sensory deficits as the cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into Specific Learning Disorder's 6-month, domain-specific, exclusion-based criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":1,"stem":"A child has been struggling with mathematics for over a year, specifically with learning arithmetic facts, processing numerical information, and performing accurate calculations, despite average reading and writing skills and no intellectual disability. Which DSM-5-equivalent term describes this specific pattern?","choices":{"A":"Dyslexia","B":"Dyscalculia","C":"Dysgraphia","D":"Dyspraxia","E":"Acalculia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dyscalculia is an alternative DSM-5-equivalent term referring to a pattern of deficits related to learning arithmetic facts, processing numerical information, and performing accurate calculations — exactly this child's presentation, with average reading/writing and no intellectual disability ruling out other causes. Dyslexia refers to reading-specific deficits (word recognition, decoding, spelling), not arithmetic. Dysgraphia refers to written expression deficits, not the numerical/arithmetic deficits described. Dyspraxia, a common name for developmental coordination disorder, involves motor coordination, unrelated to arithmetic processing. Acalculia is an acquired loss of calculation ability after brain injury, such as a left parietal lesion, not a developmental learning pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dyscalculia is the DSM-5-equivalent term for the specific learning disorder subtype affecting arithmetic and numerical processing, distinct from the reading-focused dyslexia. COMLEX: Specific learning disorder's domain-specific specifiers (reading, math, written expression) guide targeted psychoeducational accommodations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into Specific Learning Disorder's 6-month, domain-specific, exclusion-based criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old has had persistent, significant academic underachievement in written expression for 8 months despite targeted intervention. Before diagnosing specific learning disorder, which of the following must specifically be ruled out as the cause, per DSM-5 exclusion criteria?","choices":{"A":"Intellectual disability, sensory impairment, another disorder, adversity, or poor education","B":"A family history of learning disorders, which raises risk in first-degree relatives","C":"Comorbid ADHD, which co-occurs in a substantial share of affected children","D":"The child's chronologic age, which sets when school difficulties become apparent","E":"Whether the deficit affects reading versus writing, which sets the DSM-5 specifier"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Per DSM-5, specific learning disorder exclusions include intellectual disability, visual or auditory impairment, another mental disorder, another medical disorder, psychosocial adversity, and inadequate education — the underachievement must not be better explained by any of these before the diagnosis is made. A family history of learning disorders alone is not itself an exclusion criterion; family history increases risk but does not rule the diagnosis in or out. Comorbid ADHD alone is not a formal DSM-5 exclusion criterion for specific learning disorder; the two conditions can co-occur. The child's chronologic age alone is not the specific exclusion list; symptoms beginning during school-age years is a duration/onset criterion, not this exclusion set. Whether the deficit is reading versus writing determines the specifier used, not whether exclusion criteria have been satisfied.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Specific learning disorder's DSM-5 exclusion criteria (intellectual disability, sensory impairment, another mental/medical disorder, psychosocial adversity, inadequate education) must all be ruled out before diagnosis. COMLEX: This exclusion-based diagnostic logic mirrors the general DSM-5 framework applied across pediatric neurodevelopmental disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into Specific Learning Disorder's 6-month, domain-specific, exclusion-based criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":1,"stem":"An 8-year-old exhibits repetitive hand-flapping and body-rocking that began before age 3, appear self-soothing, occur at a consistent anatomical location each time, lack any premonitory urge, and can be voluntarily suppressed with effort. He has no vocal symptoms. Which diagnosis best fits, distinguishing this from Tourette disorder?","choices":{"A":"Tourette disorder","B":"Stereotypic movement disorder","C":"Developmental coordination disorder","D":"Provisional tic disorder","E":"Chronic motor tic disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Distinguishing features of stereotypic movements compared to tics include a younger age of onset, lack of changing anatomical locations, lack of a premonitory urge, and decreased response to medication management — exactly this child's presentation (early onset, fixed location, no premonitory urge). Tourette disorder requires multiple motor tics plus at least one vocal tic persisting over a year, with tics that vary in location and are preceded by irresistible premonitory urges, none of which fit this presentation, and this child has no vocal symptoms at all. Developmental coordination disorder involves impaired motor coordination performance, not repetitive stereotyped movements. Provisional tic disorder involves tics (not stereotypies) present for less than a year. Chronic motor tic disorder involves motor tics only, persisting over a year, but still with the tic-specific features (changing location, premonitory urge) this child's presentation lacks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stereotypic movements are distinguished from tics by earlier onset, fixed anatomical location, absent premonitory urge, and decreased pharmacologic responsiveness. COMLEX: This distinction directly guides whether habit-reversal/CBIT or tic-specific pharmacotherapy is the appropriate first-line management approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tourette disorder requires mul"}},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old has had multiple motor tics (eye-blinking, shoulder-shrugging) and a vocal tic (throat-clearing) that first appeared 14 months ago, with onset at age 10 and a waxing-and-waning course. Which diagnosis and first-line behavioral treatment approach best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Stereotypic movement disorder, treated with exposure and response prevention","B":"Provisional tic disorder, treated with reassurance and observation","C":"Tourette disorder, treated with habit reversal training","D":"Developmental coordination disorder, treated with occupational therapy","E":"Chronic motor tic disorder, treated with relaxation training"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tourette disorder requires a history of multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic persisting for more than a year with onset before age 18 — exactly this presentation (14 months duration, onset age 10); habit reversal has been the most extensively researched behavioral treatment for tic disorders and is currently the first-line behavioral treatment. Stereotypic movement disorder does not involve tics with premonitory urges and vocal components as described here, and exposure and response prevention, while evidence-based for tics, is not first-line ahead of habit reversal. Provisional tic disorder is diagnosed when tics have been present less than a year, not the 14 months described here, and reassurance with observation is not the first-line approach for impairing tics, for which behavioral treatment (habit reversal/CBIT) is evidence-based. Developmental coordination disorder involves motor coordination impairment, not tics. Chronic motor tic disorder involves motor tics only without a vocal tic, and relaxation training has little benefit on its own; it is used only as one supporting component of CBIT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tourette disorder requires multiple motor tics plus at least one vocal tic for over a year with onset before 18; habit reversal training is the first-line, most extensively researched behavioral treatment. COMLEX: CBIT (Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics), built around habit reversal, has strong RCT-level evidence for tic reduction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":1,"stem":"A 13-year-old with confirmed Tourette disorder has tics causing significant social impairment despite an adequate trial of habit reversal training. The treatment team wants to add a mechanism-matched pharmacologic agent with a favorable side-effect profile compared to typical antipsychotics. Which medication class and specific agent best fits this description as the preferred first pharmacologic choice?","choices":{"A":"An atypical antipsychotic, risperidone","B":"A typical antipsychotic, haloperidol","C":"An SSRI, fluoxetine","D":"A benzodiazepine, clonazepam","E":"A stimulant, methylphenidate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Risperidone, with high affinity for dopamine D2 and serotonin 5-HT2 receptors, is the most well-studied atypical antipsychotic for tics, with considerable RCT evidence and fewer adverse events compared to typical antipsychotics like haloperidol and pimozide, making it a preferred first pharmacologic choice given its more favorable side-effect profile. Haloperidol, a typical antipsychotic, has a less favorable side-effect profile (more extrapyramidal symptoms, dyskinesia, dystonia) compared to atypical agents like risperidone, the opposite of what is described. SSRIs such as fluoxetine are not described as having an evidence base specifically for tic reduction; they are used for comorbid obsessive-compulsive symptoms in tic disorder patients, not for the tics themselves. Benzodiazepines such as clonazepam are not described as a mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy for tics in this framework. Stimulants such as methylphenidate are not described as a tic treatment; in fact, stimulants can exacerbate tics in some patients, which is why alpha-agonists are sometimes preferred when ADHD and tics coexist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Risperidone's D2/5-HT2 antagonism gives it strong evidence for tic reduction with a more favorable side-effect profile than typical antipsychotics like haloperidol/pimozide, making it a preferred first pharmacologic agent. COMLEX: Dopamine-2 receptor antagonism is the core mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy class for Tourette disorder tic reduction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-year-old repeatedly eats dirt and paper, resulting in lead poisoning and iron-deficiency anemia. This behavior is not attributable to any accepted cultural practice. What is the minimum age DSM-5 requires before this behavior can be diagnosed as this disorder?","choices":{"A":"6 months of age, when infants normally mouth objects orally","B":"5 years of age, the DSM-5 minimum age set for enuresis","C":"2 years of age, once normal infant mouthing behavior has ceased","D":"12 years of age, by which ingestion is usually deliberate","E":"No minimum age, since the diagnosis rests on the ingestion pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A minimum of 2 years of age is suggested by DSM-5 in the diagnosis of pica, in order to exclude developmentally appropriate mouthing of objects by infants that may accidentally result in ingestion; this 3-year-old with persistent nonnutritive ingestion and medical complications (lead poisoning, iron-deficiency anemia) meets this age threshold. 6 months is too young; developmentally appropriate mouthing behavior at this age would not meet pica criteria. 5 years is not the described DSM-5 minimum age threshold for pica; the threshold is 2 years. 12 years is far older than the described minimum age threshold and would exclude many legitimately diagnosable younger children. No minimum age applying is incorrect; DSM-5 specifically suggests a 2-year minimum to exclude normal infant mouthing behavior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pica's DSM-5 minimum age of 2 years excludes developmentally normal infant mouthing/accidental ingestion from the diagnosis. COMLEX: Pica's medical complications (lead poisoning, iron/zinc deficiency, intestinal obstruction) directly inform the required laboratory workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood feeding/eating disorder framework into the adolescent/adult eating disorder differential covered later in the block","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"6 months is too young; develop","B":"5 years is not the described D","E":"No minimum age applying is inc"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-month-old effortlessly and painlessly brings partially digested food back into her mouth shortly after feeding, appearing to find the process pleasurable, with a characteristic arched-back straining posture, and no evidence of nausea or retching. She has been a healthy weight throughout. Which diagnosis and criterion regarding weight best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Rumination disorder, with failure to thrive as a necessary diagnostic criterion","B":"ARFID, with effortless pleasurable regurgitation as its defining feature","C":"Pica, with persistent ingestion of nonnutritive, nonfood substances","D":"Rumination disorder, with repeated effortless regurgitation at a healthy weight","E":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease, a structural and motility diagnosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rumination is an effortless, painless regurgitation of partially digested food, often appearing pleasurable, with a characteristic arched-back straining posture; we can diagnose rumination disorder even if an infant is a healthy weight, since failure to thrive is not a necessary criterion of this disorder, though it is sometimes a sequela. Rumination disorder does not require failure to thrive as a necessary criterion; a healthy-weight infant can still be diagnosed. ARFID is characterized by food avoidance/restriction and failure to meet nutritional needs, not by effortless pleasurable regurgitation of already-ingested food. Pica is persistent ingestion of nonnutritive substances, not regurgitation of previously eaten food. Gastroesophageal reflux disease is a medical differential to rule out, but this vignette's specific pleasurable, self-induced-appearing pattern with an arched-back posture is the described clinical picture of rumination disorder, which is a specific DSM-5 diagnosis with its own criteria distinct from purely structural reflux.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rumination disorder can be diagnosed at a healthy weight, since failure to thrive is a possible sequela but not a required criterion — a common testable nuance. COMLEX: Rumination's effortless, pleasurable, arched-back pattern helps distinguish it from medical causes of regurgitation like pyloric stenosis or hiatal hernia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood feeding/eating disorder framework into the adolescent/adult eating disorder differential covered later in the block","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rumination disorder does not r"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":1,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has significant food selectivity and avoidance based on sensory features of food (texture aversion), resulting in significant weight loss and dependence on a nutritional supplement. There is no distorted body image and no history of anorexia nervosa or bulimia nervosa. Which diagnosis best fits, and which condition must specifically be excluded before making it?","choices":{"A":"ARFID, diagnosed from the feeding history without further evaluation","B":"Anorexia nervosa, requiring distorted body image and fear of weight gain","C":"Pica, requiring persistent ingestion of nonnutritive, nonfood substances","D":"Rumination disorder, requiring repeated effortless regurgitation of food","E":"ARFID, after excluding anorexia, bulimia, a medical cause, or food scarcity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder is characterized by a lack of interest in food or avoidance based on sensory features, with persistent failure to meet nutritional needs (weight loss, nutritional deficiency, dependence on supplements); we should not make the diagnosis in the context of anorexia nervosa or bulimia nervosa, or if caused by a medical condition, another mental disorder, or a genuine lack of available food — all of which must be excluded, exactly as this vignette specifies (no distorted body image, no anorexia/bulimia history). ARFID does require these specific exclusions before diagnosis; it is not diagnosed without ruling them out. Anorexia nervosa specifically involves body image distortion, explicitly absent in this child, ruling it out. Pica involves ingestion of nonnutritive substances, not food avoidance based on sensory features. Rumination disorder involves regurgitation of already-swallowed food, not food selectivity/avoidance based on sensory characteristics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARFID is diagnosed only after excluding anorexia nervosa, bulimia nervosa, a medical cause, another mental disorder, or genuine food scarcity — critically, ARFID lacks the body image distortion defining anorexia nervosa. COMLEX: This exclusion-based approach ensures ARFID is not mistaken for a body-image-driven eating disorder or a purely medical feeding problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 -> Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood feeding/eating disorder framework into the adolescent/adult eating disorder differential covered later in the block","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"ARFID does require these speci","B":"Anorexia nervosa specifically "}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":1,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy repeatedly passes feces into inappropriate places, occurring more than once a month for the past 4 months. Physical exam and history reveal no evidence of constipation or overflow incontinence. Below which age would this diagnosis not be made, according to DSM-5?","choices":{"A":"Age 2 years","B":"Age 5 years","C":"Age 8 years","D":"Age 4 years","E":"Age 12 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSM-5 excludes an encopresis diagnosis in a child under age 4 years, the age at which a typically developing child masters bowel continence; this 5-year-old boy, with symptoms occurring more than once monthly for 4 months, meets both the age and duration criteria for encopresis without constipation and overflow incontinence. Age 2 is too young; encopresis is specifically excluded below age 4, since a 2-year-old would not typically be expected to have mastered bowel control. Age 5 is the enuresis age threshold, not the encopresis threshold, which is age 4. Age 8 is well above the actual DSM-5 exclusion threshold of age 4. Age 12 is far above the actual threshold and would inappropriately exclude many legitimately diagnosable younger children.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Encopresis cannot be diagnosed before age 4, the age at which typically developing children master bowel continence — distinct from enuresis's age-5 threshold. COMLEX: Encopresis has severity specifiers (with or without constipation and overflow incontinence) that guide the treatment approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary psychiatric presentation framework into distinguishing primary elimination disorders from organic causes (Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, UTI)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Age 5 is the enuresis age thre"}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has an empty rectum, no desire to defecate, yet still has overflow of feces, with symptoms present since shortly after birth. Which condition must be ruled out before diagnosing this presentation as encopresis?","choices":{"A":"Urinary tract infection","B":"Hirschsprung disease","C":"Spina bifida occulta","D":"Diabetes insipidus","E":"Sleepwalking disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chief differential medical problem for encopresis is aganglionic megacolon, or Hirschsprung disease, in which a patient may have an empty rectum and no desire to defecate but still have an overflow of feces, with signs appearing shortly after birth — exactly this presentation. Urinary tract infection is a relevant organic exclusion for enuresis, not encopresis. Spina bifida occulta is a relevant organic exclusion for enuresis (a genitourinary/neurologic cause), not the chief encopresis differential described here. Diabetes insipidus is a cause of polyuria relevant to enuresis differential diagnosis, not encopresis. Sleepwalking disorder is a differential consideration for enuresis occurring during disturbed consciousness, not for encopresis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease is the chief organic differential for encopresis, presenting with an empty rectum, absent urge, and overflow soiling from birth — a classic Step 1 distinguishing vignette. COMLEX: Organic exclusion (Hirschsprung disease) must precede a functional encopresis diagnosis, paralleling the general primary-versus-secondary diagnostic framework.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary psychiatric presentation framework into distinguishing primary elimination disorders from organic causes (Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, UTI)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":1,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy wets the bed most nights, more than twice weekly for the past 4 months, with no daytime symptoms. Medical workup rules out urinary tract infection and structural abnormality. Which evidence-based intervention is generally the most effective first-line treatment?","choices":{"A":"Fluid restriction","B":"Desmopressin","C":"Imipramine","D":"Family psychotherapy","E":"Enuresis alarm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Classical conditioning with the bell (or buzzer) and pad (alarm) apparatus is generally the most effective treatment for enuresis, with dryness resulting in more than 50 percent of cases, and is a mainstay behavioral intervention once organic causes are excluded, as they were here. Fluid restriction alone, without alarm therapy, is a supportive measure, not the described most-effective intervention on its own. Desmopressin is a pharmacologic option that works by reducing nighttime urine output, considered when enuresis causes impairment and dietary/behavioral interventions are insufficient, not the mainstay approach described here. Imipramine works through anticholinergic and bladder-capacity effects but carries a considerable side-effect profile, and is not the mainstay approach described here. Psychotherapy addresses coexisting psychiatric or family difficulties, but the behavioral therapy approach, specifically alarm therapy, is described as the treatment of choice for the enuresis itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alarm (bell-and-pad) therapy is the most effective first-line evidence-based treatment for enuresis, achieving dryness in over 50% of cases, ahead of desmopressin or imipramine. COMLEX: Organic exclusion (UTI, structural abnormality) must precede initiating first-line behavioral enuresis treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary psychiatric presentation framework into distinguishing primary elimination disorders from organic causes (Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, UTI)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Psychotherapy addresses coexis"}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl presents with compulsive eating behavior and obesity, mild-to-moderate intellectual disability, hypogonadism, short stature, and small hands and feet. Genetic testing reveals a deletion at 15q11-15q13 of paternal origin. Which syndrome does this describe, and how would the clinical picture differ if the identical deletion were of maternal origin instead?","choices":{"A":"Prader-Willi syndrome from a deletion of either parental origin","B":"Prader-Willi syndrome, and a maternal-origin deletion produces Angelman syndrome","C":"Angelman syndrome from this paternal deletion, with Prader-Willi from maternal origin","D":"Fragile X syndrome, a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion on the X chromosome","E":"Cri-du-chat syndrome, a deletion on the short arm of chromosome 5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prader-Willi syndrome results from a deletion at 15q11-15q13 of paternal origin, producing compulsive eating, obesity, intellectual disability, hypogonadism, short stature, and small hands and feet, exactly as described; the identical 15q11-15q13 deletion of maternal origin instead produces Angelman syndrome, an example of genomic imprinting, in which the same chromosomal deletion produces a different phenotype depending on the parent of origin. It is not true that parental origin makes no phenotypic difference; this is precisely the described imprinting phenomenon distinguishing Prader-Willi from Angelman syndrome. The paternal-origin deletion produces Prader-Willi syndrome, not Angelman syndrome, and it is the maternal-origin deletion, not the paternal, that produces Angelman syndrome — this option reverses the correct parent-of-origin assignment. Fragile X syndrome results from a trinucleotide repeat expansion at Xq27.3, not a 15q11-15q13 deletion. Cri-du-chat syndrome results from a chromosome 5 deletion, not 15q11-15q13.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prader-Willi (paternal 15q11-15q13 deletion) versus Angelman (maternal 15q11-15q13 deletion) syndrome is the classic genomic imprinting teaching pair on Step 1. COMLEX: Both syndromes carry distinct behavioral phenotypes (Prader-Willi: hyperphagia/compulsivity; Angelman: happy demeanor, paroxysmal laughter) tested clinically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning testing framework into the genetic-syndrome-specific behavioral phenotypes underlying many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The paternal-origin deletion p"}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has severe intellectual disability, a happy disposition with paroxysmal laughter, hand-flapping, ataxia, epilepsy with a characteristic EEG pattern, and microcephaly. Genetic testing reveals a deletion on the maternal chromosome at 15q11-15q13 that includes the UBE3A gene. Which syndrome fits, and what is a notable behavioral feature distinguishing it from Prader-Willi syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prader-Willi syndrome, marked by a happy demeanor with frequent laughter","B":"Angelman syndrome, marked by compulsive hyperphagia and early obesity","C":"Cornelia de Lange syndrome, linked to a chromosome 9q33 abnormality","D":"Williams syndrome, caused by a 7q11-23 elastin-locus deletion","E":"Angelman syndrome, marked by a happy demeanor with frequent laughter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Angelman syndrome results from a maternal-origin deletion at 15q11-15q13, with loss of maternal UBE3A expression, producing a happy disposition with paroxysmal laughter, hand flapping, ataxia, epilepsy with a characteristic EEG, and microcephaly — exactly this presentation; this happy demeanor and paroxysmal laughter, rather than Prader-Willi's compulsive hyperphagia and obesity, is a hallmark distinguishing behavioral feature. Prader-Willi syndrome results from the paternal-origin version of this deletion and its hallmark behavioral features are compulsive eating/hyperphagia and obesity, not the happy demeanor and paroxysmal laughter described in this maternal-origin case. Angelman syndrome is distinguished by its happy demeanor, not by compulsive hyperphagia, which is the Prader-Willi behavioral hallmark instead. Cornelia de Lange syndrome is not caused by this 15q11-15q13 deletion; the course reading links it to chromosome 9q33. Williams syndrome results from a 7q11-23 elastin-locus deletion, not 15q11-15q13.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Angelman syndrome's maternal 15q11-15q13 deletion produces a happy demeanor with paroxysmal laughter, epilepsy, and ataxia, contrasting with Prader-Willi's paternal-deletion hyperphagia/obesity phenotype. COMLEX: Recognizing this imprinting-based phenotype pair is a classic genetics teaching point in pediatric neurodevelopmental assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning testing framework into the genetic-syndrome-specific behavioral phenotypes underlying many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Prader-Willi syndrome results ","B":"Angelman syndrome is distingui"}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Correlate specific genetic or metabolic syndromes associated with intellectual disability (e.g., Down syndrome, Fragile X, Prader-Willi, Angelman, Lesch-Nyhan, and PKU) with their characteristic genetic inheritance patterns, physical stigmata, and behavioral phenotype.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy presents with severe intellectual disability, self-mutilating behavior including lip- and finger-biting, choreoathetoid movements, spasticity, and gouty arthritis from markedly elevated uric acid levels. Which enzyme deficiency and inheritance pattern best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, autosomal recessive, the cause of PKU","B":"Hexosaminidase A deficiency, autosomal recessive, the cause of Tay-Sachs disease","C":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase deficiency, X-linked recessive","D":"FMR1 gene silencing, X-linked, causing fragile X syndrome's distinct phenotype","E":"Elastin gene deletion, autosomal dominant, causing Williams syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation, severe intellectual disability with compulsive self-mutilation (lip- and finger-biting), choreoathetosis, spasticity, and hyperuricemia with gouty arthritis, is characteristic of Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, caused by hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT) deficiency, an X-linked recessive purine metabolism disorder that results in uric acid overproduction and the described self-injurious behavioral phenotype. Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency causes PKU, an autosomal recessive disorder producing intellectual disability, hyperactivity, and eczema, not this self-mutilation/hyperuricemia/choreoathetosis triad. Hexosaminidase A deficiency causes Tay-Sachs disease, a distinct autosomal recessive lysosomal storage disorder with neurodegeneration, not this specific presentation. FMR1 gene silencing causes fragile X syndrome, an X-linked condition, but with a distinct phenotype (macroorchidism, long face, large ears), not self-mutilation and gouty arthritis. Elastin gene deletion causes Williams syndrome, an autosomal dominant condition with a distinct sociable, outgoing behavioral phenotype, not this self-injurious presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lesch-Nyhan syndrome's HGPRT deficiency, X-linked recessive purine salvage pathway defect, produces the classic triad of self-mutilation, choreoathetosis, and hyperuricemia/gout — a high-yield biochemistry-genetics-behavior link. COMLEX: This enzyme-deficiency-to-behavioral-phenotype correlation exemplifies the genetic syndrome framework underlying pediatric intellectual disability assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 4 — extends the IQ/adaptive-functioning testing framework into the genetic-syndrome-specific behavioral phenotypes underlying many cases of intellectual disability","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old's speech is slurred, with slow, effortful articulation attributed to incoordination of the speech musculature, occurring in the context of a known cerebral palsy diagnosis. His parents ask whether this qualifies as a DSM-5 speech sound disorder. What is the correct answer, and why?","choices":{"A":"Yes, since any articulation error qualifies as speech sound disorder regardless of cause","B":"No, since the child's age falls below the diagnostic threshold for this disorder","C":"No, since speech sound disorder requires an accompanying vocal tic component","D":"Yes, since cerebral palsy is itself a required diagnostic criterion for this disorder","E":"No, since an identifiable neurologic cause, like cerebral palsy, excludes this"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Speech sound disturbances such as dysarthria and dyspraxia are not diagnosed as speech sound disorder if they are known to have a neurologic basis, according to DSM-5; speech sound abnormalities accounted for by cerebral palsy, cleft palate, deafness, traumatic brain injury, or other neurologic conditions are specifically excluded from the diagnosis, exactly this child's situation. It is not true that any articulation error qualifies regardless of cause; a known neurologic basis specifically excludes the diagnosis. Speech sound disorder does not require a vocal tic component; tics are unrelated to this communication disorder category entirely. Cerebral palsy is not a required criterion for speech sound disorder; on the contrary, it is one of the neurologic conditions that excludes the diagnosis when present. The child's age is not the reason for exclusion here; the reason is specifically the known neurologic basis (cerebral palsy) of his dysarthria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Speech sound disorder specifically excludes dysarthria/dyspraxia with a known neurologic basis (cerebral palsy, cleft palate, deafness, TBI) — a key DSM-5 exclusion-criteria teaching point. COMLEX: Recognizing organic causes of speech disturbance prevents misdiagnosing a neurologic condition as a primary developmental communication disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing neurologically based dysarthria from a primary DSM-5 communication disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cerebral palsy is not a requir"}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has a language disorder and significantly delayed developmental milestones. His parents ask what evaluation should be obtained before finalizing this diagnosis, given that hearing impairment can present similarly. What is the appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Obtain an audiogram, to assess auditory acuity as a possible cause of the delay","B":"Obtain no further workup, once a language disorder is suspected on clinical grounds","C":"Obtain a karyotype, treating chromosomal analysis as the required first diagnostic step","D":"Obtain a brain MRI, treating imaging as the required first step for this presentation","E":"Obtain a Vineland assessment, treating adaptive-functioning testing as the required step"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Children with speech and language disorders should have an audiogram to rule out or confirm the presence of deafness or hearing loss before finalizing a communication disorder diagnosis, since hearing impairment is a key organic cause that can mimic or contribute to language delay. Skipping further workup once a language disorder is clinically suspected ignores the specifically described need to exclude hearing impairment via audiogram first. A karyotype is not the described required first step for this specific presentation; an audiogram addresses the most directly relevant and described exclusion (hearing impairment). An MRI is not the described required first step here; it is not specifically indicated by this presentation absent other red flags. The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales assesses adaptive functioning broadly, useful for intellectual disability evaluation, but not the specifically indicated audiogram needed to exclude hearing impairment as the cause of this language delay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An audiogram is the specifically indicated first step before finalizing a communication disorder diagnosis, since hearing impairment must be excluded as a mimicking or contributing cause. COMLEX: This exclusion step exemplifies the broader principle of ruling out organic/sensory causes before diagnosing a primary developmental communication disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing hearing-impairment-driven language delay from a primary DSM-5 communication disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across DSM-5 communication disorders by assessing expressive/receptive language milestones, phonemic articulation errors, speech motor fluency, and social contextual rules, while ruling out secondary organic causes (e.g., hearing impairment, dysarthria, or Autism Spectrum Disorder).","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old has difficulty understanding the context of conversations, does not adjust her communication style for different listeners or settings, and struggles with the literal-versus-intended meaning of statements, despite otherwise adequate vocabulary, grammar, and articulation, and no repetitive behaviors or restricted interests. Which DSM-5 diagnosis best fits, and which condition must be distinguished from it?","choices":{"A":"Language disorder, set apart from intellectual developmental disorder","B":"Autism spectrum disorder, set apart from social, pragmatic, communication disorder","C":"Speech sound disorder, set apart from childhood-onset fluency disorder","D":"Social, pragmatic, communication disorder, set apart from autism spectrum disorder","E":"Selective mutism, set apart from generalized social anxiety disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child's difficulty with the social use of language, pragmatics, understanding context, and adjusting communication for the listener, despite intact vocabulary, grammar, and articulation, describes social, pragmatic, communication disorder; distinguishing it from autism spectrum disorder specifically requires the absence of restricted, repetitive behaviors or interests, which this child does not have. Language disorder specifically involves vocabulary, grammar, and semantic deficits, which are intact in this child; her deficit is specifically pragmatic, social use, not language structure. Autism spectrum disorder requires restricted, repetitive behaviors or interests in addition to social communication deficits, which are explicitly absent here, ruling it out. Speech sound disorder involves articulation errors, intact in this child. Selective mutism involves a failure to speak in specific situations despite speaking elsewhere, not a pragmatic language-use deficit across contexts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Social, pragmatic, communication disorder involves deficits in the social use of language and must be distinguished from autism spectrum disorder specifically by the absence of restricted, repetitive behaviors or interests. COMLEX: This distinction is essential since management (speech/language pragmatics therapy versus autism-specific behavioral intervention) differs substantially between the two.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — extends the organic-versus-nonfocal exam differentiation framework into distinguishing a primary pragmatic communication disorder from autism spectrum disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Language disorder specifically","B":"Autism spectrum disorder requi"}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old has had persistent, significant reading difficulty for 5 months despite an intensive, targeted reading intervention program. His teacher and parents want to know whether specific learning disorder can be diagnosed now. What is the correct answer based on DSM-5's duration criterion?","choices":{"A":"Yes, since any persistent difficulty despite intervention qualifies immediately","B":"Yes, since DSM-5 sets no minimum symptom duration for this diagnosis","C":"Not yet, since symptoms must persist at least 6 months despite intervention","D":"No, since specific learning disorder requires a minimum age of 12 years","E":"Not yet, since DSM-5 requires at least 12 months of persistent difficulty"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 requires specific learning disorder symptoms to persist for at least 6 months despite intervention targeted at the difficulty; this child has only had 5 months of documented difficulty despite intervention, so the duration criterion is not yet met. It is not correct that any persistent difficulty immediately qualifies regardless of duration; the specific 6-month threshold must be met first. It is not correct that there is no minimum duration requirement; DSM-5 specifically requires at least 6 months. It is not correct that specific learning disorder can never be diagnosed in a 9-year-old; academic-age children are precisely the population in which this diagnosis is made once criteria, including the 6-month duration, are met. The duration threshold is 6 months, not 12 months, so this option states an incorrect threshold even though it reaches a similar \"not yet\" conclusion for the wrong reason.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Specific learning disorder requires a minimum 6-month duration of persistent difficulty despite targeted intervention — a specific, testable DSM-5 duration threshold. COMLEX: This duration criterion, alongside domain-specific deficits and exclusion criteria, forms the complete diagnostic framework for specific learning disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into Specific Learning Disorder's explicit 6-month duration threshold","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It is not correct that there i","E":"The duration threshold is 6 mo"}},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old has documented, persistent reading difficulty meeting DSM-5 duration and domain criteria for specific learning disorder with impairment in reading. Her overall WISC-III-R full-scale IQ is average. Per current DSM-5 diagnostic practice, is a discrepancy between her specific reading deficit and her overall IQ score a required diagnostic criterion?","choices":{"A":"Yes, a significant IQ-achievement discrepancy remains a required DSM-5 diagnostic criterion","B":"Yes, but for mathematics deficits specifically, and not for reading deficits","C":"No, since DSM-5 instead simply requires her IQ score to fall below 70 outright","D":"No, DSM-5 no longer requires that IQ-achievement comparison as a criterion","E":"No, since specific learning disorder has actually been fully removed from DSM-5"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In DSM-5, there is no longer a diagnostic criterion for specific learning disorder comparing the specific deficit to overall IQ; the current consensus is that children with reading impairment have trouble with phonemic processing and word recognition regardless of their IQ, so an IQ-achievement discrepancy is not required. A significant IQ-achievement discrepancy is not a required DSM-5 criterion; this represents outdated diagnostic practice that DSM-5 has moved away from. This is not limited to only excluding the discrepancy requirement for mathematics; DSM-5 removed the discrepancy requirement across all specific learning disorder domains, including reading. Specific learning disorder does not require an IQ below 70; that threshold is more associated with intellectual disability severity classification, and this child's average IQ does not preclude her specific learning disorder diagnosis. Specific learning disorder has not been removed from DSM-5; it remains a current diagnostic category, just without the IQ-discrepancy requirement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5 removed the IQ-achievement discrepancy requirement for specific learning disorder — a frequently tested update distinguishing current from historical (DSM-IV-era) diagnostic practice. COMLEX: This reflects the modern understanding that phonemic-processing deficits, not IQ discrepancy, drive most reading-specific learning disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into clarifying that Specific Learning Disorder no longer requires an IQ-achievement discrepancy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A significant IQ-achievement d","E":"Specific learning disorder has"}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate a diagnostic assessment and management strategy for Specific Learning Disorder by evaluating persistent domain-specific academic deficits (in reading/dyslexia, mathematics/dyscalculia, or written expression/dysgraphia) that persist for at least 6 months despite intervention, ruling out global causes (such as intellectual disability or sensory deficits), and selecting evidence-based psychoeducational accommodations.","batch":2,"stem":"A school team is developing an individualized education plan for an 11-year-old with specific learning disorder with impairment in written expression, who needs concentrated, one-on-one, dedicated instruction for nearly the entire school day. According to DSM-5 severity specifiers, which severity level does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Mild, the specifier requiring minimal accommodations or support in school","B":"Severe, the specifier for near-continuous, high-intensity instructional support","C":"Moderate, the specifier requiring some periodic accommodations or targeted support","D":"Subclinical, the specifier for difficulties below the diagnostic threshold","E":"In remission, the specifier for difficulties that have since fully resolved"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DSM-5's severe specifier for specific learning disorder is defined by the need for intensive, individualized, and specialized teaching for most of the time in school, exactly matching this child's described needs. Mild severity requires only minimal accommodations or support needed in school, not intensive individualized teaching most of the time, as described here. Moderate severity requires some accommodations needed, a lesser degree of support than the intensive, individualized teaching most of the time described in this case. Subclinical is not a DSM-5 severity specifier for specific learning disorder at all. In remission is also not one of the three DSM-5 severity specifiers (mild, moderate, severe) for this disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's three severity specifiers for specific learning disorder (mild: minimal accommodations; moderate: some accommodations; severe: intensive individualized teaching most of the time) directly guide IEP-level educational planning. COMLEX: Matching severity specifier to support intensity is central to evidence-based psychoeducational accommodation selection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into Specific Learning Disorder's severity specifiers guiding accommodation intensity","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Subclinical is not a DSM-5 sev"}},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with confirmed Tourette disorder and comorbid ADHD is being considered for a noradrenergic agent that could address both the tics and ADHD symptoms simultaneously, given the risks of stimulants exacerbating his tics. Which class of agent and rationale best fits this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"Stimulants, dopaminergic agents that are first-line for ADHD generally","B":"Typical antipsychotics, potent D2 antagonists used for tic suppression","C":"Benzodiazepines, GABA-A positive modulators used for acute anxiety","D":"SSRIs, used for comorbid obsessive-compulsive symptoms in tic disorders","E":"Alpha2-adrenergic agonists like clonidine or guanfacine, reducing tics and hyperactivity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Noradrenergic agents, including clonidine and guanfacine, are sometimes preferred treatments in children with ADHD and comorbid tic disorders when stimulants exacerbated the tics, addressing both conditions through alpha2-adrenergic mechanisms with an evidence base for both ADHD and tic reduction. Stimulants are not the appropriate choice here specifically because they can exacerbate tics in some patients, the exact concern raised in this vignette, making an alternative agent preferable. Typical antipsychotics are used specifically to treat tics, not typically as a first consideration for comorbid ADHD management, and carry a less favorable side-effect profile than atypical agents or alpha2-agonists for this combined indication. Benzodiazepines are not described as having an evidence base for comorbid ADHD and tic disorders in this framework. SSRIs are used for comorbid obsessive-compulsive symptoms in tic disorder patients, not described as addressing comorbid ADHD and tics together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alpha2-adrenergic agonists (clonidine, guanfacine) are preferred over stimulants in children with comorbid ADHD and tic disorders when stimulants exacerbate tics, addressing both conditions via a shared noradrenergic mechanism. COMLEX: This mechanism-matched, comorbidity-aware pharmacologic selection is a key step-wise management principle for pediatric motor disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stimulants are not the appropr","C":"Benzodiazepines are not descri"}},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old with Tourette disorder undergoing habit reversal training is coached to deliberately engage his shoulder muscles in an opposing motion that cannot occur at the same moment as his shoulder-shrugging tic, cueing this action as soon as he notices the urge building, before the tic itself fires. Which specific component of habit reversal training does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Competing-response training, engaging an incompatible opposing movement","B":"Awareness training, the self-monitoring component that enhances urge recognition","C":"Exposure and response prevention, building tolerance to the premonitory urge","D":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy for anxiety, targeting catastrophic thoughts","E":"Bladder training, an enuresis technique using scheduled voiding intervals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In competing-response training, the patient learns to voluntarily perform a behavior that is physically incompatible with the tic, contingent on the onset of the premonitory urge or the tic itself, blocking expression of the tic — exactly this description. Awareness training is the other primary component of habit reversal, in which the child uses self-monitoring to enhance awareness of tic behaviors and premonitory urges, distinct from the voluntary incompatible-behavior component described here. Exposure and response prevention is a distinct behavioral treatment for tics, asking the patient to suppress tics for increasingly prolonged periods to break the urge-tic association, rather than performing a competing incompatible behavior. Cognitive-behavioral therapy for anxiety is unrelated to this tic-specific behavioral technique. Bladder training is an enuresis-specific behavioral intervention, unrelated to tic management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Competing-response training, a core habit reversal component, has the patient perform a tic-incompatible voluntary behavior triggered by the premonitory urge, distinct from awareness training and exposure/response prevention. COMLEX: Distinguishing habit reversal's specific components clarifies the mechanism behind this evidence-based first-line behavioral tic treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a diagnostic and step-wise management plan for pediatric motor disorders by evaluating clinical features to distinguish functional motor coordination delays and self-soothing stereotypies from Tourette Disorder (multiple motor tics ≥ 1 vocal tic persisting > 1 year with onset before age 18), and selecting appropriate first-line behavioral therapies (e.g., Habit Reversal Training/CBIT) and mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (e.g., alpha2-adrenergic agonists or dopamine2-receptor antagonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old with severe, treatment-refractory Tourette disorder has failed adequate trials of habit reversal training and risperidone. The team is considering aripiprazole. What is the mechanistic rationale for aripiprazole's use compared with a pure D2 antagonist like haloperidol?","choices":{"A":"Aripiprazole is a pure D2 receptor antagonist, as haloperidol is","B":"Aripiprazole is a partial D2 and 5-HT1A agonist, and a 5-HT2A antagonist","C":"Aripiprazole acts through alpha2-adrenergic agonism, as clonidine does","D":"Aripiprazole acts through serotonin reuptake inhibition, as an SSRI does","E":"Aripiprazole has no dopaminergic activity and acts at histamine receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aripiprazole has become a pharmacologic agent of interest in tic disorder treatment because, in addition to its D2 receptor antagonistic actions, it is also a partial D2 and 5-HT1A receptor agonist and a 5-HT2A antagonist, giving it a distinct mechanistic profile from pure D2 antagonists like haloperidol, with sedation and sleep disturbance as common side effects but less pronounced weight gain than risperidone. Aripiprazole is not a pure D2 antagonist identical to haloperidol; its partial agonist activity at D2 and 5-HT1A receptors and antagonism at 5-HT2A distinguish its mechanism. It does not act exclusively through alpha2-adrenergic agonism; that describes clonidine/guanfacine, a distinct medication class. It does not act exclusively through serotonin reuptake inhibition; that describes SSRIs, a distinct medication class used for comorbid OCD symptoms, not the described antipsychotic mechanism. It is not without dopaminergic activity; its partial D2 agonist/antagonist activity is central to its mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aripiprazole's partial D2/5-HT1A agonism plus 5-HT2A antagonism distinguishes its mechanism from pure D2 antagonists (haloperidol) and from D2/5-HT2 antagonists (risperidone), each with a distinct side-effect profile. COMLEX: Selecting among antipsychotic options by mechanism and side-effect profile is central to step-wise Tourette disorder pharmacotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 -> Obj 1 — extends the MSE-based primary-vs-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing functional motor stereotypies from organically based Tourette disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old girl presents with pallor, abdominal pain, and lack of appetite. Examination reveals she is thin and withdrawn, and her mother reports she chews on newspaper and puts plaster in her mouth. Laboratory workup reveals anemia and elevated blood lead levels. The psychiatric evaluation reveals maternal overwhelm caring for five children in a crowded household with limited affection given to this child. Which combination of findings and interventions best reflects the appropriate diagnosis and management approach?","choices":{"A":"Pica, requiring lead elimination, psychosocial intervention, and medical treatment","B":"Pica, requiring isolated pharmacotherapy, without any environmental or family intervention","C":"Rumination disorder, requiring behavioral distraction techniques during each feeding","D":"ARFID, requiring sensory-based feeding therapy for this presentation as described","E":"Failure to thrive from a purely nutritional cause, requiring caloric supplementation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation, nonnutritive substance ingestion (newspaper, plaster) leading to lead poisoning and anemia in a young child from an under-resourced, overwhelmed caregiving environment, is pica; treatment addresses toxic exposure elimination (removing lead sources), behavioral techniques, and importantly, psychosocial/family intervention addressing parental overwhelm, neglect risk, and emotional nurturance, alongside treating medical complications like lead poisoning and anemia — a specifically described multimodal approach in cases involving child neglect or maltreatment risk factors. Pharmacotherapy alone is not the described treatment approach for pica; environmental/family intervention is specifically emphasized, especially when neglect-related factors are present. Rumination disorder involves regurgitation of previously swallowed food, not ingestion of nonnutritive substances like newspaper and plaster. ARFID involves food avoidance/restriction based on sensory features or low interest, not ingestion of nonfood items. This is not simply failure to thrive from a purely nutritional cause requiring only caloric supplementation; the specific nonnutritive substance ingestion pattern and its psychosocial context define this as pica requiring the broader multimodal approach.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pica management is multimodal — eliminating toxic exposure (lead), behavioral techniques, and psychosocial/family intervention when neglect or caregiver overwhelm contributes, alongside treating medical complications. COMLEX: This case exemplifies how early childhood feeding/eating disorder management extends beyond the individual child into the caregiving environment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood pica case into the broader adolescent/adult eating disorder differential and medical-risk assessment covered later in the block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old boy began regurgitating milk after feedings, which resolved with feeding thickening and gastroesophageal reflux treatment. He then developed recurrent regurgitation again after his mother returned to work and transitioned his care to a nanny, worsening over 2 weeks. Observation reveals he purposefully places his hand in his mouth after feeding to induce regurgitation, and continues rhythmic tongue movement even without producing more milk, appearing to enjoy the behavior. Which diagnosis and first-line management approach best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease, managed with a proton pump inhibitor","B":"Pica, managed with removal of nonnutritive substances","C":"ARFID, managed with graded exposure to new foods","D":"Failure to thrive, managed with a protective services referral","E":"Rumination disorder, managed with distraction during and after feeds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation, purposeful hand-in-mouth and rhythmic tongue movements inducing regurgitation that the infant appears to find pleasurable, emerging or worsening around a disruption in caregiving (mother returning to work, transition to a nanny), is rumination disorder; behavioral techniques, particularly distraction and increased engagement during and after feeding, are the primary treatment approach, as opposed to continued escalation of antireflux medication, which had already been tried without addressing the behaviorally driven, self-induced component. Gastroesophageal reflux disease would not explain the purposeful hand-in-mouth induction and pleasurable rhythmic tongue movements described, and antireflux treatment had already been tried; a proton pump inhibitor does not address the behavior. Pica involves ingestion of nonnutritive substances, not induced regurgitation of swallowed food. ARFID involves food avoidance or restriction based on sensory features, not induced regurgitation after adequate feeding. Nor is this failure to thrive needing a protective services referral; the vignette describes a specific behavioral feeding disorder that responds to behavioral intervention with the existing caregivers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rumination disorder's purposeful, self-induced, pleasurable regurgitation pattern is managed primarily with behavioral distraction/engagement techniques, distinct from escalating gastroesophageal reflux pharmacotherapy. COMLEX: Recognizing rumination's behavioral, self-soothing mechanism (rather than purely structural reflux) is key to selecting the effective, non-pharmacologic first-line intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood rumination disorder case into the broader adolescent/adult eating disorder differential and medical-risk assessment covered later in the block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, diagnostic criteria, and medical complications of early childhood feeding and eating disorders (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder [ARFID]) by evaluating age thresholds (e.g., minimum 2 years for Pica), physical and laboratory manifestations (e.g., lead intoxication, microcytic anemia, or malnutrition), and behavioral mechanisms while ruling out structural gastrointestinal diseases and body image distortions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has been unable to establish adequate feeding since birth: she turns away from breastfeeding, drinks only small amounts before crying and refusing to continue, and has gained minimal weight, remaining below expected weight for age. She is alert, wiggly, and has difficulty staying calm and still during feedings, with normal developmental milestones otherwise. There is no vomiting, regurgitation, or ingestion of nonfood items. Which diagnosis and intervention target best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Rumination disorder, targeting behavioral distraction techniques during each feeding","B":"Pica, targeting elimination of nonnutritive substance access from the environment","C":"ARFID, targeting the feeding interaction and the infant's calming needs","D":"Anorexia nervosa, targeting body-image-focused psychotherapy for this infant","E":"Normal infant feeding variation, requiring no active intervention"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation, persistent inadequate food/milk intake with failure to achieve expected weight since birth, without vomiting, regurgitation, or nonfood ingestion, in an infant with a difficult, easily overstimulated temperament, is avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (feeding disorder of infancy); intervention optimizes the mother-infant interaction during feedings, addressing the infant's biologic regulation, fatigue level, and calming needs before and during meals, exactly the approach described for this feeding-disorder pattern. Rumination disorder involves regurgitation of already-swallowed food, absent in this case. Pica involves nonnutritive substance ingestion, also absent here. Anorexia nervosa involves body image distortion, which is not applicable to a 6-month-old infant and not part of this clinical picture; body-image-focused psychotherapy is not an appropriate intervention for an infant. This is not simply a normal feeding variation requiring no intervention; the persistent inadequate intake with resultant low weight since birth meets criteria for a feeding disorder requiring active intervention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARFID in infancy centers on optimizing the mother-infant feeding interaction and the infant's calming/regulation needs, distinct from the substance-ingestion focus of pica or the regurgitation focus of rumination disorder. COMLEX: This case demonstrates ARFID's definition by lack of interest/avoidance rather than body image distortion, distinguishing it from anorexia nervosa even when weight loss is present.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — forward bridge: extends this early-childhood ARFID case into the broader adolescent/adult eating disorder differential and medical-risk assessment covered later in the block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has daily encopresis with liquid stool leaking into his underwear. Abdominal exam and x-ray reveal a large fecal mass in the rectum and colon, with a history of previously painful bowel movements that he has come to fear. Which pathophysiologic mechanism and first-line treatment best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"An aganglionic distal colon, treated with surgical resection","B":"Spinal dysraphism with a neurogenic bowel, treated with a bowel management program","C":"Hypothyroidism slowing colonic transit, treated with levothyroxine","D":"Withholding with impaction and overflow, treated with disimpaction and scheduled toileting","E":"Oppositional refusal without constipation, treated with parent management training"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The described history (previously painful defecation leading to fear-driven withholding) and exam findings (large fecal mass, liquid overflow stool) reflect chronic stool withholding causing fecal impaction with overflow incontinence, the most common encopresis mechanism, managed with disimpaction followed by maintenance laxatives such as polyethylene glycol at a standard dose and cognitive-behavioral scheduled toileting to reduce withholding and defecation-related anxiety. An aganglionic distal colon (Hirschsprung disease) presents from infancy with an empty rectum on exam rather than the large fecal mass described here, and it is managed surgically. Spinal dysraphism can cause a neurogenic bowel, but it comes with neurologic signs such as a sacral dimple, leg weakness, or bladder dysfunction, whereas this history describes fear-driven withholding after painful stools. Hypothyroidism can slow colonic transit, but nothing here suggests it, and the fear of painful defecation points to a behavioral mechanism. Encopresis without constipation, sometimes accompanied by oppositional behavior, by definition lacks the fecal mass seen on this x-ray.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic stool withholding after painful defecation leading to fecal impaction and overflow soiling is the most common encopresis mechanism, first-line treated with disimpaction, maintenance PEG, and scheduled toileting behavioral therapy. COMLEX: This withholding-impaction pathophysiology explains why laxatives alone, without addressing the behavioral fear component, are often insufficient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary psychiatric presentation framework into distinguishing functional stool-withholding encopresis from an organic cause requiring different first-line management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has both nocturnal and diurnal enuresis, combined with urinary urgency and frequency. Which category of organic causes should be prioritized in her workup, given this specific combination of symptoms, compared with a child who has isolated nocturnal-only enuresis?","choices":{"A":"Genitourinary pathology, such as urinary infection, uropathy, or spina bifida occulta","B":"Isolated psychosocial stressors, evaluated with a family and school history","C":"Hirschsprung disease, an aganglionic segment causing chronic constipation","D":"Primary tic disorder, evaluated with a history of motor and vocal tics","E":"A specific learning disorder, evaluated with standardized academic testing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Urinary tract infections, obstructions, or anatomical conditions are found most often in children who experience both nocturnal and diurnal enuresis combined with urinary frequency and urgency, and genitourinary pathology (obstructive uropathy, spina bifida occulta, cystitis) should specifically be considered in this combined-symptom pattern, unlike isolated nocturnal-only enuresis, which more often reflects a primary, non-organic pattern. Psychosocial stressors alone, with no organic workup, would be inappropriate given this specific combined nocturnal/diurnal pattern with urgency and frequency, which is specifically described as raising organic suspicion. Hirschsprung disease is the chief organic differential for encopresis, not enuresis. Primary tic disorder is unrelated to elimination symptoms entirely. Specific learning disorder is an academic-skills diagnosis, entirely unrelated to this urinary symptom pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The combination of nocturnal plus diurnal enuresis with urgency/frequency specifically raises suspicion for genitourinary pathology (UTI, obstructive uropathy, spina bifida occulta), unlike isolated nocturnal enuresis. COMLEX: Symptom pattern (isolated nocturnal vs. combined nocturnal/diurnal with urgency) directs which organic workup is prioritized.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary psychiatric presentation framework into recognizing which enuresis symptom patterns specifically warrant a genitourinary organic workup","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Formulate a step-wise evaluation and treatment strategy for pediatric elimination disorders by differentiating developmental age thresholds (≥ 4 years for encopresis vs. ≥ 5 years for enuresis), ruling out underlying organic pathology (such as Hirschsprung disease, spina bifida occulta, or urinary tract infections), and selecting appropriate evidence-based first-line behavioral conditioning (e.g., enuresis alarm therapy, scheduled toileting) and pharmacotherapy (e.g., polyethylene glycol for constipation/overflow; desmopressin or imipramine for refractory enuresis).","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has refractory nocturnal enuresis despite adequate trials of alarm therapy and fluid restriction, causing significant social impairment (avoiding sleepovers, camp). The family wants to discuss pharmacotherapy options and their respective risk profiles. Which comparison of desmopressin and imipramine best reflects their evidence base and safety considerations?","choices":{"A":"Desmopressin risks hepatic failure, while imipramine risks agranulocytosis on long-term use","B":"Both agents are first-line for enuresis, ahead of alarm therapy and fluid restriction","C":"Desmopressin risks hyponatremic seizure, while imipramine risks anticholinergic toxicity","D":"Imipramine is the newer agent and the better tolerated of the two in children","E":"Desmopressin and imipramine both act through the same anticholinergic mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Desmopressin, an antidiuretic compound, has shown variable success but its most severe reported adverse effect is a hyponatremic seizure; imipramine, historically the first medication used for enuresis, works through anticholinergic side effects but carries a considerable overall side-effect profile, and both are considered pharmacologic options specifically once behavioral therapy (alarm therapy, dietary measures) alone has proven insufficient, exactly as in this refractory case. Hepatic failure and agranulocytosis are not the risks described for these agents; desmopressin's most severe reported effect is hyponatremic seizure, and imipramine's burden is its anticholinergic side-effect profile. Behavioral therapy (specifically alarm therapy) is described as the first-line treatment of choice, not either of these pharmacologic agents; pharmacotherapy is considered after behavioral approaches prove insufficient, as in this case. Imipramine is the older of the two agents, historically the first medication used for enuresis, not a newer or better-tolerated option than desmopressin. Desmopressin is an antidiuretic hormone analog working on renal water reabsorption, not through the anticholinergic mechanism that imipramine relies on.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Desmopressin's hyponatremic-seizure risk and imipramine's anticholinergic side-effect burden are the key safety considerations when escalating to pharmacotherapy for refractory enuresis after behavioral therapy fails. COMLEX: This risk-profile comparison directly informs step-wise, evidence-based refractory-enuresis management discussions with families.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — synthesizes the primary-versus-secondary and step-wise management framework into weighing pharmacotherapy risk profiles for refractory pediatric elimination disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic failure and agranulocy","E":"Desmopressin is an antidiureti"}}],"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":1,"stem":"A young child raised in an institutional setting with grossly negligent caregiving shows a consistent pattern of emotionally withdrawn behavior toward adults, limited positive affect, and minimal social responsiveness, with rare seeking of or response to comfort when distressed. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder","B":"Reactive attachment disorder","C":"Autism spectrum disorder","D":"Major depressive disorder","E":"Selective mutism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reactive attachment disorder is a consistent pattern of emotionally withdrawn responses toward adult caregivers, limited positive affect, and minimal social responsiveness, presumed due to grossly pathologic caregiving — exactly this presentation. Disinhibited social engagement disorder instead shows undifferentiated, unselective, overly familiar social relatedness with unfamiliar adults, the opposite of this withdrawn pattern. Autism spectrum disorder is not presumed to arise from caregiving deprivation, and children with autism spectrum disorder are typically well-nourished and alert despite social impairments. Major depressive disorder is a mood disorder diagnosis, not this specific caregiving-deprivation-based attachment disturbance. Selective mutism involves failure to speak in specific situations despite speaking elsewhere, not this global pattern of emotional withdrawal and minimal comfort-seeking.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reactive attachment disorder's hallmark is emotional withdrawal and minimal comfort-seeking from caregivers, presumed secondary to grossly pathologic caregiving — the polar opposite presentation from disinhibited social engagement disorder. COMLEX: Reactive attachment disorder is a named Trauma and stressor-related disorder requiring documented pathogenic caregiving as its presumed cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — extends the trauma/stressor-related-disorder symptom-domain-and-timing differentiation framework into pediatric caregiving-deprivation attachment disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":1,"stem":"A young child with a history of repeated changes in caregivers actively approaches and interacts with unfamiliar adults in an overly familiar way, both verbally and physically, checks back with caregivers less often than expected in unfamiliar situations, and is willing to go off with a stranger without hesitation. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Reactive attachment disorder, marked by emotional withdrawal and flat affect","B":"Social anxiety disorder, marked by fear and avoidance of social scrutiny","C":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, marked by inattention and impulsivity","D":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder, marked by boundary-free sociability","E":"Conduct disorder, marked by rights violations and destructive behavior"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder is characterized by actively approaching and interacting with unfamiliar adults in an overly familiar way, reduced checking back with caregivers, and willingness to go off with strangers without hesitation, presumed due to pathogenic caregiving such as repeated changes in caregivers — exactly this presentation. Reactive attachment disorder instead shows emotional withdrawal and minimal social responsiveness, the opposite of this overly familiar pattern. Social anxiety disorder involves fear and avoidance of social situations, not indiscriminate over-familiarity with strangers. ADHD alone does not account for these behaviors, per DSM-5, since disinhibited social engagement disorder behaviors are specifically not accounted for by impulsivity; disinhibited social engagement disorder can be comorbid with ADHD but is a distinct diagnosis presumed to be caregiving-based. Conduct disorder involves violating the rights of others and destructive behavior, not this pattern of indiscriminate social familiarity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Disinhibited social engagement disorder's indiscriminate over-familiarity with strangers, reduced caregiver checking-back, and willingness to leave with strangers are not explained by impulsivity alone, distinguishing it from ADHD. COMLEX: Both reactive attachment disorder and disinhibited social engagement disorder require documented pathogenic caregiving (neglect, repeated caregiver changes, institutional care) as the presumed cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — extends the trauma/stressor-related-disorder symptom-domain-and-timing differentiation framework into pediatric caregiving-deprivation attachment disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"ADHD alone does not account fo"}},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is evaluating a child with a history of severe early neglect for possible reactive attachment disorder or disinhibited social engagement disorder. Below which developmental age would DSM-5 preclude making either diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"A developmental age of 9 months, by which selective attachment normally forms","B":"A developmental age of 2 months, before social smiling is reliably established","C":"A developmental age of 24 months, once symbolic play is normally established","D":"A developmental age of 5 years, once peer relationships are well established","E":"No minimum developmental age, with the criteria resting on caregiving history"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DSM-5 requires that a child have a developmental age of at least 9 months before diagnosing either reactive attachment disorder or disinhibited social engagement disorder, exactly the threshold this vignette asks about. A developmental age of 2 months is far younger than the actual 9-month threshold. 24 months and 5 years are both older than the actual minimum threshold, and would inappropriately exclude legitimately diagnosable younger children. No minimum age applying is incorrect; DSM-5 specifically requires this 9-month developmental-age threshold for both disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both reactive attachment disorder and disinhibited social engagement disorder require a minimum developmental age of 9 months before diagnosis, a specific, testable DSM-5 threshold shared by both disorders. COMLEX: Both diagnoses additionally require the pattern of disturbed attachment to be present before age 5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — extends the trauma/stressor-related-disorder symptom-domain-and-timing differentiation framework into pediatric caregiving-deprivation attachment disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A developmental age of 2 month","E":"No minimum age applying is inc"}},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy presents with pervasive low mood, poor concentration, and anhedonia. His mother describes him as constantly irritable and prone to angry outbursts rather than overtly sad. Which DSM-5 age-adjusted criterion allows this presentation to still meet diagnostic criteria for a depressive episode?","choices":{"A":"Anhedonia is sufficient on its own, with no mood-quality criterion required","B":"A minimum 2-year symptom duration is required before the diagnosis applies","C":"Irritable mood may substitute for depressed mood as the core criterion","D":"A manic or hypomanic episode must be documented before the diagnosis applies","E":"Somatic symptoms replace the mood criterion entirely in this age group"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 specifically allows irritable mood to substitute for the depressed mood criterion in children and adolescents, recognizing that irritability rather than overt sadness is a common presentation of pediatric depressive disorders — exactly this boy's presentation. Anhedonia alone, without any mood criterion being met (via depressed or irritable mood), is not sufficient by itself. A 2-year duration is the dysthymia/persistent depressive disorder criterion for adults; the pediatric threshold for that specific diagnosis is 1 year, not 2. A prior manic or hypomanic episode would preclude, not support, a diagnosis of persistent depressive disorder or major depressive disorder. Somatic symptoms are not the primary age-adjusted diagnostic substitution described here; irritable mood substituting for depressed mood is the specific age-adjusted DSM-5 criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's irritable-mood-substitution criterion for pediatric depression is a classic testable age-adjustment, since children and adolescents often present with irritability rather than overt sadness. COMLEX: Recognizing this age-adjusted presentation prevents underdiagnosis of pediatric depressive disorders presenting primarily as irritability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — extends the inpatient-hospitalization/suicide-risk-assessment framework into pediatric depression's age-adjusted criteria and TADS-trial evidence base","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatic symptoms are not the p"}},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":1,"stem":"A 13-year-old girl has had a depressed and irritable mood for most of the day, more days than not, accompanied by low self-esteem and poor concentration, without symptoms resolving for more than 2 months at a time. What is the minimum duration required in children and adolescents to diagnose persistent depressive disorder, per DSM-5?","choices":{"A":"2 years, with the mood disturbance present more days than not","B":"1 month, with the mood disturbance present more days than not","C":"6 months, with the mood disturbance present more days than not","D":"1 year, with the mood disturbance present more days than not","E":"18 months, with the mood disturbance present more days than not"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSM-5 specifies that the duration criterion for persistent depressive disorder is not 2 years but 1 year for children and adolescents, exactly matching this 13-year-old's presentation. 2 years is the adult duration threshold, not the age-adjusted pediatric threshold. 1 month is far shorter than the actual pediatric threshold of 1 year. 6 months is also shorter than the actual 1-year pediatric threshold. 18 months exceeds the pediatric threshold; DSM-5 sets 1 year for children and adolescents, reserving 2 years for adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent depressive disorder's duration threshold is age-adjusted — 1 year for children/adolescents versus 2 years for adults, a frequently tested DSM-5 age modification. COMLEX: When symptoms exceed 1 year without a full major depressive episode in the first year, persistent depressive disorder rather than major depressive disorder is the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — extends the inpatient-hospitalization/suicide-risk-assessment framework into pediatric depression's age-adjusted criteria and TADS-trial evidence base","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"2 years is the adult duration ","B":"1 month is far shorter than th"}},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":1,"stem":"The Treatment for Adolescents with Depression Study (TADS) randomized adolescents to fluoxetine alone, CBT alone, combination fluoxetine plus CBT, or placebo. At 12 weeks, which treatment arm showed the highest rate of improvement, and what additional benefit did adding CBT provide regarding suicidality?","choices":{"A":"Fluoxetine monotherapy showed the highest 12-week improvement rate, with CBT adding no suicidality benefit","B":"CBT monotherapy showed the highest 12-week improvement rate, with fluoxetine adding no benefit","C":"Placebo showed the highest 12-week improvement rate, outperforming the active treatments","D":"The four treatment arms showed statistically equivalent 12-week improvement rates","E":"Combination fluoxetine plus CBT showed the highest 12-week improvement rate, with CBT reducing treatment-emergent suicidal ideation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In TADS, at 12 weeks the combination fluoxetine-plus-CBT group had significantly superior response rates (71 percent much/very much improved) compared with fluoxetine alone (60.6 percent), CBT alone (43.2 percent), or placebo (34.4 percent), and the addition of CBT to fluoxetine specifically decreased persistent suicidal ideation and potential treatment-emergent suicidal ideation — exactly this combination-superiority and suicidality-benefit finding. Fluoxetine alone was not the top-performing arm at 12 weeks; the combination arm outperformed it, and CBT did provide a suicidality benefit. CBT alone was also not the top-performing arm at 12 weeks, and fluoxetine's addition was part of the optimal combination strategy, not without benefit. Placebo had the lowest improvement rate of the four arms, not the highest. The four arms were not equivalent at 12 weeks; the combination arm was significantly superior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TADS established combination fluoxetine-plus-CBT as the 12-week optimal strategy for adolescent depression, with CBT specifically mitigating treatment-emergent suicidal ideation risk from the antidepressant. COMLEX: By 9 months, response rates across all three active treatment arms converged, informing long-term treatment planning discussions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — extends the inpatient-hospitalization/suicide-risk-assessment framework into pediatric depression's age-adjusted criteria and TADS-trial evidence base","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fluoxetine alone was not the t","B":"CBT alone was also not the top"}},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A child has severe, developmentally inappropriate temper outbursts occurring three or more times per week, along with a persistently irritable and angry mood observable by others between outbursts. Which pattern of outburst frequency and inter-outburst mood best fits DSM-5 criteria for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder?","choices":{"A":"Outbursts ≥3 times per week, with chronic, nonepisodic irritable/angry mood between outbursts","B":"Outbursts ≥3 times per week, with a fully euthymic, calm mood between outbursts","C":"Outbursts occurring about once monthly, with chronic irritable mood between outbursts","D":"Outbursts occurring during discrete manic or hypomanic episodes, with euthymic mood otherwise","E":"Outbursts ≥3 times per week, with mood between outbursts left unspecified"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for disruptive mood dysregulation disorder require temper outbursts occurring three or more times per week, along with a persistently irritable or angry mood between outbursts that is observable by others — exactly this child's presentation. A fully euthymic mood between outbursts would not meet criteria; the chronic irritable/angry mood between outbursts is a required, defining feature. Outbursts occurring only once monthly would not meet the required ≥3 times per week frequency threshold. Outbursts occurring only during discrete manic episodes would suggest bipolar disorder instead, since DMDD is specifically defined by chronic, nonepisodic irritability rather than episodic mood elevation. An inter-outburst mood requirement does exist; the chronic irritable/angry mood between outbursts is a required diagnostic feature, not an optional one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DMDD requires both the outburst frequency (≥3/week) AND the chronic, nonepisodic irritable/angry mood between outbursts — a two-part criterion that distinguishes it from episodic mood disorders like bipolar disorder. COMLEX: This nonepisodic irritability pattern is the key feature separating DMDD from the discrete mood episodes of bipolar disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — extends the mania/hypomania/bipolar-vs-unipolar differentiation framework into DMDD's role in distinguishing chronic nonepisodic pediatric irritability from bipolar disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Outbursts occurring only durin","E":"An inter-outburst mood require"}},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A child meets the outburst-frequency and duration criteria for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder, but her severe temper outbursts and chronic irritability are observed only at home and nowhere else. According to DSM-5's pervasiveness requirement, is this presentation sufficient for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Yes, a single setting such as the home is sufficient regardless of which specific setting it is","B":"No, DSM-5 requires the symptoms to be present in at least 2 settings, such as home and school","C":"No, DSM-5 requires symptoms to be present in all 3 major settings simultaneously","D":"Yes, provided that single setting is specifically the school setting","E":"No, DSM-5 requires the symptoms to be present at school specifically"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DSM-5 requires that disruptive mood dysregulation disorder symptoms be present in at least 2 settings (e.g., home and school), so a presentation confined to a single setting, such as home alone, would not meet this pervasiveness requirement — exactly the scenario this question describes. A single setting regardless of which one is not sufficient; at least 2 settings are required. All 3 major settings simultaneously is a stricter requirement than DSM-5 actually specifies; 2 settings, not 3, is the threshold. A single setting being sufficient only if it is school specifically is incorrect; the requirement is for at least 2 settings, not any particular single one. School is not a specifically required setting; any 2 settings, such as home and with peers, satisfy the criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DMDD's pervasiveness requirement (symptoms in ≥2 settings, such as home and school) ensures the diagnosis reflects a pervasive mood-dysregulation pattern rather than a situation-specific behavioral problem. COMLEX: This pervasiveness criterion parallels ADHD's cross-setting requirement, both designed to exclude purely situational presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — extends the mania/hypomania/bipolar-vs-unipolar differentiation framework into DMDD's role in distinguishing chronic nonepisodic pediatric irritability from bipolar disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A single setting being suffici","E":"School is not a specifically r"}},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is evaluating an 11-year-old boy for possible Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder, noting his chronic irritability and outbursts began at age 8. Which age range and onset requirement must be satisfied for this DSM-5 diagnosis to apply?","choices":{"A":"Onset before age 18, with the diagnosis applied between ages 10 and 25","B":"Onset before age 5, with the diagnosis applied between ages 2 and 10","C":"Onset before age 12, with the diagnosis applied between ages 4 and 17","D":"Onset after age 10, with the diagnosis applied between ages 10 and 18","E":"Onset before age 10, with the diagnosis applied between ages 6 and 18"},"correct":"E","explanation":"DSM-5 requires that disruptive mood dysregulation disorder symptoms have an onset before age 10, and the diagnosis is only applied to individuals between ages 6 and 18 — exactly matching this boy's age-8 symptom onset and current age of 11. Onset before age 18 with a 10-to-25 diagnostic range is incorrect; the actual onset requirement is before age 10, and the diagnostic range is 6 to 18. Onset before age 5 with a 2-to-10 diagnostic range is stricter and younger than DSM-5's actual thresholds. Onset before age 12 with a 4-to-17 range borrows ADHD's age-12 onset cutoff and misstates both DMDD boundaries. Onset specifically after age 10 is the opposite of the actual requirement, which requires onset before age 10.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DMDD's onset-before-age-10 requirement, combined with the 6-to-18 diagnostic age range, are both specific, frequently tested DSM-5 age boundaries for this diagnosis. COMLEX: These boundaries exist because DMDD was introduced specifically to reduce overdiagnosis of pediatric bipolar disorder in chronically irritable youth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — extends the mania/hypomania/bipolar-vs-unipolar differentiation framework into DMDD's role in distinguishing chronic nonepisodic pediatric irritability from bipolar disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Onset specifically after age 1"}},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy frequently argues with adults, loses his temper, and is easily annoyed and resentful, but does not resort to physical aggression, significant property destruction, or violating the basic rights of others. Which diagnosis best fits this symptom severity pattern?","choices":{"A":"Oppositional defiant disorder","B":"Conduct disorder","C":"Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder","D":"Intermittent explosive disorder","E":"Antisocial personality disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oppositional defiant disorder describes an enduring pattern of negativistic, hostile, and defiant behavior toward authority figures, including arguing, losing temper, and being easily annoyed, specifically in the absence of significant violations of the rights of others or property destruction — exactly this boy's presentation. Conduct disorder instead involves severe, repeated acts of aggression causing physical harm and frequent violation of others' rights, which this boy does not display. Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder requires severe temper outbursts ≥3 times weekly plus chronic irritability between outbursts and cannot co-occur with an ODD diagnosis by DSM-5 exclusion rules; this vignette describes ODD's characteristic argumentative/defiant pattern, not DMDD's outburst-frequency-plus-chronic-irritability profile. Intermittent explosive disorder involves discrete episodes of aggressive outbursts, not this enduring hostile/defiant temperament pattern. Antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before age 18 and requires a pattern of violating others' rights, neither of which fits this 9-year-old's presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ODD's defining feature is hostile, defiant behavior toward authority WITHOUT significant rights violations or destructiveness — the key distinguishing feature from conduct disorder. COMLEX: ODD and DMDD cannot be diagnosed simultaneously per DSM-5 exclusion criteria, even though both involve irritability and temper outbursts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the Cluster B/antisocial-personality-disorder diagnostic framework into conduct disorder as its childhood-onset developmental precursor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":1,"stem":"A 15-year-old with a diagnosis of conduct disorder is found to have had at least one characteristic symptom, including cruelty to animals, emerge repeatedly before age 10. Which conduct disorder age-of-onset subtype does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Adolescent-onset type","B":"Childhood-onset type","C":"Unspecified-onset type","D":"Late-onset type","E":"Preschool-onset type"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DSM-5 defines the childhood-onset subtype of conduct disorder as when at least one characteristic symptom has emerged repeatedly before age 10 years — exactly this adolescent's documented history. Adolescent-onset type requires that no characteristic persistent symptoms occur until after age 10, the opposite of this case's before-age-10 symptom emergence. Unspecified-onset type applies when the age of onset is unknown, not when it is specifically documented as before age 10, as in this case. Late-onset type is not a DSM-5-recognized subtype of conduct disorder; the three subtypes are childhood-onset, adolescent-onset, and unspecified-onset. Preschool-onset type is not a DSM-5 subtype either; onset before age 10, including in the preschool years, is classified as childhood-onset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conduct disorder's childhood-onset subtype (≥1 symptom before age 10) carries a worse prognosis and is more strongly associated with the \"with limited prosocial emotions\" specifier than adolescent-onset type. COMLEX: Distinguishing onset subtype is clinically relevant since childhood-onset conduct disorder more often persists into adult antisocial personality disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the Cluster B/antisocial-personality-disorder diagnostic framework into conduct disorder as its childhood-onset developmental precursor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adolescent-onset type requires","D":"Late-onset type is not a DSM-5","E":"Preschool-onset type is not a "}},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician evaluating a child for oppositional defiant disorder identifies symptoms from three DSM-5 symptom types: angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, and vindictiveness. How many total symptoms across these types, over what minimum duration, are required to meet diagnostic criteria?","choices":{"A":"At least 4 symptoms across the three types, present for at least 12 months","B":"At least 6 symptoms across the three types, present for at least 6 months","C":"At least 4 symptoms across the three types, present for at least 6 months","D":"At least 6 symptoms across the three types, present for at least 12 months","E":"At least 1 symptom drawn from each of the three types, present for at least 3 months"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 requires a 6-month pattern of at least 4 symptoms drawn from the three ODD symptom types (angry/irritable mood, argumentative/defiant behavior, vindictiveness) — exactly the threshold and duration this question asks about. Twelve months is the duration used for disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, not ODD. Six symptoms is higher than the ODD minimum of four. The four symptoms can come from any of the three types — DSM-5 does not require one from each — and the duration is 6 months, not 3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ODD requires ≥4 symptoms across its three symptom domains (angry/irritable, argumentative/defiant, vindictive) for ≥6 months — a specific symptom-count-plus-duration threshold distinct from DMDD's outburst-frequency criteria. COMLEX: The vindictiveness domain specifically requires spiteful or vindictive behavior at least twice within 6 months to count toward the total.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — extends the Cluster B/antisocial-personality-disorder diagnostic framework into conduct disorder as its childhood-onset developmental precursor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Twelve months is the duration ","B":"Six symptoms is higher than th","E":"The four symptoms can come fro"}},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy, adopted at age 4 after living most of his life in an orphanage with a rotating shift of caregivers, is brought for evaluation because of hyperactivity and inappropriate social behavior at school. He talks to anyone, follows strangers willingly, shows little empathy when others are hurt, and sits on the laps of teachers and students without asking. He has an extremely high pain tolerance and is frequently injured due to reckless behavior. Which diagnosis best fits, and what comorbidity pattern is commonly seen with it?","choices":{"A":"Disinhibited social engagement disorder, commonly comorbid with ADHD, PTSD, and language disorder or delay","B":"Reactive attachment disorder, more commonly comorbid with depressive and anxiety symptoms than with autism","C":"Autism spectrum disorder, more commonly comorbid with intellectual disability than with PTSD","D":"Conduct disorder, more commonly comorbid with substance use disorder than with language delay","E":"Social anxiety disorder, more commonly comorbid with selective mutism than with ADHD"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation, overly friendly indiscriminate social behavior with strangers, reduced empathy, high pain tolerance, and reckless behavior following a history of institutional care with rotating caregivers, describes disinhibited social engagement disorder; children with this disorder often show comorbid ADHD, PTSD, and language disorder or delay, and may have complex neuropsychiatric problems. Reactive attachment disorder presents with emotional withdrawal, not this overly friendly, indiscriminate pattern, and is not specifically comorbid with autism spectrum disorder. Autism spectrum disorder is not indicated by this history of pathogenic institutional caregiving as the presumed cause, and while intellectual disability can co-occur with autism spectrum disorder, that is not the diagnosis fitting this vignette. Conduct disorder involves violating others' rights and destructive behavior, not this specific pattern of indiscriminate social familiarity, and substance use disorder comorbidity is not the pattern described here. Social anxiety disorder involves fear and avoidance of social situations, the opposite of this overly familiar presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Disinhibited social engagement disorder commonly co-occurs with ADHD, PTSD, and language disorder/delay, reflecting the broader neuropsychiatric impact of early institutional deprivation. COMLEX: Post-institutionalized children with DSED often show improvement in physical growth after adoption but persistent difficulty forming selective attachments to new caregivers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — extends the primary-versus-secondary trauma/stressor-disorder differentiation framework into distinguishing disinhibited social engagement disorder from ADHD and autism spectrum disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Social anxiety disorder involv"}},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":2,"stem":"A malnourished infant with documented severe neglect and grossly negligent caregiving is diagnosed with reactive attachment disorder and admitted for failure to thrive. Beyond addressing immediate safety and nutritional needs, which intervention approach is described as the essential next step in this infant's treatment?","choices":{"A":"Antidepressant pharmacotherapy","B":"Permanent placement in foster care","C":"Individual play therapy with the infant","D":"Therapy and education with the caregivers","E":"Sensory integration therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After addressing safety and nutritional status, the treatment team must begin extensive and intensive therapy and education with the mother or both parents when possible, working to improve the unsatisfactory caregiver-child relationship — exactly the described essential next step, as demonstrated by programs like the Attachment and Biobehavioral Catch-up (ABC) intervention that improved secure attachment rates. Antidepressant pharmacotherapy is not the described first intervention for an infant with reactive attachment disorder; caregiver-focused relational intervention is emphasized instead. Permanent foster placement with no reunification attempt is not the described universal approach; the strategy depends on the child's safety and the caregiving situation, and improving the caregiver relationship is specifically emphasized when possible. Individual play therapy with an infant is developmentally inappropriate; the intervention works through the caregiver. Sensory integration therapy targets sensory processing, not the disrupted caregiver-child relationship that defines reactive attachment disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reactive attachment disorder treatment prioritizes improving the caregiver-child relationship through intensive caregiver-focused therapy and education, as validated by programs like the Attachment and Biobehavioral Catch-up (ABC) intervention. COMLEX: Suspected ongoing maltreatment must be reported to child protective services and law enforcement as part of the initial safety assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — extends the first-line-pharmacotherapy-versus-psychotherapy selection framework into reactive attachment disorder's caregiver-relationship-focused, non-pharmacologic first-line treatment approach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, underlying caregiving etiologies, and evidence-based management of pediatric trauma- and stressor-related disorders by evaluating social attachment behaviors (e.g., emotional withdrawal vs. indiscriminate social familiarity with adult strangers), trauma exposure parameters, developmental age cutoffs (minimum 9 months for RAD/DSED), and selecting appropriate first-line psychosocial interventions (e.g., Trauma-Focused CBT, caregiver dyadic therapy).","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician is distinguishing reactive attachment disorder from autism spectrum disorder in a malnourished, socially withdrawn young child with a history of severe neglect. Which finding regarding nutritional/growth status and intellectual functioning would favor reactive attachment disorder over autism spectrum disorder?","choices":{"A":"Normal nutritional status, with severe intellectual disability when it is present","B":"Significant malnourishment, with mild intellectual disability when it is present","C":"Significant malnourishment, with severe intellectual disability when it is present","D":"Normal nutritional status, with intellectual functioning uniformly preserved","E":"Normal nutritional status, with intellectual functioning varying unpredictably"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Children with autism spectrum disorder are typically well nourished and of age-appropriate size and weight despite their social impairments, and often have significant intellectual disability, whereas children with reactive attachment disorder are frequently malnourished and, when intellectual disability occurs, it is generally relatively mild — exactly the differentiating pattern described. Normal nutritional status with severe intellectual disability describes the autism spectrum disorder pattern, not reactive attachment disorder, making this option's pairing with reactive attachment disorder incorrect. Malnourishment with severe intellectual disability is not the pattern that specifically favors reactive attachment disorder over autism spectrum disorder; reactive attachment disorder's intellectual disability, when present, is typically mild, not severe. Nutritional status is a relevant, described differentiating feature; it is not irrelevant to this distinction. Both features are described as relevant differentiating findings between these two diagnoses, not irrelevant to the distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Autism spectrum disorder classically presents with normal nutritional status despite social impairment, while reactive attachment disorder classically presents with malnourishment/failure to thrive — a useful bedside differentiator. COMLEX: Both disorders can present with impaired reciprocal social interaction, making growth/nutritional status and severity of any intellectual disability important discriminating features.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — extends the primary-versus-secondary/organic differentiation framework into distinguishing reactive attachment disorder's caregiving-based etiology from autism spectrum disorder's neurodevelopmental etiology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Malnourishment with severe int"}},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl newly started on fluoxetine monotherapy for major depressive disorder returns 3 weeks later. Her parents report she seems more energized but also mention new, fleeting thoughts about death that were not present before starting the medication. Which FDA safety consideration and management approach best fits this scenario?","choices":{"A":"The FDA black box warning for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality applies, warranting closer monitoring given her new symptoms","B":"No specific antidepressant-related suicidality warning exists for this age group, despite documented FDA labeling for pediatric use","C":"This finding indicates fluoxetine is now entirely contraindicated in all adolescents going forward, regardless of monitoring or dose adjustment","D":"This finding requires immediate discontinuation of all psychiatric treatment, with no medication or therapy substitution considered","E":"This finding is an expected medication effect, requiring no specific monitoring or intervention beyond routine follow-up"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The FDA has placed a black box warning on antidepressants for treatment-emergent suicidality risk, particularly early in treatment, requiring close monitoring; TADS additionally demonstrated that adding CBT to fluoxetine specifically decreased persistent and treatment-emergent suicidal ideation compared with fluoxetine alone — exactly the appropriate consideration and management approach for this scenario. A specific FDA warning does exist for this age group; the black box warning specifically addresses pediatric and young adult antidepressant-related suicidality risk. Fluoxetine is not categorically contraindicated in all adolescents; it remains first-line therapy with appropriate monitoring, not an absolute contraindication. Immediate discontinuation of all psychiatric treatment is not the appropriate response; close monitoring and consideration of augmenting with CBT, not treatment cessation, is the described approach. This finding is not something requiring no monitoring; the black box warning specifically mandates vigilant monitoring for emergent suicidality, especially early in antidepressant treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The FDA black box warning on antidepressants for treatment-emergent suicidality in children/adolescents mandates close monitoring, especially in the early weeks of treatment — a frequently tested pediatric psychopharmacology safety point. COMLEX: TADS demonstrated that combining CBT with fluoxetine, rather than discontinuing the antidepressant, specifically reduced this treatment-emergent suicidality risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — extends the antidepressant safety-monitoring framework into the pediatric-specific FDA black box warning for treatment-emergent suicidality","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Fluoxetine is not categoricall","E":"This finding is not something "}},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":2,"stem":"Postmortem studies of adolescents who completed suicide reveal the most significant neurobiological alterations in which two brain regions, and what functional role do these regions share that is relevant to suicide risk?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellum and brainstem, both regions primarily involved in motor coordination and balance","B":"The prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, both involved in emotion regulation and problem-solving","C":"The primary visual cortex and occipital lobe, both primarily involved in visual processing and perception","D":"The basal ganglia and substantia nigra, both primarily involved in motor initiation and dopaminergic signaling","E":"The thalamus and medulla, both primarily involved in sensory relay and autonomic regulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Postmortem studies in adolescents who have completed suicide show the most significant alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, brain regions that are also associated with emotion regulation and problem-solving — exactly the regions and shared functional role this question asks about. The cerebellum and brainstem, involved in motor coordination, are not the regions specifically implicated in these postmortem suicide studies. The visual cortex and occipital lobe, involved in visual processing, are similarly not the implicated regions. The basal ganglia and substantia nigra, involved in motor initiation, are not the regions found to be most significantly altered. The thalamus and medulla, involved in sensory relay, are also not the specifically implicated regions in this postmortem literature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmortem studies of adolescent suicide completers show the most significant alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, regions governing emotion regulation and problem-solving — a specific, testable neurobiological finding. COMLEX: Reduced serotonin transporter receptor density in the prefrontal cortex is an additional biologic correlate identified in individuals with suicidal behavior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — extends the structural/functional neuroimaging-to-neurochemical-driver correlation framework into the specific prefrontal-cortex/hippocampal alterations associated with adolescent suicide risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Formulate an evidence-based diagnostic assessment and treatment plan for pediatric depressive disorders and suicidality by evaluating age-adjusted diagnostic criteria (e.g., irritable mood substitution, 1-year duration threshold for dysthymia), identifying key neurobiological markers of suicidal risk (such as alterations in the prefrontal cortex and hippocampus), and selecting first-line multimodal management strategies (combining Fluoxetine and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy [CBT] per TADS trial findings) while monitoring for antidepressant treatment-emergent suicidality.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has major depressive disorder. Her family prefers psychotherapy first. Her therapist proposes a 12- to 16-week treatment adapted from an adult therapy built around loss, interpersonal disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits, with an added adolescent focus on separation from parents, relationships with authority figures, peer pressure, and dyadic relationships. Which psychotherapy is being proposed?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy for depression","B":"Dialectical behavior therapy for adolescents","C":"Parent-child interaction therapy for depression","D":"Interpersonal psychotherapy for adolescents","E":"Short-term psychodynamic psychotherapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock describe interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) as focusing on loss, interpersonal disputes, role transition, and interpersonal deficits, and IPT for adolescents (IPT-A) as adding separation from parents, authority figures, peer pressures, and dyadic relationships; trials over 12 weeks in outpatients and 16 weeks in a school-based clinic showed fewer depressive symptoms and better social functioning. Cognitive-behavioral therapy is also evidence-based for adolescent depression, but it targets distorted thoughts and behaviors rather than these relationship domains. Dialectical behavior therapy, including its adolescent adaptation, targets self-harm and emotional dysregulation through skills groups and individual therapy. Parent-child interaction therapy adapted for depression, PCIT-ED, was piloted for preschool depression in children aged 3 to 7. Short-term psychodynamic psychotherapy explores unconscious conflict and is not organized around these four adolescent areas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fluoxetine and escitalopram are FDA-approved for adolescent major depression, and TADS found fluoxetine plus CBT most effective. COMLEX: for mild to moderate adolescent depression, CBT or IPT-A is a reasonable first step, with an SSRI and close suicide monitoring for more severe illness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TADS and SSRI treatment of adolescent depression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has had severe temper outbursts with screaming and hitting 4 times a week for the past 14 months, at home and at preschool, out of proportion to the triggers. Between outbursts his parents and teachers describe him as irritable and angry most of the day. He has never had a distinct period of elevated mood. His parents ask whether he has disruptive mood dysregulation disorder. Which response is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Yes, since outbursts 3 or more times weekly have lasted beyond 12 months","B":"Yes, since his irritability is present in at least two different settings","C":"No, since DMDD requires that symptom onset follow age 10","D":"No, since DMDD requires temper outbursts on at least 6 days a week","E":"No, since DMDD is not diagnosed in children younger than 6 years"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock state that DMDD requires outbursts about three or more times a week with persistent irritability between them, onset before age 10, duration of at least 12 months, and presence in at least two settings, and that it is not diagnosed in children younger than 6 years or older than 18. This boy meets the frequency, duration, setting, and mood criteria, but at age 5 he is too young for the diagnosis. Frequency and duration alone are insufficient because of the age rule. Two settings are required but do not override the age limit. Onset must occur before age 10, not after it. The frequency threshold is three or more outbursts a week, not six days a week.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DMDD = outbursts 3 or more times a week plus chronic irritability for 12 months or more in 2 or more settings, onset before 10, diagnosed only from age 6 to 18; it cannot coexist with ODD or intermittent explosive disorder, and bipolar disorder takes precedence. COMLEX: youth with DMDD are at higher risk for later unipolar depression and anxiety, not classic bipolar disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oppositional defiant disorder in the differential of childhood irritability","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Frequency and duration alone a","B":"Two settings are required but "}},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"A child meets full DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for disruptive mood dysregulation disorder. His clinician is also considering a comorbid diagnosis of oppositional defiant disorder given his frequent argumentative and defiant behavior. Per DSM-5 exclusion criteria, can both diagnoses be assigned simultaneously?","choices":{"A":"No, DSM-5 bars a concurrent oppositional defiant disorder diagnosis","B":"Yes, both may be assigned concurrently whenever criteria for each are met","C":"No, because oppositional defiant disorder is not valid in school-age children","D":"Yes, provided intermittent explosive disorder is also formally diagnosed","E":"Yes, provided the irritability is documented as episodic in character"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DSM-5 specifically states that disruptive mood dysregulation disorder cannot co-occur with oppositional defiant disorder, intermittent explosive disorder, or bipolar disorder — exactly the exclusion rule this question describes. Both diagnoses being routinely assigned together with no restriction is incorrect; DSM-5 explicitly excludes this co-occurrence. ODD is a legitimately diagnosable disorder in school-age children; the exclusion is specifically about DMDD-ODD co-occurrence, not about ODD being inapplicable to this age group generally. Diagnosing both DMDD and ODD together simply by also adding intermittent explosive disorder is not a valid workaround; DSM-5's exclusion applies regardless, and intermittent explosive disorder is itself excluded from co-occurring with DMDD as well. Episodic irritability would point away from DMDD altogether, toward a discrete mood episode, and it creates no exception that permits a concurrent ODD diagnosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's DMDD exclusion criteria specifically prohibit co-diagnosis with oppositional defiant disorder, intermittent explosive disorder, and bipolar disorder — when DMDD criteria are met, that diagnosis takes precedence over these overlapping disorders. COMLEX: This exclusion structure reflects DMDD's intended role as capturing a specific chronic-irritability phenotype rather than layering onto related diagnoses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — extends the psychotic-features-in-mood-episodes differentiation framework into DMDD's formal DSM-5 exclusion criteria versus overlapping disruptive/mood diagnoses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Both diagnoses being routinely","D":"Diagnosing both DMDD and ODD t"}},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Construct a diagnostic evaluation and clinical differentiation for Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder (DMDD) by evaluating characteristic outburst parameters (>3 times per week with chronic, non-episodic baseline irritability), setting pervasiveness (>2 settings), diagnostic age boundaries (onset before age 10; age range 6–18), and longitudinal risk for adult unipolar depression and anxiety disorders rather than bipolar disorder.","batch":2,"stem":"Longitudinal studies following youth diagnosed with disruptive mood dysregulation disorder into adulthood have examined which adult psychiatric outcome is most strongly associated with this childhood diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic adult bipolar disorder with discrete manic episodes","B":"Adult schizophrenia-spectrum and other primary psychotic disorders","C":"Adult unipolar depressive disorders and adult anxiety disorders","D":"Adult antisocial personality disorder with persistent rule-breaking","E":"No measurable increase in adult psychiatric morbidity of any kind"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Longitudinal data suggest that children with chronic irritability and severe mood dysregulation who would meet DMDD criteria do not typically develop classic bipolar disorder in late adolescence or early adulthood; instead they are at higher risk for future unipolar depressive disorders and anxiety disorders — exactly the outcome pattern this question asks about, which is precisely why DMDD was introduced as a distinct diagnosis rather than folding these children into a pediatric bipolar disorder diagnosis. Classic adult bipolar disorder is specifically described as NOT the typical longitudinal outcome for these youth, the opposite of what this option states. Adult schizophrenia-spectrum disorders are not the described longitudinal outcome for DMDD; unipolar depression and anxiety disorders are specifically described instead. Adult antisocial personality disorder is more classically associated with childhood conduct disorder's trajectory, not DMDD's described longitudinal outcome. An increased risk of adult psychiatric disorder is specifically described, not absent; unipolar depression and anxiety disorders are the specifically elevated-risk outcomes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DMDD's longitudinal outcome data (unipolar depression and anxiety disorders in adulthood, not bipolar disorder) is a key piece of evidence supporting DMDD as a distinct diagnostic category separate from pediatric bipolar disorder. COMLEX: This evidence base directly informed DSM-5's decision to introduce DMDD as a new diagnosis to reduce bipolar disorder overdiagnosis in chronically irritable youth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — extends the acute-mania/bipolar-depression/maintenance-treatment-priority framework into DMDD's distinct longitudinal trajectory toward unipolar depression and anxiety rather than bipolar disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Adult schizophrenia-spectrum d","E":"An increased risk of adult psy"}},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy has an extensive history since age 8 of cruelty to animals, bullying, deliberate destruction of property, and theft, causing considerable harm to others. He shows a persistent lack of remorse or guilt, callous lack of empathy, and shallow affect regarding his actions. Which conduct disorder severity level and specifier best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Mild conduct disorder, adolescent-onset type, with the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier","B":"Severe conduct disorder, childhood-onset type, with the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier","C":"Moderate conduct disorder, unspecified-onset type, without the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier","D":"Severe conduct disorder, adolescent-onset type, with the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier","E":"Oppositional defiant disorder, severe type, with the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, conduct problems causing considerable harm to others (severe severity level) beginning with symptoms before age 10 (childhood-onset type), combined with a persistent pattern of lack of remorse/guilt, callous lack of empathy, and shallow affect, meets the \"with limited prosocial emotions\" specifier, which DSM-5 notes is more likely to occur in individuals with childhood-onset type who also meet criteria for \"severe\" disorder — exactly this boy's described presentation. Mild severity involves few conduct problems beyond the minimum with only minor harm, not this considerable-harm presentation, and adolescent-onset type requires no persistent symptoms until after age 10, contradicted by this boy's documented age-8 symptom onset. Moderate severity falls between mild and severe, not matching this considerable-harm presentation, and unspecified-onset applies only when the age of onset is unknown, not when it is specifically documented as age 8. Severe severity and the limited-prosocial-emotions specifier fit, but adolescent-onset type is contradicted by his documented symptom onset at age 8. This is not oppositional defiant disorder; ODD specifically excludes significant violations of others' rights and destructive behavior, both of which this boy exhibits extensively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The \"with limited prosocial emotions\" specifier (lack of remorse/guilt, callous lack of empathy, unconcerned about performance, shallow/deficient affect) is more common in childhood-onset, severe conduct disorder and predicts a worse prognosis. COMLEX: This specifier profile overlaps conceptually with adult psychopathy/callous-unemotional traits research.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — extends the Cluster A/B defense-mechanism analysis framework into conduct disorder's \"limited prosocial emotions\" specifier as a developmental precursor to adult antisocial/psychopathic traits","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Severe severity and the limite"}},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician is evaluating a 4-year-old who occasionally argues with his parents and refuses requests, behavior his parents describe as typical for his age and developmental stage. How should this presentation be distinguished from oppositional defiant disorder?","choices":{"A":"By the presence of any argumentative or request-refusing behavior","B":"By whether the behavior occurs at school as well as at home, on a setting count","C":"By whether the child has a documented history of disrupted or disordered caregiving","D":"By treating any oppositional behavior before school age as diagnostically meaningless","E":"By comparing frequency and intensity against what is normative for same-mental-age peers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Oppositional behaviors are both healthy and adaptive within an expected range at specific developmental stages, so distinguishing normative negativism from oppositional defiant disorder requires comparing whether the behavior is considerably more frequent or intense than that seen in other children of the same mental age, not simply noting that oppositional behavior is present at all — exactly the distinguishing approach this question describes. The mere presence of any argumentative behavior, regardless of frequency or intensity, is not the correct distinguishing approach; developmentally normative oppositional behavior is expected at this age and does not by itself indicate ODD. Whether behavior occurs exclusively at school versus home is not the described distinguishing feature between normative negativism and ODD; ODD symptoms may in fact start only at home. Foster care placement history is not the described distinguishing feature for this normative-versus-ODD comparison; it is more relevant to reactive attachment disorder/disinhibited social engagement disorder's caregiving-based etiology instead. ODD can be distinguished from normative behavior at this age; the frequency/intensity-versus-developmental-expectation comparison is specifically the described method for doing so.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing developmentally normative oppositional behavior from ODD hinges on frequency/intensity relative to same-mental-age peers, not the mere presence of any defiance — a critical distinction to avoid overdiagnosing normal childhood behavior. COMLEX: This developmental-comparison principle parallels how other pediatric behavioral diagnoses (e.g., specific learning disorder, ADHD) require comparison against age-expected norms rather than absolute symptom presence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — extends the DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome + duration + impairment + exclusion) into ODD's developmental-norm-referenced distinction from typical childhood oppositional behavior","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"ODD can be distinguished from "}},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Design a diagnostic evaluation and management strategy for childhood disruptive behavior disorders (Oppositional Defiant Disorder [ODD] and Conduct Disorder [CD]) by evaluating symptom severity (hostility/defiance toward authority in ODD vs. active violation of basic rights/property destruction in CD), age of onset specifiers (childhood-onset","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old with a long-documented history of conduct disorder symptoms since childhood continues to display persistent aggression, rights violations, and deceitfulness. Under what specific circumstance can a diagnosis of conduct disorder still be applied to a person of this age, per DSM-5?","choices":{"A":"If the person is over age 18, the upper limit for the conduct disorder diagnosis","B":"If the person did not meet criteria for oppositional defiant disorder in childhood","C":"If the person does not meet the criteria for antisocial personality disorder","D":"If the symptoms first began after age 18, in adulthood","E":"If antisocial personality disorder has already been formally diagnosed as well"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 specifies that conduct disorder may be diagnosed in a person older than 18 years only if the person does not meet the criteria for antisocial personality disorder — exactly the specific circumstance this question asks about. Conduct disorder is not categorically barred from diagnosis after age 18; the specific exception (not meeting antisocial personality disorder criteria) allows it. A prior ODD diagnosis history is not the described determining factor for diagnosing conduct disorder after 18; the antisocial personality disorder exclusion is the specifically described criterion. Conduct disorder specifically requires symptom onset in childhood or adolescence per its diagnostic framework (with onset subtypes defined by before/after age 10), not onset after age 18, making that option internally inconsistent with the conduct disorder construct. Conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder are not always diagnosed together after 18; DSM-5's rule specifically excludes a conduct disorder diagnosis when antisocial personality disorder criteria are met, meaning typically only one or the other applies, not both automatically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conduct disorder can persist as a diagnosis past age 18 only when antisocial personality disorder criteria are not met — once ASPD criteria are satisfied, that diagnosis supersedes conduct disorder. COMLEX: This age-18 exclusion rule reflects conduct disorder's conceptual role as antisocial personality disorder's childhood/adolescent developmental precursor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — extends the antisocial-personality-disorder diagnostic-feature framework into the DSM-5 rule governing when conduct disorder, rather than antisocial personality disorder, still applies past age 18","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A prior ODD diagnosis history ","D":"Conduct disorder specifically ","E":"Conduct disorder and antisocia"}}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":1,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl becomes extremely distressed at school drop-off, worrying persistently that harm will come to her mother while they are apart, and has nightmares related to separation. Which pediatric anxiety disorder's symptom-provoking context best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Generalized anxiety disorder","B":"Social anxiety disorder","C":"Selective mutism","D":"Specific phobia","E":"Separation anxiety disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Separation anxiety disorder is developmentally inappropriate, excessive anxiety related to separation from the primary attachment figure, including pervasive worry that harm will come to a parent upon separation and separation-related nightmares — exactly this girl's presentation. Generalized anxiety disorder's trigger is pervasive worry across multiple domains (academics, performance, natural disasters), not specifically separation from an attachment figure. Social anxiety disorder's trigger is performance scrutiny or humiliation in social situations, not separation. Selective mutism involves failure to speak in specific settings, not this fear-of-harm-to-a-parent pattern. Specific phobia involves excessive fear of a specific object or situation, not separation from an attachment figure specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Separation anxiety disorder's defining trigger is fear of harm coming to an attachment figure upon separation — distinct from GAD's pervasive multi-domain worry and social anxiety disorder's performance-scrutiny trigger. COMLEX: Separation anxiety disorder requires ≥3 symptoms of excessive separation-related worry for at least 4 weeks per DSM-5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the adult anxiety-disorder trigger/timeline/impairment differentiation framework into pediatric-specific symptom-provoking contexts and physiologic correlates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":1,"stem":"Behaviorally inhibited young children, who display intense and persistent fear, shyness, and social withdrawal in unfamiliar settings, are at higher risk for later developing separation anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, and social anxiety disorder. Which characteristic physiologic trait pattern is described in these behaviorally inhibited children?","choices":{"A":"Higher-than-average resting heart rate, higher morning cortisol levels, and low heart rate variability","B":"Lower-than-average resting heart rate, with low morning cortisol levels","C":"Elevated evening cortisol, with normal resting heart rate and normal heart rate variability throughout the day","D":"High heart rate variability, with normal morning and evening cortisol levels","E":"Normal heart rate and cortisol, with elevated skin conductance on novelty"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behaviorally inhibited children, as a group, exhibit characteristic physiologic traits including higher-than-average resting heart rates, higher morning cortisol levels than average, and low heart rate variability — exactly the pattern this question asks about. Lower-than-average resting heart rate and low cortisol is the opposite of the actual described pattern. Elevated evening cortisol with normal heart rate does not match the described morning-cortisol-elevation and elevated-resting-heart-rate pattern. High heart rate variability with normal cortisol is also the opposite of the described low-heart-rate-variability pattern. Normal heart rate and cortisol with only elevated skin conductance omits the elevated resting heart rate, elevated morning cortisol, and low heart rate variability that are the described markers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Behaviorally inhibited children's physiologic signature (elevated resting heart rate, elevated morning cortisol, low heart rate variability) is a well-described biomarker pattern predicting later anxiety disorder risk. COMLEX: This autonomic/endocrine profile provides an objective correlate to complement clinical observation in identifying at-risk children.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the adult anxiety-disorder trigger/timeline/impairment differentiation framework into pediatric-specific symptom-provoking contexts and physiologic correlates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is comparing the minimum symptom duration required to establish a diagnosis of separation anxiety disorder versus generalized anxiety disorder in a child. Which duration pairing is correct per DSM-5?","choices":{"A":"At least 6 months for separation anxiety disorder, and 4 weeks for generalized anxiety disorder","B":"At least 1 year for separation anxiety disorder, and 1 year for generalized anxiety disorder as well","C":"At least 4 weeks for separation anxiety disorder, and 6 months for generalized anxiety disorder","D":"At least 1 month for separation anxiety disorder, and 1 month for generalized anxiety disorder too","E":"No minimum duration specified for separation anxiety disorder, nor for generalized anxiety disorder either"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DSM-5 requires at least 4 weeks of symptoms to establish a diagnosis of separation anxiety disorder, while generalized anxiety disorder requires a minimum duration of at least 6 months — exactly this pairing. The reversed pairing (6 months for separation anxiety disorder, 4 weeks for GAD) is incorrect; the actual thresholds are 4 weeks and 6 months respectively. A 1-year minimum for both disorders is longer than either actual threshold. A 1-month minimum for both disorders is close to separation anxiety disorder's threshold but does not match GAD's actual 6-month threshold. Minimum durations are specified for both disorders; they are not left unspecified.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Separation anxiety disorder's 4-week minimum duration is notably shorter than generalized anxiety disorder's 6-month minimum — a frequently tested pairing of pediatric anxiety-disorder duration thresholds. COMLEX: Social anxiety disorder's duration threshold is typically at least 6 months as well, paralleling GAD rather than separation anxiety disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — extends the adult anxiety-disorder trigger/timeline/impairment differentiation framework into pediatric-specific symptom-provoking contexts and physiologic correlates","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The reversed pairing (6 months"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":1,"stem":"The Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study (CAMS) randomized 488 children and adolescents with separation anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, or social anxiety disorder to CBT alone, sertraline alone, combined CBT plus sertraline, or placebo. Which treatment arm showed the highest 12-week response rate?","choices":{"A":"Sertraline monotherapy, with the single highest response rate observed among all four separate treatment arms tested","B":"Combined CBT plus sertraline, which produced the highest response rate of all four arms, at 80.7 percent","C":"CBT monotherapy, with the highest response rate among all four arms, exceeding even the combined treatment group","D":"Placebo, with the highest response rate among all four arms, exceeding every one of the active treatments","E":"All four treatment arms showed statistically equivalent response rates with no meaningful separation among them"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In CAMS, the combined CBT-plus-sertraline group had an 80.7 percent response rate of much or very much improved after the 12-week acute treatment phase, compared with 59.7 percent for CBT alone, 54.9 percent for sertraline alone, and 23.7 percent for placebo — exactly this combination-superiority finding. Sertraline alone, at 54.9 percent, was not the highest-response arm; the combination arm outperformed it. CBT alone, at 59.7 percent, was also not the highest-response arm. Placebo, at 23.7 percent, had the lowest response rate of the four arms, not the highest. The four arms were not statistically equivalent; the combination arm was significantly superior to each monotherapy arm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CAMS established combined CBT-plus-sertraline (80.7 percent response) as the most effective treatment for pediatric separation anxiety disorder, GAD, and social anxiety disorder, all three of which were superior to placebo individually as well. COMLEX: A trial of CBT alone may be applied first for children who can function sufficiently in daily activities, while combination treatment is recommended for severe impairment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — extends the first-line pharmacologic/psychotherapeutic/combined treatment-selection framework into pediatric anxiety's CAMS/RUPP-trial-based multimodal management","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sertraline alone, at 54.9 perc","C":"CBT alone, at 59.7 percent, wa","D":"Placebo, at 23.7 percent, had "}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is selecting pharmacotherapy for a 10-year-old with severe generalized anxiety disorder and wants to avoid any agent carrying a meaningful risk of heart-rhythm or conduction disturbance. Which medication class is specifically avoided in current pediatric practice for this reason?","choices":{"A":"SSRIs, which carry gastrointestinal upset and activation as their main adverse effects","B":"Buspirone, which carries dizziness and headache as its main adverse effects","C":"Beta-adrenergic receptor antagonists, which carry bradycardia and bronchospasm risk","D":"Diphenhydramine, which carries sedation and anticholinergic effects at usual doses","E":"Tricyclic antidepressants, which prolong cardiac conduction intervals at therapeutic doses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Current practice recommends against using tricyclic drugs in pediatric anxiety disorders due to their potentially serious cardiac adverse effects — exactly the concern this question describes. SSRIs are the first-choice medication class for pediatric anxiety disorders, not the class avoided for cardiac reasons. Buspirone has been used clinically in children with anxiety disorders, though without strong efficacy data, but it is not described as avoided for cardiotoxicity. Beta-adrenergic receptor antagonists like propranolol have been used clinically without efficacy data support, but they are not the class specifically avoided for cardiac adverse effects. Diphenhydramine may be used short-term for sleep disturbances in anxious children and is not the class avoided for cardiotoxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tricyclic antidepressants are specifically avoided in pediatric anxiety disorder treatment due to serious cardiac adverse effect risk, reinforcing SSRIs as the first-choice medication class. COMLEX: This cardiotoxicity concern with TCAs parallels their well-known cardiac risk profile in overdose across other pediatric and adult psychiatric indications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — extends the first-line pharmacologic/psychotherapeutic/combined treatment-selection framework into pediatric anxiety's CAMS/RUPP-trial-based multimodal management","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SSRIs are the first-choice med","B":"Buspirone has been used clinic","D":"Diphenhydramine may be used sh"}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":1,"stem":"A 14-year-old with severe school refusal related to separation anxiety disorder cannot tolerate a full school day despite ongoing treatment. Which management approach is described as appropriate when a full return to school is overwhelming for the child?","choices":{"A":"A progressive program helping the child gradually increase the time spent at school","B":"Immediate full-day school return with no graduated, incremental approach","C":"Permanent homeschooling with no attempt at school reintegration","D":"Stopping treatment until the child voluntarily returns to school on her own","E":"No specific school-related intervention approach is described for this scenario"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When a return to a full school day is overwhelming, the treatment team and school should help arrange a program so the child can progressively increase the time spent at school, since school refusal associated with separation anxiety disorder is considered a psychiatric emergency requiring firm encouragement of attendance alongside appropriate support — exactly this progressive approach. Immediate full-day return with no graduated approach is not the described method for an overwhelmed child; a progressive, incremental program is specifically described instead. Permanent homeschooling with no reintegration attempt contradicts the described emphasis on maintaining firm encouragement of eventual school attendance. Discontinuing treatment until voluntary return is not the described approach; active, structured intervention is emphasized rather than passive waiting. A specific school-related intervention approach is described, not absent; the progressive attendance-increase program is that specific approach.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Severe school refusal from separation anxiety disorder is treated as a psychiatric emergency, with graded/progressive school-attendance programs as the described management approach when full-day return is overwhelming. COMLEX: Family interventions are critical in this management, maintaining firm encouragement of attendance while providing appropriate support; some severe cases require hospitalization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — extends the first-line pharmacologic/psychotherapeutic/combined treatment-selection framework into pediatric anxiety's CAMS/RUPP-trial-based multimodal management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl speaks fluently and animatedly at home but has been persistently silent at school for the past 3 months, despite having the ability to speak appropriately in other settings. Excluding the first month of school, what is the minimum duration of this pattern required by DSM-5 to diagnose selective mutism?","choices":{"A":"1 week of persistent failure to speak in the expected setting","B":"6 months of persistent failure to speak in the expected setting","C":"1 year of persistent failure to speak in the expected setting","D":"1 month of persistent failure to speak in the expected setting","E":"3 months of persistent failure to speak in the expected setting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSM-5 requires a duration of at least 1 month, not counting the first month of school, for a diagnosis of selective mutism — exactly matching this girl's 3-month pattern of persistent lack of speech in a specific social situation despite having the ability to speak appropriately elsewhere. 1 week is far shorter than the actual 1-month threshold. 6 months is longer than the actual threshold; this is the more typical duration criterion for social anxiety disorder or generalized anxiety disorder, not selective mutism's minimum. 1 year is also considerably longer than the actual 1-month threshold. 3 months is how long this girl has been silent, not the diagnostic threshold; DSM-5 requires 1 month, and excludes transient muteness during the first month of school from counting toward it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective mutism's 1-month duration threshold specifically excludes the first month of school, since transient muteness on school entry is common and often resolves spontaneously. COMLEX: This exclusion distinguishes selective mutism from ordinary \"transient adaptation shyness\" seen in many children starting school.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder differentiation framework into distinguishing selective mutism (an anxiety disorder) from primary speech/language pathology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"6 months is longer than the ac","C":"1 year is also considerably lo","E":"3 months is how long this girl"}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"A child is being evaluated for possible selective mutism versus a primary communication disorder. Which finding is essential to confirm before diagnosing selective mutism rather than a communication disorder?","choices":{"A":"The child demonstrates fluent speech in certain settings, such as at home, despite silence elsewhere","B":"The child is unable to speak in any setting, suggesting a global expressive-language impairment","C":"The child has never met any developmental language milestones, pointing toward an underlying primary language disorder","D":"The child has a documented hearing impairment, a distinct condition that must first be ruled out separately","E":"No specific confirmatory finding is needed, since the diagnosis rests solely on caregiver report"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selective mutism is diagnosed only after it is clear that a child has adequate language skills and speaks fluently in some environments but not in others, distinguishing it from a communication disorder, in which the child has an inability, rather than a refusal, to speak — exactly the essential confirmatory finding this question asks about. A child unable to speak in any setting whatsoever would suggest a communication disorder or another condition causing global inability, not the situation-specific pattern that defines selective mutism. Never meeting developmental language milestones would point toward a primary communication disorder or intellectual disability, not selective mutism, which specifically requires demonstrated fluent language ability elsewhere. A documented hearing impairment is a differential consideration to rule out, not the confirmatory finding for selective mutism itself. A specific confirmatory finding is needed to make this distinction; demonstrated situational speaking ability is that finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective mutism requires confirming the child CAN speak appropriately in other settings — an ability/refusal distinction that separates it from communication disorders, in which the impairment is a true inability to speak. COMLEX: This same ability-versus-inability principle also distinguishes selective mutism from pervasive developmental disorders and intellectual disability.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder differentiation framework into distinguishing selective mutism (an anxiety disorder) from primary speech/language pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with selective mutism and comorbid social anxiety disorder begins treatment with a therapist who develops a hierarchy of easy, medium, and challenging speaking situations, paired with a system of small, medium, and large rewards for completing each speaking assignment. Which first-line treatment modality does this describe?","choices":{"A":"Psychoanalytic psychotherapy focused on interpreting unconscious conflict and defenses","B":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy using graded exposure paired with contingent reinforcement","C":"Pharmacotherapy with an SSRI titrated to effect and continued as monotherapy","D":"Family systems therapy addressing household roles, alliances, and communication","E":"Applied behavior analysis targeting discrete developmental skill acquisition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This approach, developing a graduated hierarchy of speaking situations from easy to most challenging paired with a tiered reward system, describes cognitive-behavioral therapy using graded exposure with reinforcement, the first-line treatment for selective mutism, sometimes combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated. Psychoanalytic psychotherapy focused on unconscious conflict is not the described approach; a structured, graded behavioral exposure hierarchy with rewards is specifically described instead. Pharmacotherapy with an SSRI as monotherapy without any behavioral component does not match this description, which is specifically behavioral/exposure-based; SSRIs may be added but are not described as the sole intervention here. Family systems therapy without a direct child-focused exposure component does not match this specifically child-directed, graded-exposure-with-rewards approach. Applied behavior analysis targeting unrelated developmental milestones does not match this speech-specific, anxiety-focused graded exposure hierarchy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Graded exposure with a reward-based reinforcement hierarchy is the first-line cognitive-behavioral approach for selective mutism, progressing from lower-anxiety to higher-anxiety speaking situations. COMLEX: SSRI pharmacotherapy may be added to CBT for selective mutism when indicated, paralleling the combined-treatment approach used across other pediatric anxiety disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder differentiation framework into distinguishing selective mutism (an anxiety disorder) from primary speech/language pathology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Family systems therapy without"}},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with adults with OCD, children and adolescents with OCD more often present with which distinguishing feature regarding their obsessional thoughts and repetitive behaviors?","choices":{"A":"More often have complete, adult-level insight that their obsessions are unreasonable","B":"Experience compulsions without any obsessions","C":"More often lack insight that these symptoms are unreasonable","D":"Always meet criteria for the \"absent insight\" specifier by definition","E":"Show no difference in insight compared to adults with OCD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The overall clinical presentation of OCD in youth is similar to that in adults; however, compared to adults, children and adolescents with OCD more often do not consider their obsessional thoughts or repetitive behaviors to be unreasonable — exactly this distinguishing feature. Children more often having complete, adult-level insight is the opposite of the actual described pattern; reduced insight relative to adults is what is described. Children with OCD do not lack obsessions entirely; in most cases of pediatric OCD, both obsessions and compulsions are present. Not every child with reduced insight automatically meets the \"absent insight\" DSM-5 specifier; insight exists on a spectrum from good to absent, and reduced insight relative to adults does not mean universally meeting the most severe specifier. A difference in insight compared to adults IS described; this is not an area of no difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pediatric OCD patients more often lack adult-level insight into the unreasonableness of their obsessions/compulsions compared to adults with OCD — a key developmental distinction in presentation. COMLEX: DSM-5's insight specifiers (good, fair, poor, absent) apply across the lifespan, with weaker insight correlating with a stronger belief in the OCD obsessions/compulsions themselves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — extends the CSTC neurocircuitry pathogenesis framework into pediatric OCD's autoimmune/PANDAS-related etiologic considerations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":1,"stem":"A subgroup of children with abrupt-onset OCD symptoms following documented group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal (GABHS) infection has been proposed to represent an autoimmune-mediated process, termed PANDAS. What did a prospective longitudinal study following youth with PANDAS over 2 years find regarding the relationship between GABHS infections and OCD symptom exacerbations?","choices":{"A":"A strong, consistent temporal association confirming GABHS as a definitive cause of exacerbations","B":"No evidence of a temporal association between GABHS infections and OCD symptom exacerbations","C":"GABHS infections were found to be protective against OCD symptom exacerbations","D":"No such study has been conducted, so no prospective data on this relationship exist","E":"GABHS infections caused permanent remission of OCD symptoms in the youth followed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A prospective longitudinal study of youth with PANDAS followed over 2 years found no evidence of a temporal association between GABHS infections and OCD symptom exacerbations in children who met PANDAS criteria — exactly this finding, which is part of why PANDAS-triggered OCD remains a minority and controversial presentation of pediatric OCD. A strong, consistent temporal association confirming GABHS as a definitive cause is the opposite of what this specific longitudinal study found. GABHS infections being protective against exacerbations is not the described finding; the study found no temporal association in either direction, not a protective effect. The prospective study was in fact conducted, and its findings are reported above. GABHS infections causing permanent remission is not the described finding at all; the study addressed exacerbations, not remission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Despite the PANDAS hypothesis, a prospective longitudinal study found no temporal association between GABHS infections and OCD symptom exacerbations — reinforcing that PANDAS-triggered OCD remains a controversial minority presentation. COMLEX: Even antigen/antibody evidence of recent GABHS exposure does not establish a causal relationship between GABHS and OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — extends the CSTC neurocircuitry pathogenesis framework into pediatric OCD's autoimmune/PANDAS-related etiologic considerations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":1,"stem":"The Pediatric OCD Treatment Study (POTS), a large-scale randomized, placebo-controlled trial, compared sertraline alone, CBT alone, and combination treatment for childhood-onset OCD. Which treatment approach demonstrated the highest rates of remission?","choices":{"A":"Sertraline monotherapy, the trial's single-medication arm","B":"CBT monotherapy, the trial's single-psychotherapy arm","C":"Placebo, the trial's inactive pill control arm","D":"Combined sertraline and CBT, the trial's combination arm","E":"All three active arms, with no separation between them"},"correct":"D","explanation":"POTS demonstrated that the highest rates of remission in pediatric OCD are achieved with a combination of both a serotonergic agent (sertraline) and CBT together, though each treatment alone also provided encouraging levels of response — exactly this combination-superiority finding, paralleling the pattern also seen with CAMS for pediatric anxiety disorders. Sertraline alone was not superior to the combination approach; the combination outperformed sertraline monotherapy. CBT alone was also not superior to the combination approach. Placebo was not superior to any active treatment approach; POTS demonstrated efficacy of the active treatments, with combination therapy the most effective. The three approaches did not produce identical remission rates; combination treatment specifically demonstrated the highest remission rates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: POTS established combination sertraline-plus-CBT as achieving the highest pediatric OCD remission rates, mirroring CAMS's combination-superiority finding for pediatric anxiety disorders. COMLEX: Treatment guidelines recommend a trial of CBT first for mild-to-moderate pediatric OCD, reserving combination treatment for more severe presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 5 — extends the CSTC neurocircuitry pathogenesis framework into pediatric OCD's autoimmune/PANDAS-related etiologic considerations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CBT alone was also not superio","C":"Placebo was not superior to an","E":"The three approaches did not p"}},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":1,"stem":"An adolescent at a nightclub ingests a club drug and subsequently develops dry mouth, tachycardia, hyperthermia, muscle spasm, and mild hallucinogenic perceptual changes. This drug is a derivative of methamphetamine that inhibits both serotonin and dopamine reuptake. Which club drug best fits this description?","choices":{"A":"Gamma-hydroxybutyrate","B":"Flunitrazepam","C":"MDMA","D":"Phencyclidine","E":"Marijuana"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MDMA is a derivative of methamphetamine with both stimulant and hallucinogenic properties, inhibiting serotonin and dopamine reuptake, and can result in dry mouth, increased heart rate, fatigue, muscle spasm, and hyperthermia — exactly this presentation. Gamma-hydroxybutyrate (GHB) is primarily a CNS depressant, not a stimulant/hallucinogenic agent producing this sympathomimetic toxidrome. Flunitrazepam (Rohypnol) is a benzodiazepine and primarily a depressant, the opposite mechanism from this presentation. Phencyclidine is a dissociative NMDA receptor antagonist, not a serotonin/dopamine reuptake inhibitor with this stimulant profile. Marijuana's active ingredient THC produces impairment in memory/learning, distorted perception, and increased heart rate, but does not produce this specific sympathomimetic, serotonin/dopamine-reuptake-inhibition toxidrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MDMA's combined stimulant/hallucinogenic profile (via serotonin and dopamine reuptake inhibition) produces a sympathomimetic toxidrome including hyperthermia and tachycardia, distinguishing it mechanistically from the depressant club drugs GHB, flunitrazepam, and ketamine. COMLEX: MDMA-associated hyperthermia can progress to serotonin syndrome and severe hyponatremia, both potentially life-threatening complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 6 — extends the MDMA acute-physiologic-complication mechanism framework into the broader adolescent substance-use toxidrome, risk-stratification, and treatment-planning process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":1,"stem":"Gamma-hydroxybutyrate (GHB) and flunitrazepam (Rohypnol) share which characteristic that has made them associated with drug-facilitated sexual assault, leading to federal legislation addressing their misuse?","choices":{"A":"Both are strong stimulants that cause visible agitation alerting bystanders to something being wrong","B":"Both require injection, eliminating any risk of undetected drink tampering","C":"Both have a strong, easily detectable bitter taste that would alert most victims immediately","D":"Both are colorless, tasteless, and odorless CNS depressants that can be added to drinks undetected","E":"Neither drug has ever been associated with any legislative response, despite decades of documented misuse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GHB and flunitrazepam (a benzodiazepine) are primarily depressants that can be added to drinks without detection because they are often colorless, tasteless, and odorless, and Congress passed the Drug-Induced Rape Prevention and Punishment Act after finding that these drugs were used in date rapes — exactly this described characteristic and legislative response. Both being strong stimulants causing visible agitation is the opposite of their actual depressant profile. Both requiring injection and being unable to be added to beverages contradicts the specifically described drink-tampering risk. Both having a strong, easily detectable bitter taste is the opposite of their described colorless, tasteless, odorless profile, which is precisely what makes them dangerous when added to drinks. Legislative response did occur; the Drug-Induced Rape Prevention and Punishment Act specifically addressed this concern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GHB and flunitrazepam's colorless, tasteless, odorless depressant properties specifically enable covert drink-tampering, the basis for federal drug-facilitated-sexual-assault legislation. COMLEX: Recognizing these club drugs' depressant toxidrome (versus MDMA's stimulant/hallucinogenic profile) guides appropriate clinical suspicion and management in suspected drug-facilitated assault cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 6 — extends the MDMA acute-physiologic-complication mechanism framework into the broader adolescent substance-use toxidrome, risk-stratification, and treatment-planning process","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Legislative response did occur"}},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":1,"stem":"A school-based program checks every student seen in the health center for any reason, flags those whose responses suggest substance use, delivers a short motivational-interviewing conversation on the spot, and connects students who need more than that with a specialist for ongoing care. Which evidence-based protocol does this describe?","choices":{"A":"CALOCUS","B":"POTS","C":"CAMS","D":"TADS","E":"SBIRT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Per SAMHSA's goals, a school-based Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment (SBIRT) protocol screens all students seen in a clinic for any reason, offers brief motivational-interviewing-based intervention to those identified with substance use, and refers for more intensive treatment when needed — exactly this description, as demonstrated in a study of 629 adolescents across 13 New Mexico high schools. CALOCUS (Child and Adolescent Level of Care Utilization System) is a levels-of-care assignment instrument used once a substance use disorder has already been identified, not the initial screening/brief-intervention protocol described here. POTS (Pediatric OCD Treatment Study) is a pediatric OCD treatment trial, unrelated to substance use screening. CAMS (Child/Adolescent Anxiety Multimodal Study) is a pediatric anxiety disorder treatment trial, also unrelated to substance use screening. TADS (Treatment for Adolescents with Depression Study) is an adolescent depression treatment trial, likewise unrelated to this substance-use screening/intervention protocol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SBIRT is a school-based, motivational-interviewing-driven screening and brief-intervention protocol for adolescent substance use, distinct from CALOCUS, which assigns level of care after a diagnosis is established. COMLEX: SBIRT studies show decreased self-reported substance use at follow-up regardless of the severity of use at initial screening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 -> Obj 6 — extends the MDMA acute-physiologic-complication mechanism framework into the broader adolescent substance-use toxidrome, risk-stratification, and treatment-planning process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy refuses to sleep away from home or near anyone other than his mother, has stomachaches and vomiting when anticipating separation from her, and has good peer relationships as long as no separation is involved. His differential diagnosis list includes generalized anxiety disorder, panic disorder with agoraphobia, PTSD, and oppositional defiant disorder. Which finding most specifically supports separation anxiety disorder over this differential?","choices":{"A":"The presence of gastrointestinal symptoms during periods of distress","B":"The preservation of good peer relationships in school and neighborhood settings","C":"The precipitating stressor is the loss of proximity to caregivers","D":"A vague, generalized worry spanning many separate life domains","E":"A history of a discrete, identifiable traumatic event preceding onset"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Separation anxiety disorder's precipitating stressor is specifically separation from home or attachment figures, with psychophysiological symptoms (stomachaches, vomiting, dizziness) anticipating separation, and typically good peer relationships when no separation is involved — exactly this boy's presentation, distinguishing it from GAD's pervasive multi-domain worry, panic disorder with agoraphobia's fear of being incapacitated by a panic attack, PTSD's trauma-exposure requirement, and ODD's defiance-based (not anxiety-based) pattern. GI symptoms alone are not specifically distinguishing since psychophysiological symptoms overlap across several of these anxiety disorders; it is the separation-specific trigger context that is distinguishing, not the mere presence of GI symptoms. Good peer relationships alone, without connecting them to the separation-specific trigger, is not the complete distinguishing feature; the combination of good peer relationships specifically when separation is not involved is what is diagnostically informative. A vague, generalized worry across multiple domains describes GAD's trigger pattern, not this boy's separation-specific presentation. A discrete, identifiable traumatic event would point toward PTSD, not separation anxiety disorder, and no such event is described in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Separation anxiety disorder's trigger specificity (separation from attachment figures, otherwise-intact peer functioning) is the key differentiator from GAD's pervasive worry and panic disorder with agoraphobia's fear-of-incapacitation trigger. COMLEX: Panic disorder with agoraphobia is uncommon before age 18, further supporting separation anxiety disorder over that differential in a 9-year-old.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — extends the adult panic/GAD/social anxiety/phobia differentiation-by-trigger framework into distinguishing pediatric separation anxiety disorder's attachment-figure-specific trigger from its closest differentials","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GI symptoms alone are not spec","B":"Good peer relationships alone,"}},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl reports persistent worry about her grades, athletic performance, and the possibility of natural disasters like earthquakes, interfering with her daily activities. She seeks excessive reassurance about her performance from teachers and parents, experiences muscle tension and difficulty falling asleep, and has autonomic symptoms of tachycardia and dizziness when anticipating a performance. Which anxiety disorder's characteristic multi-domain worry pattern best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Generalized anxiety disorder, whose worry is diffuse and free-floating","B":"Separation anxiety disorder, whose worry centers on attachment figures","C":"Selective mutism, whose core feature is situational failure to speak","D":"Specific phobia, whose fear attaches to one circumscribed trigger","E":"Panic disorder, whose hallmark is discrete unexpected panic attacks"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Generalized anxiety disorder in children involves significant distress across multiple life domains (academics, sports performance, natural disasters), excessive reassurance-seeking about performance, and at least one symptom such as muscle tension or sleep disturbance, plus autonomic hyperarousal symptoms like tachycardia and dizziness — exactly this girl's multi-domain presentation. Separation anxiety disorder's trigger is specifically separation from attachment figures, not this pervasive, non-attachment-specific multi-domain worry pattern. Selective mutism involves failure to speak in specific settings, which contradicts this girl's described verbal reassurance-seeking behavior. A specific phobia is limited to a single, circumscribed trigger, not this broad multi-domain worry pattern spanning academics, sports, and natural disasters. Panic disorder involves discrete, unexpected panic attacks, not this pattern of persistent, pervasive worry across many domains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Generalized anxiety disorder's hallmark is pervasive worry across MULTIPLE unrelated domains (academic, social, natural disasters) plus excessive reassurance-seeking, distinguishing it from the single-trigger-focused anxiety disorders. COMLEX: GAD is the most common anxiety disorder among youth and is more common in adolescents than in younger children.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — extends the anxiety-disorders-across-the-lifespan comparison framework into pediatric generalized anxiety disorder's multi-domain worry pattern and its distinguishing autonomic hyperarousal profile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the diagnostic criteria, situational triggers, and physiologic correlates of pediatric anxiety disorders by evaluating symptom-provoking contexts (e.g., fear of harm to attachment figures vs. performance scrutiny vs. pervasive outcome worry), developmental age thresholds, and associated autonomic/somatic manifestations (e.g., elevated morning cortisol and higher resting heart rates in behaviorally inhibited youth, GI distress, and cardiovascular symptoms).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy experiences intense discomfort specifically during public speaking assignments in class, including blushing, inadequate eye contact, a soft voice, and rigid posture, but functions normally in other academic and social settings not involving public performance. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Social anxiety disorder, generalized type","B":"Social anxiety disorder, performance type","C":"Separation anxiety disorder","D":"Specific phobia, situational type","E":"Generalized anxiety disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A child or adolescent with social anxiety disorder may exhibit the performance-only type, which targets a specific type of performing, such as fear of public speaking, typically manifesting in school or academic settings requiring public presentations — exactly this boy's presentation, with described physical manifestations (blushing, inadequate eye contact, soft voice, rigid posture) and otherwise normal functioning outside performance contexts. The generalized type of social anxiety disorder would involve consistent anxiety and distress in almost all social situations, not this performance-specific pattern with otherwise normal functioning. Separation anxiety disorder's trigger is separation from attachment figures, not public performance scrutiny. Specific phobia, situational type, covers fear of a situation itself, such as flying or enclosed spaces; when the fear is of being scrutinized or judged by others, as in public speaking, the diagnosis is social anxiety disorder. Generalized anxiety disorder involves pervasive worry across multiple unrelated domains, not this performance-circumscribed pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Social anxiety disorder's performance-only type is limited to specific performance tasks (public speaking, playing an instrument) with otherwise normal social functioning, distinct from the generalized type's pervasive social anxiety. COMLEX: Untreated social anxiety disorder is associated with significant long-term impact, including school dropout and reduced adult occupational productivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — extends the adult social anxiety disorder trigger-based differentiation framework into the pediatric performance-only subtype's specific-task, otherwise-intact-functioning pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with severe school refusal from separation anxiety disorder has been unable to attend school at all for the past 3 months despite outpatient CBT and sertraline, and his condition has now deteriorated to the point of requiring hospitalization. According to the described management framework for severe school refusal, which comprehensive approach best fits the ongoing treatment plan?","choices":{"A":"A plan resting on individual pharmacotherapy adjustment, with no school coordination","B":"A plan returning the child to a full school day at discharge, with no reintegration","C":"A plan excluding behavioral techniques, resting on ongoing medication management","D":"A comprehensive plan spanning child, parents, peers, and school, using graded contact","E":"A plan ending at hospitalization itself, with no structured aftercare arranged"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A comprehensive treatment plan for severe school refusal involves the child, the parents, and the child's peers and school, with graded contact with the object of anxiety as a behavior modification technique useful for any type of separation anxiety, and some severe cases specifically require hospitalization as part of this comprehensive approach — exactly matching this boy's described escalating presentation and management needs. A plan involving only individual pharmacotherapy adjustment with no school or family involvement contradicts the specifically described multi-party, comprehensive approach. Requiring immediate full-day return with no graded approach contradicts the specifically described progressive, graded-contact method. Excluding behavioral modification techniques contradicts the specific description of graded contact with the anxiety-provoking object/situation as a core behavioral intervention. Further intervention beyond hospitalization itself IS described; hospitalization is presented as one component of a broader comprehensive, multi-party plan, not the endpoint.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Severe school refusal management is comprehensive and multi-party (child, parents, peers, school), using graded contact/exposure as the core behavioral technique, with hospitalization reserved for the most severe cases as one component of this larger plan. COMLEX: This multi-party, graded-exposure framework parallels general anxiety-disorder treatment principles applied to a pediatric psychiatric emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — extends the stepped-treatment-approach framework (severity, functional impairment, comorbidity) into pediatric severe school refusal's comprehensive, multi-party, graded-exposure management plan","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":2,"stem":"The Research Units in Pediatric Psychopharmacology (RUPP) study, a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of 128 children and adolescents with separation anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, and social anxiety disorder, investigated fluvoxamine. What percentage of children treated with fluvoxamine showed significant improvement compared with placebo, and how quickly was response noticeable?","choices":{"A":"76 percent improved with fluvoxamine versus 29 percent with placebo, with response emerging after 6 months","B":"45 percent improved with fluvoxamine versus 29 percent with placebo, with response noticeable within 2 weeks","C":"45 percent improved with fluvoxamine versus 40 percent with placebo, with response emerging after 6 months","D":"76 percent improved with fluvoxamine versus 60 percent with placebo, with response emerging after 6 months","E":"76 percent improved with fluvoxamine versus 29 percent with placebo, with response noticeable within 2 weeks"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The RUPP study revealed that 76 percent of children treated with fluvoxamine showed significant improvement compared with 29 percent of those on placebo, with response to medication noticeable after only 2 weeks of treatment — exactly matching this question's figures. Response was noticeable within 2 weeks, not after 6 months. The placebo response was 29 percent, well below the 40 to 60 percent some options give, and 45 percent understates the fluvoxamine response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RUPP demonstrated fluvoxamine's rapid (2-week) and robust (76 percent vs. 29 percent placebo) efficacy for pediatric separation anxiety disorder, GAD, and social anxiety disorder, reinforcing SSRIs' first-line role. COMLEX: Youths who responded to fluvoxamine in RUPP largely remained responders at the 6-month continuation mark, supporting continued treatment duration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — extends the SSRI/SNRI/buspirone/benzodiazepine mechanism-and-safety comparison framework into fluvoxamine's specific pediatric anxiety-disorder efficacy data from the RUPP trial","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Response was noticeable within"}},{"id":"sdl36_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Design an evidence-based management plan for pediatric anxiety disorders by integrating first-line multimodal interventions (combining Cognitive Behavioral Therapy with SSRIs like sertraline or fluvoxamine per CAMS/RUPP trial findings), implementing progressive exposure protocols for psychiatric emergencies like severe school refusal, and recognizing contraindications to non-first-line agents (e.g., avoiding tricyclic antidepressants due to cardiotoxicity).","batch":2,"stem":"A child with moderate, non-emergent generalized anxiety disorder is able to function sufficiently in daily activities to engage in outpatient treatment. According to the described stepped-treatment framework, which initial treatment approach is described as reasonable to apply first, if available, before escalating to a combination approach?","choices":{"A":"CBT monotherapy with graded exposure","B":"Combined CBT and an SSRI from the start","C":"Tricyclic antidepressant monotherapy","D":"Scheduled benzodiazepine monotherapy","E":"Partial hospitalization program"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A trial of CBT may be applied first, if available, when a child can function sufficiently to engage in daily activities while obtaining this treatment, with combination treatment (CBT plus SSRI) applied specifically for a child with severe impairment — exactly this stepped approach for a child who can still function sufficiently. Immediate combination treatment regardless of severity does not match the described stepped approach, which reserves combination treatment specifically for severe impairment rather than applying it universally as a first step. Tricyclic antidepressant monotherapy carries cardiotoxicity risk in pediatric anxiety treatment and is not a described first step. Benzodiazepine monotherapy is not described as the initial step; open trials suggest some agents like alprazolam or clonazepam may help adjunctively in specific circumstances, but this is not the described initial stepped-treatment approach. Hospitalization or a partial hospitalization program is reserved for severe cases like severe school refusal, not the described first step for a child who can still function sufficiently in daily activities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stepped-treatment framework for pediatric anxiety reserves CBT-alone as a reasonable first step for functionally intact children, escalating to combination CBT-plus-SSRI specifically for severe impairment — a severity-matched treatment-selection principle. COMLEX: This stepped approach mirrors general psychiatric treatment-escalation principles applied specifically to pediatric anxiety disorder severity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — extends the stepped-treatment-approach framework (severity, functional impairment) into the specific CBT-first-versus-combination-treatment decision point for pediatric anxiety disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has not spoken at school or to anyone outside her immediate family for 2 years, despite being animated and fluent at home in both English and Mandarin, with normal developmental milestones and above-average intelligence documented. A home videotape confirms fluent, spontaneous speech with her sisters. In the clinical evaluation, she fails to make eye contact or respond verbally to the intake clinician. Which diagnosis and comorbid condition best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A primary communication disorder, comorbid with intellectual disability","B":"Autism spectrum disorder, comorbid with global developmental delay","C":"A simple language barrier from bilingual upbringing, with no psychiatric diagnosis","D":"Selective mutism, comorbid with generalized social anxiety disorder","E":"Conversion disorder, with mutism pervasive across all settings"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation, persistent lack of speech in specific social situations (school, unfamiliar people) for over a month despite documented fluent, spontaneous speech at home in both languages, normal developmental milestones, and above-average intelligence, along with extreme social anxiety and \"freezing\" in certain situations, is selective mutism comorbid with social anxiety disorder — exactly this girl's presentation, since selective mutism is believed to be related to social anxiety disorder. A primary communication disorder comorbid with intellectual disability is contradicted by her documented normal developmental milestones, above-average intelligence, and demonstrated fluent speech at home; her mutism reflects an inability-versus-refusal pattern inconsistent with a primary communication disorder. Autism spectrum disorder comorbid with global developmental delay is also contradicted by her normal milestones and demonstrated situational (not pervasive) social engagement difficulties. A language barrier from bilingual upbringing alone does not explain her mutism, since she is fluent in both English and Mandarin and her silence is not specific to one language but occurs broadly outside the family in social situations. This is not conversion disorder, in which mutism would be pervasive across settings, not selectively confined to specific social situations while remaining fluent at home.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Selective mutism's hallmark is demonstrated situational (not global) speech ability, here documented via videotape evidence of home fluency, distinguishing it from communication disorders and autism spectrum disorder. COMLEX: Social anxiety disorder is almost always present in children with selective mutism, and behavioral disturbances like temper tantrums may also occur at home due to this underlying social anxiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder secondary-organic-cause-exclusion framework into confirming selective mutism's anxiety-based, situation-specific etiology over a primary communication or developmental disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A family recently immigrated, and their 7-year-old child, newly enrolled in a school that uses a different language than the family speaks at home, is reticent to use the new language at school despite having gained communicative competence in it. Under what specific condition should selective mutism still be diagnosed in this scenario?","choices":{"A":"If the child also refuses to converse in their native language","B":"If the child is reticent with the newly learned second language","C":"If the child is bilingual or recently immigrated, regardless of native speech","D":"If standardized language-proficiency testing scores fall below the cutoff","E":"If the reticence began within the first week of school enrollment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Children introduced into an environment using a different language may be reticent to begin using the new language, and we should only diagnose selective mutism when children also refuse to converse in their native language and when they have gained communicative competence in the new language but refuse to speak it — exactly this specific diagnostic condition. Selective mutism should NOT always be diagnosed whenever reticence to use a new language occurs; this reticence alone, without native-language refusal, is a normal adjustment reaction, not selective mutism. Being bilingual or recently immigrated is not by itself grounds for the diagnosis, whatever the child's native-language speech; refusal to speak the native language is still required. Standardized language-proficiency testing alone is not the described diagnostic condition; the specific behavioral pattern of refusing the native language despite demonstrated competence is what matters. The timing of reticence onset relative to school enrollment week is not the described distinguishing condition here; the native-language-refusal requirement is the specific criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In recently immigrated or bilingual children, selective mutism requires refusal to speak the NATIVE language despite demonstrated competence — reticence limited to a newly learned language alone reflects normal adjustment, not a diagnosable disorder. COMLEX: This distinction prevents overdiagnosing selective mutism in children undergoing an expected, time-limited linguistic adjustment period.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — extends the ruling-out-secondary-organic-causes framework into distinguishing selective mutism from normal bilingual/immigration-related language adjustment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Selective mutism should NOT al"}},{"id":"sdl36_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate Selective Mutism from primary communication disorders and Social Anxiety Disorder by evaluating situational speech specificity (fluent speech at home vs. persistent failure to speak in setting-specific expectations like school for ≥ 1 month, ruling out language barriers or developmental speech delays, and selecting first-line cognitive-behavioral exposure strategies combined with SSRI pharmacotherapy when indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A therapist treating a child with selective mutism begins with low-stress tasks such as whispering to the mother while the therapist sits in the corner of the room, then progresses to nodding yes/no, pointing, whispering to a stuffed animal, whispering to the mother while facing the therapist, and eventually responding to the therapist directly, using animal puppets to help the child \"warm up.\" Which specific cognitive-behavioral technique does this graduated sequence exemplify?","choices":{"A":"Flooding, requiring immediate full exposure to the most feared situation","B":"Stimulus fading, advancing through a graded hierarchy toward the feared situation","C":"Systematic desensitization paired with pharmacologic sedation each session","D":"Aversion therapy pairing an unwanted behavior with an unpleasant consequence","E":"Psychodynamic free association with interpretation of unconscious resistance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This graduated sequence of increasingly less-avoidant speaking tasks (whispering to mother with distance, then progressively closer proximity and direct engagement with the therapist) exemplifies stimulus fading through a graded exposure hierarchy, a core cognitive-behavioral technique for selective mutism that gradually reduces the distance/difficulty between the child and the feared speaking situation. Flooding requires immediate full exposure to the most feared situation, the opposite of this described graduated, incremental approach. Systematic desensitization paired exclusively with pharmacologic sedation is not described here; this is a purely behavioral, exposure-based sequence without a described sedation component. Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behavior with an unpleasant consequence, the opposite of this approach, which pairs successful speaking behaviors with positive rewards (stickers, small toys) rather than punishing avoidance. Psychodynamic free association with no structured behavioral component contradicts this highly structured, stepwise behavioral hierarchy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stimulus fading through a graded exposure hierarchy — progressively reducing distance/difficulty toward the feared speaking situation — is the core CBT technique for selective mutism, often paired with positive reinforcement. COMLEX: This same graded-exposure principle underlies CBT for other pediatric anxiety disorders, including specific phobias and social anxiety disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder framework into the specific cognitive-behavioral exposure technique (stimulus fading) used to treat selective mutism's anxiety-driven speech avoidance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":2,"stem":"A previously high-functioning 12-year-old boy develops an abrupt onset of severe contamination fears, washing rituals, repeated questioning, and reassurance-seeking over 2 to 3 months, without any documented recent streptococcal infection. He meets full DSM-5 criteria for OCD, and his older sister had a milder, similar but untreated history. Given the absence of a documented preceding GABHS infection, which etiology is most strongly supported for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A PANDAS autoimmune etiology, following a documented streptococcal infection","B":"A purely environmental etiology, driven by modeling and family stress","C":"A structural traumatic brain injury etiology, from a prior closed head injury","D":"A genetic contribution, given the affected first-degree relative","E":"An idiopathic etiology, with no identifiable genetic or environmental driver"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The rate of OCD among first-degree relatives of children and adolescents who develop OCD is 10 times greater than in the general population, and genetic factors contribute significantly to early-onset OCD; this boy's family history of a similar, milder presentation in his sister, combined with the absence of a documented GABHS infection preceding his abrupt-onset symptoms, more strongly supports a genetic contribution than a PANDAS/autoimmune-triggered etiology, which specifically requires documented GABHS exposure or infection. A PANDAS/autoimmune-triggered etiology is specifically less supported here given the explicit absence of a documented preceding GABHS infection, a defining feature of the PANDAS hypothesis. A purely environmental etiology with no genetic contribution is contradicted by the described elevated familial OCD risk and this boy's documented positive family history. A structural traumatic brain injury etiology is not indicated by anything in this vignette; no such injury is described. An etiology can be reasonably assessed here; the family history and absence of GABHS documentation both point toward a more genetically-mediated presentation over PANDAS specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A positive family history of OCD (10x elevated risk in first-degree relatives) supports a genetic contribution to early-onset OCD, which should be weighed against PANDAS specifically when no documented GABHS infection precedes symptom onset. COMLEX: Even when GABHS exposure IS documented, causality remains controversial per prospective longitudinal data, making genetic and other etiologic factors equally important to consider.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — extends the primary-OCD-versus-secondary-medical/PANDAS-differentiation framework into weighing genetic contribution against autoimmune etiology when GABHS documentation is absent","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"An etiology can be reasonably "}},{"id":"sdl36_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":2,"stem":"Adult neuroimaging studies of untreated individuals with OCD have provided evidence of hypermetabolism in which specific neural network, and how do successful medication and behavioral interventions affect this finding?","choices":{"A":"The cerebellar-vestibular network, with both medication and behavioral interventions producing no measurable change in its metabolic rate","B":"The visual association cortex network, with both medication and behavioral interventions further increasing its already elevated metabolic rate","C":"The brainstem reticular activating system, with both medication and behavioral interventions producing no measurable change in its metabolic rate","D":"No specific hypermetabolic network has been identified, so neither medication nor behavioral interventions have any established metabolic correlate to report in this literature","E":"Frontal cortical-striatal-thalamocortical, or CSTC, networks, with both medication and behavioral interventions reducing orbitofrontal and caudate metabolic rates"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adult studies have provided evidence of hypermetabolism of frontal cortical-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) networks in untreated individuals with OCD, and imaging studies before and after treatment have revealed that both medication and behavioral interventions lead to a reduction of orbitofrontal and caudate metabolic rates in children and adults with OCD — exactly this finding and its treatment-responsive pattern, which points to CSTC circuit dysfunction. The cerebellar-vestibular network is not the network implicated in this OCD hypermetabolism literature. The visual association cortex network is also not implicated, and interventions are specifically described as reducing, not increasing, the relevant metabolic activity. The brainstem reticular activating system is not the network described in this OCD neuroimaging literature. A specific hypermetabolic network HAS been identified; the CSTC network's hypermetabolism, and its treatment-responsive normalization, is specifically described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSTC network hypermetabolism (particularly orbitofrontal and caudate regions) is a core neurobiological finding in OCD, and its reduction with successful treatment (medication or behavioral therapy) supports CSTC circuit dysfunction as central to OCD pathophysiology. COMLEX: This treatment-responsive neuroimaging finding provides objective biological support for both pharmacologic and CBT/ERP approaches to pediatric OCD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — extends the CSTC neurocircuitry pathogenesis framework (orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, striatum/caudate) into the specific treatment-responsive metabolic-imaging evidence supporting this circuit's role in OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Formulate a multimodal management and neurobiological evaluation plan for pediatric Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) by identifying cortico-striatal-thalamocortical (CSTC) circuit dysfunction and autoimmune etiologies (e.g., PANDAS secondary to Group A Streptococcal infection), evaluating level of insight and tic comorbidities, and selecting first-line FDA-approved therapeutics (combining Exposure and Response Prevention [ERP] CBT with SSRIs such as sertraline, fluoxetine, or fluvoxamine per POTS trial findings).","batch":2,"stem":"A child with comorbid OCD and Tourette syndrome, whose OCD has been treatment-resistant to high-dose SSRI monotherapy, is being considered for augmentation therapy. Compared with children who have OCD without a comorbid tic disorder, which compulsion type is this child more likely to exhibit, and which augmentation strategy is appropriate for this tic-related, treatment-resistant presentation?","choices":{"A":"Excessive washing and cleaning compulsions are more likely, warranting adjunctive clomipramine augmentation","B":"Counting, arranging, or ordering compulsions are more likely, warranting continued SSRI monotherapy without augmentation","C":"Counting, arranging, or ordering compulsions are more likely, warranting adjunctive risperidone augmentation","D":"Hoarding compulsions are more likely, warranting adjunctive benzodiazepine augmentation","E":"No compulsion-type difference exists, warranting the same SSRI dose escalation used in other OCD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Children with comorbid OCD and tic disorders are more likely to exhibit counting, arranging, or ordering compulsions and less likely to manifest excessive washing and cleaning compulsions, and for treatment-resistant or tic-related OCD cases, adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (such as risperidone) are an appropriate step-wise augmentation strategy alongside high-dose SSRIs and ERP — exactly this child's presentation and appropriate management. Excessive washing/cleaning compulsions being more likely is the opposite of the actual described pattern; these are LESS likely, not more likely, in tic-comorbid OCD. Counting/ordering compulsions being more likely is correctly identified, but continued high-dose SSRI monotherapy alone, without augmentation, does not match the described step-wise approach for treatment-resistant, tic-related cases, which specifically calls for adjunctive atypical antipsychotic augmentation. Hoarding compulsions specifically being more likely, with benzodiazepine augmentation, does not match either the described compulsion-type association or the described augmentation strategy. A compulsion-type difference based on tic comorbidity DOES exist; counting/arranging/ordering compulsions are specifically more common in this comorbid population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OCD with comorbid tic disorders shows a distinct compulsion phenotype (more counting/arranging/ordering, less washing/cleaning) and specifically benefits from adjunctive atypical antipsychotic augmentation (e.g., risperidone) when treatment-resistant. COMLEX: The shared genetic vulnerability hypothesized among OCD, Tourette syndrome, and ADHD may help explain both this phenotypic overlap and the augmentation strategy's effectiveness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — extends the step-wise OCD treatment framework (high-dose SSRIs, ERP, adjunctive atypical antipsychotics for treatment-resistant/tic-related cases) into the specific compulsion-phenotype difference seen in tic-comorbid pediatric OCD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A compulsion-type difference b"}},{"id":"sdl36_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old male with heavy marijuana use since age 15 begins to develop new-onset paranoid ideation and perceptual disturbances. A large longitudinal military cohort study is referenced regarding this risk. According to that study, individuals who had used marijuana by age 18 had how much greater relative risk of developing schizophrenia, and which additional cognitive domain is associated with chronic marijuana use in adolescents?","choices":{"A":"2.4 times greater relative risk of schizophrenia, with cognition fully preserved","B":"No increased relative risk of schizophrenia, with cognition fully preserved","C":"10 times greater relative risk of schizophrenia, with verbal memory deficits","D":"2.4 times lower relative risk of schizophrenia, with verbal memory deficits","E":"2.4 times greater relative risk of schizophrenia, with verbal memory deficits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A 15-year follow-up of 50,465 Swedish males in the military reported that participants who had used marijuana by 18 years of age were 2.4 times more likely to develop schizophrenia, and deficits in verbal learning, memory, and attention occur in chronic marijuana users — exactly matching both figures this question asks about. The 2.4-times figure is correct, but cognitive deficits (verbal learning, memory, attention) ARE specifically described as associated with chronic marijuana use, not absent. No increased risk being found is the opposite of this cohort study's actual finding of significantly elevated risk. A 10-times greater relative risk overstates the actual described 2.4-times figure. A 2.4-times LOWER risk (protective effect) is the opposite direction from the actual described increased-risk finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Swedish military cohort study's 2.4-times increased schizophrenia risk with adolescent marijuana use by age 18 is a specific, high-yield epidemiologic figure for cannabis-associated psychotic risk. COMLEX: Chronic marijuana use is separately associated with verbal learning, memory, and attention deficits, detectable via reduced cerebral blood flow to relevant brain regions on functional imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 6 — extends the MDMA-specific acute-physiologic-complication mechanism framework into cannabis's distinct long-term neurodevelopmental and psychotic-risk sequelae in adolescents","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The 2.4-times figure is correc","B":"No increased risk being found "}},{"id":"sdl36_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with a 2-year history of heavy alcohol use presents with new difficulties in sustained attention and short-term memory that her parents have noticed affecting her schoolwork. Which specific neurodevelopmental structural change has been reported to potentially explain this cognitive pattern in adolescents with chronic alcohol exposure?","choices":{"A":"Increased hippocampal volume, a structure central to memory consolidation","B":"Isolated cerebellar vermis atrophy, a structure central to motor coordination","C":"Diminished hippocampal volume, a structure central to memory consolidation","D":"Increased basal ganglia volume, a structure central to habit and movement","E":"Diffuse cortical thinning, sparing the subcortical memory structures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"One report has stated that adolescent exposure to alcohol may result in diminished hippocampal brain volume, and because the hippocampus is involved with attention, it is conceivable that adolescent alcohol use could result in compromised cognitive function, especially concerning attention — exactly matching this girl's described attention and memory difficulties. Increased hippocampal brain volume is the opposite of the actual reported finding, which describes diminished, not increased, volume. Isolated cerebellar vermis atrophy with no hippocampal involvement is not the described finding; the hippocampus specifically is implicated. Increased basal ganglia volume specifically is not the described structural change associated with adolescent alcohol exposure and attention/memory deficits; the hippocampal finding is what is described. Diffuse cortical thinning sparing subcortical structures is not the reported finding, and would not account for memory deficits, since the hippocampus is the structure implicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adolescent alcohol exposure's association with diminished hippocampal volume offers a plausible structural explanation for attention and memory deficits, given the hippocampus's role in these cognitive domains. COMLEX: Adolescents rarely develop the classic adult chronic-alcohol sequelae (Wernicke encephalopathy, Korsakoff syndrome, cirrhosis), making this hippocampal-volume finding a distinctly adolescent-relevant neurodevelopmental concern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 6 — extends the MDMA-specific mechanism framework into alcohol's distinct long-term neurodevelopmental sequela of reduced hippocampal volume in adolescents","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased hippocampal brain vo"}},{"id":"sdl36_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Create a comprehensive diagnostic assessment, risk stratification, and management plan for adolescent substance use disorders by evaluating drug-specific clinical manifestations and toxidromes (e.g., sympathomimetic effects of cocaine and MDMA vs. depressant/sedative properties of club drugs like GHB and Rohypnol), identifying long-term neurodevelopmental and psychiatric sequelae (such as cannabis-associated psychotic risk and reduced hippocampal volume from alcohol exposure), recognizing high-frequency psychiatric comorbidities (e.g., conduct disorder and depression), and applying evidence-based screening protocols (SBIRT), level-of-care assignment models (CALOCUS), and targeted pharmacotherapeutic interventions (e.g., bupropion for nicotine cessation; SSRIs for comorbid mood disorders).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy with escalating substance use, now requiring 2 or more structured treatment visits per week but not requiring 24-hour supervised care, also has a comorbid nicotine use disorder. Using the CALOCUS levels-of-care framework, which level best fits his current substance use severity, and which pharmacotherapy is specifically indicated for his comorbid nicotine use disorder?","choices":{"A":"Level 6, secure 24-hour medical management, with bupropion for the nicotine use disorder","B":"Level 3, intensive outpatient care, with bupropion for the nicotine use disorder","C":"Level 3, intensive outpatient care, with an SSRI for the nicotine use disorder","D":"Level 1, recovery maintenance, with bupropion for the nicotine use disorder","E":"Level 0, basic prevention services, with varenicline for the nicotine use disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CALOCUS Level 3 is defined as intensive outpatient care requiring 2 or more visits per week, matching this boy's described treatment frequency without requiring 24-hour supervised care (which would be Levels 5 or 6), and bupropion is specifically described as aiding in diminishing nicotine cravings and being beneficial for smoking cessation — exactly matching both the level-of-care assignment and the appropriate pharmacotherapy for his comorbid nicotine use disorder. Level 6, secure 24-hour medical management, does not match this boy's description of not requiring 24-hour supervised care. An SSRI is not the described first-line nicotine-cessation pharmacotherapy; bupropion is specifically described in that role, while SSRIs are described as useful for comorbid mood disorders instead. Level 1, recovery maintenance/relapse prevention, is a lower level of care than the 2-or-more-visits-per-week intensive outpatient level actually described for his presentation. Level 0, basic prevention services, is far below his needs, and bupropion, not varenicline, is the agent described for adolescent nicotine cessation (varenicline is not FDA-approved under age 18).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CALOCUS's six levels of care (0 through 6) match treatment intensity to substance use severity, with bupropion specifically indicated for nicotine cessation and SSRIs reserved for comorbid mood disorder management in adolescent substance use treatment. COMLEX: Matching level of care to clinical severity, alongside targeted pharmacotherapy for specific comorbid substance use disorders, exemplifies comprehensive adolescent substance use risk stratification and management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 6 — extends the specific-drug-mechanism framework into the broader adolescent substance-use risk-stratification (CALOCUS) and targeted pharmacotherapy (bupropion for nicotine) management approach","isHighYield":true}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"DSM-5 collapsed the formerly separate autism spectrum disorders into a single continuum diagnosis defined by impairments in two core domains. Which two domains are these?","choices":{"A":"Deficits in expressive language, and deficits in receptive language","B":"Intellectual disability, and impairment of motor coordination","C":"Aberrant language development, and abnormal sleep architecture","D":"Social communication deficits, and restricted, repetitive behavior","E":"Hallucinations, and fixed delusional beliefs held with conviction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"DSM-5 collapses the core diagnostic impairments of autism spectrum disorder into two domains: deficits in social communication, and restricted and repetitive behaviors or interests, moving away from the former five-way split (autistic disorder, Asperger disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder, Rett syndrome, and PDD-NOS) toward a continuum model — exactly these two domains. Expressive and receptive language deficits alone are not the two DSM-5 domains; aberrant language development and usage is explicitly no longer considered a core diagnostic feature, only an associated feature in some individuals. Intellectual disability and motor coordination impairment are common associated features, not the two defining diagnostic domains themselves. Aberrant language development and abnormal sleep architecture are not the two core domains either. Hallucinations and delusions are not features of ASD at all; they characterize childhood-onset schizophrenia, one of ASD's key differentials.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's two-domain model (social communication deficits, restricted/repetitive behaviors) replaced the prior five-way pervasive-developmental-disorder split, reflecting the current continuum conceptualization of ASD. COMLEX: Aberrant language development and usage is now an associated, not core diagnostic, feature of ASD per DSM-5.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — extends the psychotic-spectrum-disorder timeline/symptom-domain differentiation framework into distinguishing ASD from childhood-onset schizophrenia and its other core differentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Children with ASD often struggle to infer what others are feeling, wanting, or intending, leaving them unable to take another person's perspective or read unspoken interpersonal cues. What is this specific cognitive impairment called?","choices":{"A":"A deficit in working memory, impairing short-term information holding","B":"A deficit in visual-spatial processing, impairing spatial reasoning","C":"A deficit in declarative memory, impairing recall of facts and events","D":"A deficit in procedural memory, impairing motor-skill learning","E":"A deficit in theory of mind, impairing inference of others' mental states"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The impaired ability to make attributions about the motivations or intentions of others, leading to difficulty developing empathy and interpreting others' social behavior, is termed a deficit in theory of mind — exactly this cognitive impairment. A working memory deficit is a distinct cognitive domain not specifically defined by this social-attribution impairment. A visual-spatial processing deficit is also not this impairment; in fact, children with ASD are frequently more skilled in visual-spatial tasks than in verbal reasoning tasks. A procedural memory deficit does not describe this social-cognitive impairment. A long-term declarative memory deficit is also not the specific impairment described; theory of mind concerns social-cognitive attribution, not memory storage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Theory-of-mind deficits are the specific cognitive impairment underlying ASD's difficulty inferring others' emotional states and intentions, distinct from the disorder's relatively preserved or superior visual-spatial skills. COMLEX: fMRI studies find altered activation in the right temporal lobe and other theory-of-mind-related regions during these tasks in individuals with ASD, hypothesized to reflect mirror neuron system dysfunction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — extends the psychotic-spectrum-disorder timeline/symptom-domain differentiation framework into distinguishing ASD from childhood-onset schizophrenia and its other core differentials","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A child has difficulty with conventional greetings, taking turns in conversation, and responding to verbal and nonverbal cues of a listener, and occurs with higher frequency in relatives of individuals with ASD. However, the child does not exhibit any restricted or repetitive behaviors or interests. Which diagnosis best fits, and what specifically distinguishes it from ASD?","choices":{"A":"Autism spectrum disorder, requiring restricted or repetitive behaviors and interests","B":"Social, or pragmatic, communication disorder, a deficit in the social use of language","C":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia, which is characterized by hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized thinking beginning before adolescence","D":"An isolated language disorder, centering on vocabulary, grammar, and syntax","E":"Rett syndrome, an MECP2 disorder with regression and stereotypic hand movements"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Social (pragmatic) communication disorder involves difficulty conforming to typical storytelling and understanding the rules of social communication through language, including conventional greetings, turn-taking, and responding to listener cues, and it occurs with higher frequency in relatives of individuals with ASD — but unlike ASD, it does not include restricted or repetitive behaviors and interests, exactly the key distinguishing feature this question asks about. Autism spectrum disorder is defined by requiring restricted/repetitive behaviors, not lacking them, making this option the opposite of the actual distinction. Childhood-onset schizophrenia is characterized by hallucinations and delusions, not by this pattern of social-communication-rule difficulty, and is not what is described here. Language disorder alone is not specifically described as occurring at higher frequency in relatives of individuals with ASD, unlike social (pragmatic) communication disorder. Rett syndrome occurs exclusively in females and involves regression after a period of normal development, not this pattern of pragmatic language difficulty.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Social (pragmatic) communication disorder shares ASD's pragmatic-language difficulties and elevated rate in ASD relatives, but the presence versus absence of restricted/repetitive behaviors and interests is the key differentiating criterion. COMLEX: This distinction matters clinically because social (pragmatic) communication disorder does not respond to ASD-specific interventions targeting repetitive-behavior symptom clusters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — extends the psychotic-spectrum-disorder timeline/symptom-domain differentiation framework into distinguishing ASD from childhood-onset schizophrenia and its other core differentials","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Language disorder alone is not"}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":1,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy with ASD is found to have intellectual disability, gross and fine motor impairments, an unusual facies, macroorchidism, and significantly diminished expressive language ability, caused by a nucleotide repeat in the 5' untranslated region of the FMR1 gene. Which genetic syndrome is this, and what proportion of individuals with ASD does it account for?","choices":{"A":"Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited cause, present in 2 to 3 percent of individuals with ASD","B":"Tuberous sclerosis, present in 2 to 3 percent of individuals with ASD, an autosomal dominant tumor-forming disorder","C":"Down syndrome, present in 2 to 3 percent of individuals with ASD, caused by trisomy of chromosome 21","D":"Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited cause, present in 30 to 40 percent of individuals with ASD","E":"Prader-Willi syndrome, present in 2 to 3 percent of individuals with ASD, caused by loss of paternal chromosome 15 genes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fragile X syndrome, an X-linked disorder with atypical inheritance with a nucleotide repeat in the 5' untranslated region of the FMR1 gene, is the most common inherited genetic disorder causing ASD symptoms, present in 2 to 3 percent of individuals with ASD, and characteristically presents with intellectual disability, motor impairments, unusual facies, macroorchidism, and diminished expressive language — exactly this boy's presentation and figure. Tuberous sclerosis is a distinct autosomal dominant disorder characterized by multiple benign tumors, found in up to 2 percent of children with ASD, not the FMR1-linked X-linked disorder described here. Down syndrome is not the FMR1-linked disorder described and is not specifically the genetic syndrome most closely associated with this described nucleotide-repeat mechanism. The 2-to-3-percent figure is correct for Fragile X, not the much higher 30-to-40-percent figure. Prader-Willi syndrome is not the FMR1-linked disorder described in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fragile X syndrome (FMR1 trinucleotide repeat, X-linked with atypical inheritance) is the most common single inherited genetic cause of ASD symptoms, present in 2 to 3 percent of ASD cases, with macroorchidism as a classic associated physical finding. COMLEX: Female carriers of Fragile X are typically less impaired than affected males, consistent with its X-linked inheritance and variable expression in females.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome-with-intellectual-disability framework (Fragile X, Down, Prader-Willi) into ASD's own high-risk genetic conditions and neurobiological biomarkers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with ASD is also found to have multiple benign tumors on imaging, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Up to what percentage of children with ASD also have this second most common neurocutaneous genetic disorder?","choices":{"A":"Up to 25 percent, the disorder being tuberous sclerosis","B":"Up to 50 percent, the disorder being neurofibromatosis type 1","C":"Up to 75 percent, the disorder being Sturge-Weber syndrome","D":"Up to 90 percent, the disorder being incontinentia pigmenti","E":"Up to 2 percent, the disorder being tuberous sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tuberous sclerosis, the second most common of the neurocutaneous disorders, characterized by multiple benign tumors and inherited by autosomal dominant transmission, is found with higher frequency among children with ASD, with up to 2 percent of children with ASD also having tuberous sclerosis — exactly this figure. Up to 25 percent overstates the actual described 2-percent figure considerably. Up to 50 percent and up to 75 percent both overstate the actual figure even further. Incontinentia pigmenti is a rarer neurocutaneous disorder and is not the one described; tuberous sclerosis is the second most common, and 90 percent grossly overstates any such co-occurrence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tuberous sclerosis (autosomal dominant, multiple benign tumors) is found in up to 2 percent of children with ASD, the second most common genetic disorder associated with the ASD phenotype after Fragile X syndrome. COMLEX: Both Fragile X syndrome and tuberous sclerosis exemplify genetic disorders that cause ASD symptoms as part of a broader syndromic phenotype, distinct from idiopathic polygenic ASD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome-with-intellectual-disability framework (Fragile X, Down, Prader-Willi) into ASD's own high-risk genetic conditions and neurobiological biomarkers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":1,"stem":"The first biomarker identified in ASD was an elevated laboratory finding involving a specific neurotransmitter, measured almost exclusively in one blood component. What is this biomarker?","choices":{"A":"Elevated whole-blood dopamine, held almost entirely in the plasma","B":"Decreased whole-blood serotonin, held almost entirely in the platelets","C":"Elevated whole-blood GABA, held almost entirely in the red blood cells","D":"Elevated whole-blood serotonin, held almost entirely in the platelets","E":"Elevated whole-blood norepinephrine, held almost entirely in leukocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first biomarker identified in ASD was elevated serotonin (5-HT) in whole blood, almost exclusively in the platelets, which acquire 5-HT through the SERT (serotonin transporter) as they pass through the intestinal circulation, with the genes mediating SERT and the 5-HT2A receptor each encoding the same respective protein in platelets as in brain — exactly this platelet hyperserotonemia biomarker. Elevated whole-blood dopamine in the plasma is not the described first biomarker; the finding is specifically serotonin in platelets, not dopamine in plasma. Decreased whole-blood serotonin is the opposite direction from the actual elevated finding. Elevated whole-blood GABA in red blood cells is not this biomarker; GABA inhibitory system alterations are a separately described, later-identified biomarker category, not measured this way. Elevated whole-blood norepinephrine in white blood cells is also not the described finding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Platelet hyperserotonemia, mediated by the SERT and HTR2A genes shared between platelets and brain, was the first identified ASD biomarker, reflecting serotonin's broader role in brain development and neuronal migration. COMLEX: Other ASD biomarker categories include mTOR-linked synaptic plasticity mechanisms and GABA inhibitory system alterations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome-with-intellectual-disability framework (Fragile X, Down, Prader-Willi) into ASD's own high-risk genetic conditions and neurobiological biomarkers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with ASD exhibits severe irritability, including aggression and self-injurious behavior. Which medication is considered first-line for this specific target symptom, and what are its main limiting side effects?","choices":{"A":"Aripiprazole, limited mainly by weight gain and metabolic effects such as hyperglycemia","B":"Risperidone, limited mainly by akathisia and insomnia","C":"Risperidone, limited mainly by weight gain and metabolic effects such as hyperglycemia","D":"Fluoxetine, limited mainly by behavioral activation and agitation","E":"Methylphenidate, limited mainly by appetite suppression and sleep delay"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Risperidone, a high-potency antipsychotic with combined dopamine D2 and serotonin 5-HT2 receptor antagonist properties, is considered the first-line medication treatment for children and adolescents with ASD who exhibit severe irritability, though its efficacy is limited in some individuals by weight gain, metabolic side effects like hyperglycemia, prolactin elevation, and dyslipidemia — exactly this first-line status and side-effect profile. Aripiprazole is FDA-approved for the same indication but is not designated the first-line agent here, and its weight gain, while still a moderate adverse event, is less pronounced than risperidone's. Risperidone's main limiting side effects are specifically weight gain and metabolic effects, not primarily akathisia and insomnia, which are more characteristic of aripiprazole's side-effect profile. Fluoxetine is not the first-line agent for ASD-related irritability and does not carry this weight-gain/metabolic side-effect profile as its main limitation. Methylphenidate is a stimulant used for ADHD symptoms, not the first-line agent for ASD-related irritability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Risperidone is first-line for severe ASD-related irritability, but its clinical use is limited by weight gain and metabolic adverse effects (hyperglycemia, dyslipidemia, prolactin elevation) requiring monitoring. COMLEX: Risperidone should be used cautiously in patients with underlying cardiac abnormalities or hypotension, given its risk of contributing to orthostatic hypotension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — extends the antipsychotic-selection-by-indication framework (which explicitly includes autism-related irritability) into risperidone/aripiprazole's targeted role in ASD symptom management","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Fluoxetine is not the first-li","E":"Methylphenidate is a stimulant"}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with ASD-related aggression and self-injury is started on the second FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotic for this indication, at a dose of 10 mg/day. Compared with risperidone, this agent's weight gain is less pronounced but still a moderate adverse event. Which additional adverse effects are more characteristic of this specific agent?","choices":{"A":"Hyperprolactinemia, galactorrhea, and orthostatic hypotension","B":"Agranulocytosis, neutropenia, and fever","C":"Hepatotoxicity, elevated transaminases, and jaundice","D":"QT prolongation, torsades de pointes, and syncope","E":"Sedation, dizziness, insomnia, akathisia, nausea, and vomiting"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aripiprazole, the second FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotic for treatment of irritability in ASD, has main side effects including sedation, dizziness, insomnia, akathisia, nausea, and vomiting, with weight gain present but less pronounced than with risperidone — exactly this described adverse-effect profile. Hyperprolactinemia and orthostatic hypotension are more characteristic of risperidone's combined D2/5-HT2 antagonism, not specifically highlighted as aripiprazole's distinguishing adverse effects here. Hepatotoxicity requiring liver function monitoring is not the described adverse-effect profile for aripiprazole in ASD treatment. Severe QT prolongation requiring universal baseline ECG is not the described adverse-effect profile for aripiprazole here. Agranulocytosis requiring weekly blood counts is a clozapine-specific concern, not a described aripiprazole adverse effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aripiprazole's adverse-effect profile in ASD-related irritability (sedation, dizziness, insomnia, akathisia, nausea/vomiting) differs from risperidone's more prominent metabolic/prolactin-related effects, though weight gain still occurs with both. COMLEX: Choice between risperidone and aripiprazole for ASD-related irritability can be individualized based on which adverse-effect profile a given patient is more likely to tolerate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 4 — extends the antipsychotic-selection-by-indication framework (which explicitly includes autism-related irritability) into risperidone/aripiprazole's targeted role in ASD symptom management","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hepatotoxicity requiring liver"}},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":1,"stem":"A 2-year-old with ASD begins an intervention occurring in naturalistic settings such as daycare and home, in which parents learn to act as co-therapists, with a focus on developing basic play and relationship skills by integrating applied behavior analysis into the child's daily routine. Which early intensive behavioral and developmental intervention model does this describe?","choices":{"A":"The Early Start Denver Model, designed for toddlers within everyday routines","B":"The UCLA/Lovaas-based model, an intensive one-on-one applied-behavior-analysis intervention","C":"TEACCH, a structured-teaching model using visual supports and picture schedules","D":"Pivotal Response Training, targeting specific gateway social behaviors for parent-facilitated mastery","E":"Social skills training in a group setting, typically implemented with older, school-age children"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Early Start Denver Model (ESDM) occurs in naturalistic settings such as daycare, home, and play with other children, with parents trained as co-therapists, focusing on developing basic play and relationship skills by integrating applied behavior analysis into the child's daily routine, specifically for very young children — exactly this description. The UCLA/Lovaas-based model is an intensive, manualized, primarily one-on-one applied-behavior-analysis intervention given for many hours per week, not this naturalistic, parent-co-therapist, daily-routine-integrated approach. TEACCH is a structured-teaching educational intervention using visual supports and picture schedules, not this naturalistic play-and-relationship-focused model. Pivotal Response Training targets specific gateway/pivotal social behaviors for parent-facilitated mastery with the expectation of natural generalization, a related but distinct parent-training approach from ESDM's broader daily-routine integration. Social skills training in a group setting is a school-age, peer-group-based intervention, not this very-young-child, naturalistic, parent-co-therapist model.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Early Start Denver Model is distinguished by its naturalistic-setting, parent-as-co-therapist, daily-routine-integrated approach for very young children with ASD, contrasted with the UCLA/Lovaas model's intensive one-on-one clinical format. COMLEX: Five RCTs of early intensive comprehensive behavioral interventions in children age 2 to 5 have shown increases in language acquisition, social interaction, and educational achievement versus controls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 -> Obj 5 — extends the conditioning-based behavioral technique framework into early intensive behavioral interventions for ASD (ABA, ESDM)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is applying DSM-5's age-dependent symptom threshold for diagnosing ADHD. How many symptoms of inattention or hyperactivity/impulsivity are required in a 9-year-old child versus an 18-year-old adult?","choices":{"A":"Seven or more symptoms in the child; six or more symptoms in the adult","B":"Seven or more symptoms in the child; five or more symptoms in the adult","C":"Four or more symptoms in the child; four or more symptoms in the adult","D":"Six or more symptoms in the child; four or more symptoms in the adult","E":"Six or more symptoms in the child; five or more symptoms in the adult"},"correct":"E","explanation":"DSM-5 requires six or more symptoms of either inattention or hyperactivity/impulsivity, persisting for at least 6 months, for a diagnosis of ADHD in children, but for older adolescents (17 years and older) and adults, only five or more symptoms are required — exactly this age-dependent pairing. The child threshold is six, not seven or four, and the adult threshold is five, not six or four.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's reduced five-symptom threshold for ages 17 and older (versus six for younger children) reflects that ADHD symptoms may present more subtly, or be better compensated for, as patients age. COMLEX: Both age groups still require symptoms to have been present prior to age 12, cross-setting pervasiveness in at least two settings, and clear functional impairment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 5 — extends the stimulant-induced dopamine/serotonin neurochemistry framework into ADHD's own dopaminergic/noradrenergic prefrontal circuit dysregulation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The child threshold is six, no"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":1,"stem":"A child's parents report significant inattention and hyperactivity at home, but his teachers report no concerns whatsoever at school. Per DSM-5 criteria, can this child be diagnosed with ADHD based on the home-only symptom pattern, and by what age must symptoms have appeared?","choices":{"A":"Yes, symptoms confined to a single setting suffice, with symptoms present before age 12","B":"No, pervasiveness across at least 2 settings is required, with symptoms present before age 7","C":"Yes, symptoms confined to a single setting suffice, with symptoms present before age 7","D":"No, pervasiveness across at least 2 settings is required, with symptoms present before age 12","E":"Yes, symptoms confined to a single setting suffice, with no age-of-onset threshold"},"correct":"D","explanation":"To confirm a diagnosis of ADHD, impairment from inattention or hyperactivity/impulsivity must be present in at least two settings, and several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms must have been present prior to age 12 years per DSM-5 — exactly this cross-setting and age-of-onset pairing, meaning this child's home-only pattern does not meet the pervasiveness criterion as described. Symptoms confined to a single setting are not sufficient for diagnosis; the 2-or-more-setting requirement exists to rule out situation-specific behavioral issues. The DSM-5 age-of-onset threshold is before age 12; before age 7 was the prior DSM-IV criterion, since revised. A specific age-of-onset threshold does apply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's cross-setting pervasiveness requirement (≥2 settings) and age-12 onset threshold (revised from DSM-IV's age 7) both help distinguish true ADHD from situation-specific behavioral problems or later-onset presentations better explained by another cause. COMLEX: A comprehensive ADHD evaluation should include reports from at least two independent settings (e.g., parent and teacher) to confirm this pervasiveness criterion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 5 — extends the stimulant-induced dopamine/serotonin neurochemistry framework into ADHD's own dopaminergic/noradrenergic prefrontal circuit dysregulation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Symptoms confined to a single ","B":"The DSM-5 age-of-onset thresho"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Neurobiological models of ADHD implicate a specific brain region and neurotransmitter system underlying the disorder's hallmark executive dysfunction. Which region and mechanism are most centrally implicated?","choices":{"A":"Prefrontal disinhibition, with dopamine and norepinephrine dysregulation","B":"Occipital cortical disinhibition, with serotonergic circuit dysregulation","C":"Cerebellar disinhibition, with cholinergic circuit dysregulation","D":"Hippocampal disinhibition, with glutamatergic circuit dysregulation","E":"Striatal disinhibition, with histaminergic circuit dysregulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The prefrontal cortex is a focus of interest in ADHD neurobiology because of its high dopamine utilization and its reciprocal connections with other brain regions involved in attention, inhibition, decision-making, response inhibition, working memory, and vigilance, with dopaminergic and noradrenergic circuit dysregulation implicated in the disorder's executive dysfunction — exactly this region and mechanism. Occipital cortical disinhibition with serotonergic dysregulation is not the implicated region/mechanism; the occipital cortex is primarily visual processing, not executive function. Cerebellar disinhibition with cholinergic dysregulation is also not the primary implicated mechanism for ADHD's executive dysfunction. Hippocampal disinhibition with glutamatergic dysregulation is not the described mechanism either; the hippocampus is more centrally implicated in memory formation. Striatal disinhibition with histaminergic dysregulation is not the implicated pairing; histamine has no established role in ADHD executive dysfunction, and the prefrontal cortex is the region of interest.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prefrontal cortical dysfunction with dopaminergic/noradrenergic dysregulation is the core neurobiological model for ADHD's executive dysfunction, directly explaining why stimulants targeting these same neurotransmitter systems are first-line treatment. COMLEX: This same prefrontal dopaminergic/noradrenergic framework explains atomoxetine's and the alpha2-adrenergic agonists' mechanism-matched non-stimulant alternatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 5 — extends the stimulant-induced dopamine/serotonin neurochemistry framework into ADHD's own dopaminergic/noradrenergic prefrontal circuit dysregulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":1,"stem":"A child being considered for stimulant treatment of ADHD has a family history of sudden cardiac death and a personal history of syncope. What is the recommended baseline pre-treatment step for this specific child before initiating a stimulant?","choices":{"A":"Proceed with routine vital signs and no additional cardiac evaluation","B":"Initiate the stimulant now and obtain an ECG if symptoms develop","C":"Defer cardiac evaluation until adulthood, when the risk becomes relevant","D":"Consider electrocardiography and cardiology referral, then start the stimulant","E":"Withhold stimulants permanently given any family history of cardiac death"},"correct":"D","explanation":"National guidelines recommend that physicians consider electrocardiography and/or cardiology referral before initiating psychostimulants in patients with a history of heart disease, palpitations, syncope, or seizures, or with a family history of sudden cardiac death, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, or long QT syndrome — exactly the recommended step for this child's described risk factors. No additional cardiac evaluation beyond routine vitals contradicts this specific guideline for a child with these described red-flag risk factors. Initiating the stimulant first and only obtaining an ECG if symptoms develop reverses the recommended pre-treatment sequence for this higher-risk child. This cardiac evaluation recommendation applies based on specific risk factors, not exclusively to adults; it specifically addresses pediatric ADHD stimulant initiation here. Stimulants are not absolutely contraindicated by family cardiac history alone; rather, further cardiac evaluation (ECG and/or cardiology referral) is recommended before proceeding, not an automatic contraindication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baseline pre-treatment ADHD screening should include a complete cardiac and neurologic exam, with ECG and/or cardiology referral specifically considered when red-flag features (personal syncope/palpitations, family history of sudden cardiac death or channelopathies) are present. COMLEX: Height, weight, heart rate, blood pressure, symptoms, mood, and adherence should all be monitored at follow-up visits after stimulant initiation as well.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 4 — extends the mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy framework for pediatric motor disorders (alpha2-adrenergic agonists, D2 antagonists) into ADHD's own stimulant-versus-non-stimulant treatment-selection process","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"This cardiac evaluation recomm","E":"Stimulants are not absolutely "}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician is selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy for a newly diagnosed 8-year-old with ADHD. Which class of medication is considered first-line, and which two classes are considered non-stimulant alternatives?","choices":{"A":"Stimulants first-line; atomoxetine and alpha2-adrenergic agonists as alternatives","B":"Atomoxetine first-line; stimulants and alpha2-adrenergic agonists as alternatives","C":"Alpha2-adrenergic agonists first-line; stimulants and atomoxetine as alternatives","D":"Tricyclic antidepressants first-line; atomoxetine and alpha2-adrenergic agonists as alternatives","E":"Benzodiazepines first-line; atomoxetine and bupropion as alternatives"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Psychostimulants, which increase dopamine and norepinephrine availability, are first-line and most effective for core ADHD symptoms, with atomoxetine and the alpha2-adrenergic agonists (guanfacine, clonidine) as non-stimulant alternatives for patients who do not respond to, cannot tolerate, or have contraindications to stimulants — exactly this described treatment hierarchy. Atomoxetine being first-line reverses the actual hierarchy; atomoxetine is specifically a second-line non-stimulant alternative, not first-line. Alpha2-adrenergic agonists being first-line similarly reverses the hierarchy; they too are non-stimulant alternatives, not first-line agents. Tricyclic antidepressants are not described as first-line or even as one of the two standard non-stimulant alternative classes for ADHD. Benzodiazepines are not indicated for ADHD treatment at all and are not one of the described non-stimulant alternative classes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants remain first-line for ADHD given their superior effect size, with atomoxetine (selective NET inhibitor) and alpha2-adrenergic agonists (guanfacine, clonidine) as the two established non-stimulant alternative classes. COMLEX: Behavioral therapy is the primary treatment recommended for children younger than 6 years, while medication is recommended to start treatment in children 6 years and older.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 4 — extends the mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy framework for pediatric motor disorders (alpha2-adrenergic agonists, D2 antagonists) into ADHD's own stimulant-versus-non-stimulant treatment-selection process","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Atomoxetine being first-line r","C":"Alpha2-adrenergic agonists bei"}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":1,"stem":"A 17-year-old with ADHD and a co-occurring substance use disorder needs pharmacotherapy. Which non-stimulant agent is specifically favored in this situation, and why?","choices":{"A":"Atomoxetine, favored in comorbid substance use disorder for its rapid onset","B":"Atomoxetine, favored in comorbid substance use disorder for its low misuse risk","C":"Short-acting methylphenidate, favored in comorbid substance use for its safety","D":"Lisdexamfetamine, favored in comorbid substance use disorder as a non-stimulant","E":"Bupropion, favored in comorbid substance use disorder for its dual indication"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Atomoxetine is a reasonable choice in the treatment of patients with both ADHD and substance abuse because it has no abuse potential, unlike stimulant medications, particularly short-acting formulations, which carry addictive potential requiring caution in this comorbid population — exactly this rationale. Atomoxetine does not have the fastest onset of action; in fact, it has a slower initial response than stimulants, with symptom improvement typically noticed only after several weeks, so a fast-onset rationale is incorrect. Short-acting methylphenidate is specifically the type of stimulant formulation requiring the most caution in comorbid substance use disorder due to its addictive potential, not a no-abuse-potential option. Lisdexamfetamine is not a non-stimulant at all; Kaplan and Sadock list it with the amphetamine stimulants, so it carries the stimulant misuse concern this patient raises. Bupropion is not the non-stimulant highlighted for this comorbidity; atomoxetine's lack of abuse potential is the specific rationale given.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atomoxetine's lack of abuse potential makes it a preferred non-stimulant choice for ADHD with comorbid substance use disorder, or when stimulant-seeking behavior is a clinical concern. COMLEX: Short-acting stimulant formulations carry the greatest addictive-potential concern in this population; long-acting stimulant formulations or non-stimulants are preferred alternatives when stimulant treatment is still considered.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 -> Obj 4 — extends the mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy framework for pediatric motor disorders (alpha2-adrenergic agonists, D2 antagonists) into ADHD's own stimulant-versus-non-stimulant treatment-selection process","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Atomoxetine does not have the "}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"Methylphenidate's pharmacologic activity results mainly from a specific mechanism at the presynaptic neuron. What is this primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Promoting presynaptic efflux of dopamine into the extracellular space","B":"Directly agonizing postsynaptic alpha2-adrenergic receptors in the prefrontal cortex","C":"Selectively inhibiting the norepinephrine transporter, sparing dopamine uptake","D":"Blocking the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters, inhibiting their reuptake","E":"Activating orexin-producing neurons of the lateral hypothalamus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pharmacological activity of methylphenidate results mainly from blocking the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters (DAT and NET), thus inhibiting the reuptake of these neurotransmitters into presynaptic neurons — exactly this described primary mechanism. Promoting presynaptic dopamine efflux into the extracellular space is amphetamines' additional, distinguishing mechanism beyond reuptake blockade, not methylphenidate's primary described mechanism. Directly agonizing postsynaptic alpha2-adrenergic receptors describes guanfacine and clonidine's mechanism, not methylphenidate's. Selectively inhibiting only the norepinephrine transporter describes atomoxetine's mechanism, not methylphenidate's combined DAT/NET blockade. Activating hypothalamic hypocretin-producing neurons describes modafinil's proposed mechanism, not methylphenidate's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Methylphenidate's mechanism is combined DAT/NET reuptake blockade, distinguishing it from amphetamines' additional presynaptic dopamine-efflux-promoting action, even though both increase synaptic dopamine/norepinephrine availability. COMLEX: This reuptake-blockade mechanism underlies methylphenidate's rapid onset of action, typically within 20 to 30 minutes for short-acting methylphenidate (AAFP table, SDL 37 reading).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 4 — extends the sympathomimetic toxidrome mechanism framework into the therapeutic-dose mechanisms of methylphenidate, amphetamines, modafinil, and atomoxetine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"Both methylphenidate and amphetamines block the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters. Which additional mechanism do amphetamines possess that methylphenidate does not, accounting for their differing pharmacology?","choices":{"A":"Inhibiting the norepinephrine transporter with no effect on dopamine","B":"Directly agonizing postsynaptic alpha2-adrenergic receptors in the cortex","C":"Promoting presynaptic efflux of dopamine into the synaptic cleft","D":"Activating orexin-producing neurons of the lateral hypothalamus","E":"Directly stimulating postsynaptic D1 and D2 dopamine receptors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Besides inhibiting DAT and NET through direct binding as a false substrate, amphetamines also promote increased presynaptic efflux of dopamine into the extracellular space, a mechanism methylphenidate does not share, accounting for amphetamines' generally greater effect sizes in reducing ADHD symptoms compared with methylphenidate — exactly this additional distinguishing mechanism. Inhibiting the norepinephrine transporter without a dopamine effect is atomoxetine's mechanism. Direct postsynaptic alpha2-adrenergic agonism is how guanfacine and clonidine act. Activating orexin-producing hypothalamic neurons is modafinil's proposed wake-promoting mechanism. Amphetamines raise synaptic dopamine indirectly; they do not stimulate D1 or D2 receptors directly the way a dopamine agonist does.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Amphetamines' additional presynaptic-dopamine-efflux mechanism, beyond the DAT/NET reuptake blockade shared with methylphenidate, helps explain amphetamines' generally larger ADHD symptom-reduction effect sizes. COMLEX: This mechanistic distinction is relevant when a patient fails to respond adequately to one stimulant class and a trial of the other class is considered.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 4 — extends the sympathomimetic toxidrome mechanism framework into the therapeutic-dose mechanisms of methylphenidate, amphetamines, modafinil, and atomoxetine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"Modafinil's specific mechanism of action remains unknown, but it does not appear to act through a primarily dopaminergic mechanism. Its wakefulness-promoting effect can be attenuated by prazosin, an alpha1-adrenergic antagonist. What does this observation suggest about modafinil's mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Modafinil is a pure dopamine transporter inhibitor with no adrenergic activity","B":"Modafinil directly activates hypocretin receptors as its primary mechanism","C":"Modafinil is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor with no adrenergic activity","D":"Modafinil has alpha1-adrenergic antagonist properties blunting its own effect","E":"Modafinil has alpha1-adrenergic agonist properties accounting for its alerting effect"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Modafinil has alpha1-adrenergic agonist properties, which may account for its alerting effects, since the wakefulness induced by modafinil can be attenuated by prazosin, an alpha1-adrenergic antagonist, and some evidence also suggests modafinil has norepinephrine reuptake blocking effects — exactly this described mechanistic inference. Modafinil is explicitly described as NOT appearing to work through a primarily dopaminergic mechanism, contradicting a \"pure dopamine transporter inhibitor\" characterization, and it does have adrenergic activity, contradicting \"no adrenergic activity.\" Modafinil does not directly activate hypocretin receptors as its primary mechanism; rather, hypocretin-producing neurons are activated after modafinil administration through an indirect, not fully understood, pathway. Modafinil is not described as an SSRI or having a serotonergic mechanism. Alpha1-adrenergic antagonist properties would have modafinil blunting its own alerting effect, the opposite of the inference the prazosin finding supports, which is alpha1 agonism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Modafinil's mechanism is distinct from classic stimulants — not primarily dopaminergic, but implicating alpha1-adrenergic agonism (per prazosin attenuation) and hypocretin-neuron activation, with narcolepsy-cataplexy itself resulting from hypocretin deficiency. COMLEX: Modafinil is equally useful as amphetamine-like drugs at maintaining wakefulness but with a lower risk of excessive activation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 -> Obj 4 — extends the sympathomimetic toxidrome mechanism framework into the therapeutic-dose mechanisms of methylphenidate, amphetamines, modafinil, and atomoxetine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Modafinil is not described as "}},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":1,"stem":"A child on stimulant medication for ADHD develops anorexia, nausea, and mild weight loss. Which management strategies are specifically recommended for these adverse effects?","choices":{"A":"Discontinue afternoon dosing and switch to a short-acting form","B":"Give the stimulant with meals and add caloric supplements","C":"Overlap stimulant dosing so coverage spans the whole day","D":"Reduce the dose and add lithium as an adjunctive agent","E":"Stop the stimulant and switch to a non-stimulant agent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The management of stimulant-induced anorexia, nausea, and weight loss is to administer the stimulant with meals, use caloric-enhanced supplements, and discourage forcing meals — exactly this described strategy. Discontinuing afternoon dosing and changing to a short-acting preparation instead targets sleep-onset difficulty, not anorexia/weight loss. Overlapping stimulant dosing throughout the day instead targets rebound phenomena as the dose wears off, not appetite loss. Reducing the dose with adjunctive lithium instead targets mood lability and irritability, not specifically anorexia/weight loss. Stopping the stimulant and switching agents is a disproportionate response to mild appetite loss, which is managed with dosing and caloric measures before any change of agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stimulant-induced anorexia/weight loss is managed with meal-timing (administering with meals) and caloric supplementation rather than forcing intake, distinct from the strategies used for insomnia, rebound, or irritability. COMLEX: Longitudinal studies do not indicate that sympathomimetics cause growth suppression despite this appetite-reduction effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — extends the side-effect-profile-to-receptor-mechanism framework into the specific medical management strategies for stimulant-induced insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":1,"stem":"A child on a stimulant for ADHD develops difficulty falling asleep and nightmares. Which management strategies are specifically recommended?","choices":{"A":"Administer the stimulant with meals and add caloric-enhanced supplements","B":"Overlap stimulant dosing so a second dose covers the late afternoon","C":"Dose earlier in the day, use a short-acting form, and drop evening doses","D":"Change to a long-acting form, or combine long- and short-acting forms","E":"Reduce the dose and add lithium or an antidepressant as an adjunct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The recommended management of stimulant-induced insomnia and nightmares is to administer stimulants earlier in the day, change to short-acting preparations, discontinue afternoon or evening dosing, and consider adjunctive treatment such as antihistamines, clonidine, or antidepressants if needed — exactly this described strategy. Giving the stimulant with meals and adding caloric supplements is the listed management for anorexia. Overlapping doses, or moving to a long-acting or combined long- and short-acting regimen, is the listed management for rebound symptoms as a dose wears off — and extending drug exposure later into the day would make insomnia worse. Reducing the dose and adding lithium or an antidepressant is the listed approach to irritability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stimulant-induced insomnia/nightmares is managed by shifting dosing earlier in the day and favoring short-acting formulations, the mechanistic opposite of the long-acting/dose-overlap strategy used for rebound phenomena. COMLEX: Adjunctive antihistamines, clonidine, or antidepressants may be considered when timing/formulation changes alone are insufficient for stimulant-induced insomnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — extends the side-effect-profile-to-receptor-mechanism framework into the specific medical management strategies for stimulant-induced insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Overlapping doses, or moving t"}},{"id":"sdl37_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":1,"stem":"A child on a short-acting stimulant develops worsening irritability and hyperactivity each afternoon as the medication's effect wears off, before the next dose. Which management strategies specifically address this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Administer the stimulant with meals, and discourage forcing meals at the table","B":"Give the dose earlier in the day, and discontinue any evening dosing","C":"Encourage fluid intake, and monitor blood pressure at follow-up visits","D":"Reduce the dose, and add lithium as an adjunctive mood-stabilizing agent","E":"Overlap stimulant dosing, or move to an extended-release or combined regimen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This pattern of worsening symptoms as a short-acting stimulant's effect wears off, before the next dose, describes rebound phenomena, managed by overlapping stimulant dosing, or changing to a long-acting preparation or a combined long-acting/short-acting regimen, with adjunctive or alternative treatment such as clonidine or antidepressants considered if needed — exactly this described strategy. Administering with meals and discouraging forced meals is the recommended management for anorexia. Moving the dose earlier and stopping evening doses is the approach to stimulant-induced insomnia; here it would leave the afternoon even less covered. Encouraging fluid intake and monitoring blood pressure is the recommended management for dizziness. Reducing the dose with adjunctive lithium is the recommended management for irritability, which is not tied to this wearing-off timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stimulant rebound phenomena (symptom worsening as the dose wears off) is managed by dose overlap or switching to long-acting/combined formulations, the opposite formulation strategy from insomnia management's short-acting, earlier-dosing approach. COMLEX: Assessing the specific timing of symptom worsening (peak effect versus withdrawal phase) helps distinguish rebound phenomena from other stimulant adverse effects requiring different management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 4 — extends the side-effect-profile-to-receptor-mechanism framework into the specific medical management strategies for stimulant-induced insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"Atomoxetine is metabolized principally through the CYP2D6 pathway. Compared with normal or extensive metabolizers, how do CYP2D6 poor metabolizers' plasma atomoxetine levels differ?","choices":{"A":"At least fivefold higher area under the curve and about fivefold higher peak","B":"About fivefold higher area under the curve, with an unchanged peak concentration","C":"About twofold higher area under the curve and twofold higher peak concentration","D":"About twofold higher area under the curve, with an unchanged peak concentration","E":"Unchanged area under the curve, with a tenfold higher peak concentration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Poor metabolizers of atomoxetine, which is cleared mainly by CYP2D6, reach at least a fivefold higher area under the curve and about a fivefold higher peak plasma concentration than extensive metabolizers. Because clearance falls, total exposure (AUC) and the peak both rise, so an unchanged peak does not fit. The twofold figures understate the size of the effect, and an unchanged AUC is not possible when clearance is reduced this much.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CYP2D6 poor metabolizers reach at least fivefold higher atomoxetine AUC and about fivefold higher Cmax than extensive metabolizers, a clinically significant pharmacokinetic variation relevant to dosing and toxicity risk. COMLEX: Approximately 7 to 10 percent of white individuals and 3 to 8 percent of African American individuals are unable to metabolize CYP2D6 substrates effectively, representing the poor-metabolizer phenotype.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — extends the CYP450 drug-drug-interaction framework (fluoxetine/paroxetine-induced CYP2D6 inhibition) into its specific effect on atomoxetine plasma concentrations and toxicity risk","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Because clearance falls, total","E":"The twofold figures understate"}},{"id":"sdl37_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on atomoxetine for ADHD is started on an SSRI for comorbid depression. Which SSRIs specifically raise atomoxetine plasma levels through CYP2D6 inhibition, requiring caution with this combination?","choices":{"A":"Sertraline and citalopram","B":"Fluvoxamine and escitalopram","C":"Fluoxetine and paroxetine","D":"Citalopram and escitalopram","E":"Sertraline and fluvoxamine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fluoxetine and paroxetine are potent CYP2D6 inhibitors and may raise atomoxetine levels, a clinically important consideration since atomoxetine's antidepressant-like pharmacology has led to its use as an add-on to SSRIs or other antidepressants — exactly these two named agents. Sertraline, citalopram and escitalopram are the SSRIs least likely to cause interactions, with little CYP2D6 inhibition at usual doses. Fluvoxamine's interactions come mainly through CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 — theophylline, clozapine, alprazolam — rather than CYP2D6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fluoxetine and paroxetine are specifically named CYP2D6 inhibitors that can raise atomoxetine plasma concentrations, mirroring the general principle that potent CYP2D6-inhibiting SSRIs elevate levels of co-administered CYP2D6 substrates. COMLEX: Bupropion is also a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor with the same atomoxetine-elevating drug-interaction risk, though it is not itself an SSRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — extends the CYP450 drug-drug-interaction framework (fluoxetine/paroxetine-induced CYP2D6 inhibition) into its specific effect on atomoxetine plasma concentrations and toxicity risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":1,"stem":"A genetically normal CYP2D6 metabolizer taking atomoxetine is started on paroxetine, a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor, for comorbid anxiety. What is the clinical significance of this combination in terms of the patient's effective metabolizer phenotype?","choices":{"A":"No change, as metabolizer status is fixed by CYP2D6 genotype","B":"Paroxetine levels rise instead, as atomoxetine inhibits CYP2D6","C":"It phenocopies a poor metabolizer, so atomoxetine levels rise","D":"Clearance shifts to CYP3A4, so atomoxetine levels stay the same","E":"Atomoxetine induces CYP2D6, offsetting paroxetine's inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because atomoxetine is metabolized principally through CYP2D6, and CYP2D6 inhibitors such as paroxetine can raise atomoxetine levels, a genetically normal metabolizer taking a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor is pharmacologically converted into a phenocopy of a poor metabolizer, reaching the same elevated, potentially toxic atomoxetine concentrations that a genetic poor metabolizer would reach — exactly this clinical significance. Metabolizer status is not fixed by genotype: a potent inhibitor can turn a normal metabolizer into a functional poor metabolizer, which is the whole point of phenoconversion. Atomoxetine is the CYP2D6 substrate in this pair; it neither inhibits nor induces the enzyme to any clinically meaningful degree. Because CYP2D6 is its principal pathway, no alternative route compensates, so levels rise rather than stay the same.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Concurrent CYP2D6-inhibitor therapy can pharmacologically phenocopy a poor metabolizer even in a genetically normal patient, a key concept for predicting toxicity risk with atomoxetine and other CYP2D6-substrate drugs. COMLEX: This phenocopying principle applies broadly across CYP2D6-metabolized psychotropic medications whenever a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor is co-administered.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — extends the CYP450 drug-drug-interaction framework (fluoxetine/paroxetine-induced CYP2D6 inhibition) into its specific effect on atomoxetine plasma concentrations and toxicity risk","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Atomoxetine is the CYP2D6 subs","D":"Because CYP2D6 is its principa"}},{"id":"sdl37_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has a history of poor social relatedness, aberrant language with echolalia, stereotyped phrases, and stereotyped repetitive behaviors since infancy, with intact adaptive functioning apart from social impairment. There is no family history of psychotic disorders, and no hallucinations or delusions are present. Which diagnosis best fits, and what age-of-onset feature most strongly supports it over its key psychotic differential?","choices":{"A":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia, since it typically begins before age 3","B":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia, since its onset peaks between ages 3 and 5","C":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia, since ASD is rarely evident before age 5","D":"ASD, since childhood-onset schizophrenia is almost unseen before age 5","E":"ASD, since schizophrenia first appears in adulthood, after age 18"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This 3-year-old's early developmental-period onset, poor social relatedness with aberrant language/echolalia/stereotyped phrases, stereotyped repetitive behaviors, and intact adaptive functioning outside the social domain fit ASD, and his age strongly supports this over childhood-onset schizophrenia, since schizophrenia is rare in children younger than 12 years and almost nonexistent before age 5 — exactly this age-based distinguishing feature. Childhood-onset schizophrenia does not typically begin before age 3 or peak between 3 and 5; it is rare before 12 and almost unseen before 5. ASD, by contrast, is usually evident in the second year of life, so early onset is expected in ASD. Schizophrenia can begin in childhood, rarely, so onset after 18 is not the distinguishing fact; the near absence of cases before age 5 is.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ASD's early developmental-period onset versus childhood-onset schizophrenia's rarity before age 5 (and near-nonexistence before puberty) is a key distinguishing feature, alongside family history of schizophrenia (increased in schizophrenia, not increased in ASD) and the presence of hallucinations/delusions (schizophrenia) versus stereotyped language/behavior (ASD). COMLEX: Prenatal and perinatal complications are increased in ASD but not in childhood-onset schizophrenia, another distinguishing feature from the same comparison table.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — extends the psychotic-spectrum-disorder timeline-based differentiation framework into using age-of-onset, family history, and symptom pattern to distinguish ASD from its closest psychotic differential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia ","B":"Childhood-onset schizophrenia ","C":"ASD, by contrast, is usually e","E":"Schizophrenia can begin in chi"}},{"id":"sdl37_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl has difficulty with conventional greetings, taking turns in conversation, and understanding nonverbal listener cues, occurring at higher frequency in her extended family, several of whom have ASD. She has no stereotyped motor movements, no rigid adherence to routines, and no restricted or fixed interests. Which diagnosis fits, and what specific feature rules out ASD despite the strong family history?","choices":{"A":"Language disorder, as her vocabulary and sentence structure are impaired","B":"Social pragmatic communication disorder, given no stereotypies or rigid routines","C":"Social pragmatic communication disorder, as ASD runs strongly in her extended family","D":"Language disorder, as she lacks stereotypies and rigid routines","E":"Language disorder, as her difficulty centers on conversational turn-taking"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This girl's pattern of pragmatic language difficulty without any stereotyped motor movements, rigid routines, or restricted/fixed interests fits social (pragmatic) communication disorder, which occurs with higher frequency in relatives of individuals with ASD but, unlike ASD, does not include restricted or repetitive behaviors and interests — exactly this required distinguishing absence. The positive family history raises the likelihood of both conditions, so it cannot be what separates them; the absence of restricted, repetitive behaviors does. Language disorder is a deficit in the structure of language — vocabulary, grammar, sentence formation — which is not described here; her difficulty is in the social use of language, the defining feature of social (pragmatic) communication disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Social (pragmatic) communication disorder's elevated rate in ASD relatives makes family history alone an unreliable diagnostic cue; the presence versus absence of restricted/repetitive behaviors remains the required distinguishing criterion. COMLEX: This distinction has direct treatment implications, since social (pragmatic) communication disorder does not respond to interventions specifically targeting ASD's repetitive-behavior symptom cluster.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — extends the psychotic-spectrum symptom-domain-based diagnostic framework into requiring the specific restricted/repetitive-behavior criterion, not merely family history, to differentiate ASD from social (pragmatic) communication disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Language disorder is a deficit","C":"The positive family history ra","D":"Language disorder is a deficit","E":"Language disorder is a deficit"}},{"id":"sdl37_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical manifestations, diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnoses of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) by evaluating the two core DSM-5 domains assessing \"Theory of Mind\" impairments, and distinguishing ASD from Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder, Childhood-Onset Schizophrenia, and primary language disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has significant expressive and receptive language delay. His parents ask whether this alone confirms a diagnosis of ASD, noting that some children with ASD also show language delay. Which response most accurately reflects the relationship between language disorder and ASD per DSM-5?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because aberrant language is one of the two core DSM-5 domains of ASD","B":"No, because language delay rules out ASD, which spares language development","C":"No, because DSM-5 made aberrant language an associated, not core, feature","D":"Yes, provided the child had spoken no single words by 12 months of age","E":"No, because language disorder and ASD cannot be diagnosed together"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Language delay alone is not diagnostic of ASD, since DSM-5 moved aberrant language development and usage out of the core diagnostic criteria and into an associated feature present in some, not all, individuals with ASD, based on sibling studies suggesting symptom domains transmit separately — exactly this accurate framing for this boy's parents. The two core DSM-5 domains are social communication and restricted, repetitive behavior; aberrant language is no longer one of them. Language delay neither rules out ASD nor bars a dual diagnosis — some children with language disorders also have ASD features, which is why careful differentiation is needed. DSM-5 sets no 12-month word-count threshold for ASD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5 moved aberrant language development/usage from a core ASD diagnostic criterion to an associated feature present in only some individuals, meaning language delay alone (without documented social communication deficits and restricted/repetitive behaviors) does not establish an ASD diagnosis. COMLEX: Some children with language disorders also have ASD features, requiring careful comparison to differentiate the two rather than assuming either automatic diagnosis or automatic exclusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 2 — extends the DSM-5 communication-disorder secondary-organic-cause-exclusion framework (ruling out ASD before diagnosing a primary communication disorder) into clarifying that language delay alone, conversely, does not establish an ASD diagnosis either","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The two core DSM-5 domains are","E":"Language delay neither rules o"}},{"id":"sdl37_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":2,"stem":"A longitudinal imaging study follows infants who later were diagnosed with ASD. Their head circumferences were normal at birth. By age 4 years, what proportion of these children had larger brain volumes than controls, and what proportion specifically met criteria for macrocephaly?","choices":{"A":"90 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, and 10 percent met criteria for macrocephaly","B":"60 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, and 37 percent met criteria for macrocephaly","C":"60 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, and 10 percent met criteria for macrocephaly","D":"37 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, and 5 percent met criteria for macrocephaly","E":"90 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, and 37 percent met criteria for macrocephaly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In infants who later had ASD with normal neonatal head circumferences, by 4 years, 90 percent had larger brain volumes than controls, with 37 percent of the disordered group meeting criteria for macrocephaly — exactly this pairing, part of the broader finding that 15 to 20 percent of children with ASD develop macrocephaly by about age 5. Enlargement was nearly universal in this cohort, while a smaller subset, a little over a third, crossed the threshold for frank macrocephaly; the other pairings misstate one figure or both. Source: Kaplan & Sadock Ch 2 (course reading for SDL 34–37, PDF p145).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dynamic pattern of increased total brain volume in early ASD (90 percent by age 4, with 37 percent meeting macrocephaly criteria), despite normal neonatal head circumference, supports the \"critical period\" hypothesis of disrupted brain plasticity in ASD pathogenesis. COMLEX: In contrast, structural MRI studies of children with ASD ages 5 to 16 did not find mean total brain volume increased, suggesting this volume difference is most prominent in early development.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome-with-ID framework's attention to structural/physical phenotype markers into ASD's own dynamic early-childhood brain-volume and macrocephaly neuroimaging biomarker","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enlargement was nearly univers","B":"Enlargement was nearly univers","C":"Enlargement was nearly univers","D":"Enlargement was nearly univers"}},{"id":"sdl37_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old with ASD is evaluated for a comorbid neurologic condition after his parents report episodes concerning for seizures. What proportion of individuals with ASD have grand mal seizures at some point, and what proportion show ventricular enlargement on CT scan?","choices":{"A":"4 to 32 percent have grand mal seizures, and 2 to 5 percent show ventricular enlargement","B":"1 to 2 percent have grand mal seizures, and 20 to 25 percent show ventricular enlargement","C":"1 to 2 percent have grand mal seizures, and 2 to 5 percent show ventricular enlargement","D":"4 to 32 percent have grand mal seizures, and 20 to 25 percent show ventricular enlargement","E":"50 to 60 percent have grand mal seizures, and 60 to 70 percent show ventricular enlargement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EEG abnormalities and seizure disorders occur with higher-than-expected frequency in individuals with ASD: 4 to 32 percent have grand mal seizures at some time, and about 20 to 25 percent show ventricular enlargement on CT scans — exactly this pairing of figures. One to two percent is close to general-population rates, which ASD clearly exceeds, and half or more overstates both findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ASD's comorbid neurologic findings include a wide 4-to-32-percent range for grand mal seizures and 20-to-25-percent ventricular enlargement on CT, both occurring with higher-than-expected frequency versus the general population. COMLEX: Various EEG abnormalities occur in 10 to 83 percent of children with autistic disorder, though no single EEG finding is specific to the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome-with-ID framework's attention to comorbid neurologic complications (e.g., seizures in Rett syndrome) into ASD's own elevated grand mal seizure and ventricular enlargement comorbidity rates","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"One to two percent is close to","C":"One to two percent is close to","E":"One to two percent is close to"}},{"id":"sdl37_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis and associated genetic syndromes of Autism Spectrum Disorder by analyzing polygenic inheritance patterns, high-risk genetic conditions (Fragile X syndrome and Tuberous Sclerosis), structural neuroimaging findings, biochemical biomarkers (platelet hyperserotonemia), and comorbid neurologic disorders such as epilepsy.","batch":2,"stem":"Two twin studies of ASD found markedly different concordance rates: one found 36 percent in monozygotic twins versus 0 percent in dizygotic twins, while the other found about 96 percent in monozygotic twins versus about 27 percent in dizygotic twins. Both studies, taken together, most strongly support which conclusion about ASD's genetic architecture?","choices":{"A":"A significant heritable contribution that is incompletely penetrant and polygenic","B":"A purely environmental etiology shaped by prenatal and perinatal exposures","C":"A single-gene, fully penetrant autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","D":"An inheritance pattern identical to Fragile X syndrome's X-linked mechanism","E":"A wholly stochastic developmental process with no genetic architecture"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Family and twin studies suggest ASD has a significant heritable contribution that does not appear to be fully penetrant, with expression in most cases dependent on multiple genes, consistent with polygenic rather than single-gene inheritance — exactly the conclusion these two twin studies, despite their differing exact concordance figures, both support (monozygotic concordance consistently well above dizygotic concordance in both studies, but not reaching 100 percent in either). A purely environmental etiology with no heritable contribution is contradicted by the consistently higher monozygotic-versus-dizygotic concordance found in both studies. A single-gene, fully penetrant autosomal dominant pattern is contradicted by the incomplete (well below 100 percent) monozygotic concordance in both studies; full penetrance would predict near-100-percent monozygotic concordance. ASD's inheritance is not identical to Fragile X's X-linked mechanism in all cases; only up to 15 percent of ASD cases are associated with a known genetic mutation like Fragile X, with most cases being polygenic. A wholly stochastic process with no genetic architecture is contradicted by monozygotic concordance far exceeding dizygotic concordance in both studies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Twin studies showing high but incomplete monozygotic concordance (well above dizygotic concordance, but not 100 percent) are the classic evidence pattern for significant but incompletely penetrant, polygenic heritability, distinguishing ASD's typical genetic architecture from single-gene disorders like Fragile X syndrome. COMLEX: High rates of cognitive impairment in the nonautistic monozygotic co-twin with perinatal complications suggest gene-environment interaction also contributes to ASD's variable expression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 1 — extends the genetic/metabolic-syndrome inheritance-pattern framework (X-linked recessive Fragile X, autosomal dominant tuberous sclerosis) into contrasting idiopathic ASD's polygenic, incompletely penetrant twin-study evidence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old with ASD and severe self-injurious behavior is started on risperidone 0.5 mg, later titrated to 1.5 mg, with good response. Six months later, the family decides to discontinue the medication due to improvement. What does the literature on risperidone discontinuation in ASD predict will happen?","choices":{"A":"Permanent remission with no relapse risk after discontinuation","B":"A gradual, months-long symptom return in the poorest responders","C":"No published data on risperidone discontinuation outcomes in ASD","D":"Symptom return confined to abruptly discontinued cases","E":"A rapid return of symptoms in good responders after discontinuation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Continuation studies of risperidone in the treatment of irritability in ASD found persistent efficacy and tolerability over 6 months, but with a rapid return of symptoms in good responders when discontinuing the risperidone — exactly this described outcome pattern, directly relevant to this family's discontinuation decision. Permanent remission with no relapse risk contradicts the specifically described rapid symptom return in good responders upon discontinuation. A gradual, months-long return specifically in poor responders misstates the finding, which describes rapid return specifically in GOOD responders, not a gradual course in poor responders. Published continuation-study data DO exist on this exact outcome; this is not an unstudied question. The rapid return was reported for good responders generally, not only for abruptly discontinued cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Risperidone's persistent 6-month efficacy/tolerability alongside rapid symptom return upon discontinuation in good responders is an important counseling point before families elect to stop treatment for ASD-related irritability. COMLEX: This rapid-relapse pattern supports careful, individualized discussion of long-term treatment duration rather than assuming sustained response implies safe discontinuation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — extends the antipsychotic-selection-by-indication framework into risperidone's specific discontinuation/relapse pattern when used for its autism-related-irritability indication","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A gradual, months-long return ","D":"The rapid return was reported "}},{"id":"sdl37_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old with ASD begins an intensive, manualized intervention utilizing techniques derived from applied behavior analysis, provided one-on-one for many hours per week, with a therapist and child practicing specific social skills, language usage, and target play skills with reinforcement for accomplishments. Which model does this describe, and how does it differ from the Early Start Denver Model?","choices":{"A":"The Early Start Denver Model, which is intensive and one-on-one, versus the UCLA/Lovaas model's naturalistic, parent-co-therapist approach","B":"TEACCH, a structured-teaching educational intervention using visual supports, not an intensive one-on-one applied-behavior-analysis model","C":"The UCLA/Lovaas-based model, which is intensive and one-on-one, versus ESDM's naturalistic, parent-co-therapist approach","D":"Pivotal Response Training, a parent-training approach targeting pivotal social behaviors for natural generalization, not an intensive one-on-one applied-behavior-analysis model","E":"These two models are functionally identical with no meaningful distinction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This intensive, manualized, one-on-one intervention using applied behavior analysis techniques for many hours per week describes the UCLA/Lovaas-based model, which differs from ESDM's naturalistic-setting, parent-as-co-therapist approach integrated into the child's daily routine — exactly this described model and its key point of contrast. The Early Start Denver Model is not the intensive, one-on-one clinical model described here; it is specifically the naturalistic, parent-co-therapist alternative, making this option's pairing reversed. TEACCH is a structured-teaching educational intervention using visual supports, not this intensive one-on-one behavior-analysis model. Pivotal Response Training is a parent-training approach targeting pivotal social behaviors for natural generalization, not this intensive one-on-one clinical model. These two models are NOT functionally identical; their setting (clinical one-on-one versus naturalistic daily-routine) and format represent a genuine, clinically relevant distinction in early intensive ASD intervention approaches.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The UCLA/Lovaas model's intensive, one-on-one, manualized applied-behavior-analysis format contrasts with ESDM's naturalistic, parent-co-therapist, daily-routine-integrated approach — both are early intensive behavioral/developmental interventions but differ meaningfully in setting and delivery format. COMLEX: Both models fall under the broader evidence base of five RCTs showing early intensive comprehensive behavioral interventions improve language, social interaction, and educational achievement in children ages 2 to 5.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 5 — extends the treatment-selection-by-mechanism-and-format framework into distinguishing ASD's two major early intensive behavioral intervention models by clinical setting and delivery format","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The Early Start Denver Model i"}},{"id":"sdl37_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Formulate an evidence-based intervention and management plan for Autism Spectrum Disorder by integrating early intensive behavioral and developmental approaches (e.g., Applied Behavior Analysis/ESDM) and selecting FDA-approved second-generation antipsychotics (risperidone or aripiprazole) for the targeted management of severe irritability, aggression, or self-injurious behavior.","batch":2,"stem":"Two children with ASD both have severe irritability requiring pharmacotherapy. One child's family is most concerned about avoiding significant weight gain and metabolic side effects; the other child's family is most concerned about avoiding akathisia and insomnia. Based on their relative side-effect profiles, which medication choice best matches each family's priority?","choices":{"A":"Risperidone for the first family, given its milder weight gain; aripiprazole for the second","B":"Aripiprazole for the first family, given its milder weight gain; risperidone for the second","C":"Aripiprazole for both families, given its freedom from adverse effects","D":"Risperidone for both families, given its freedom from adverse effects","E":"Either medication for either family, their side-effect profiles being equivalent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aripiprazole's weight gain, while still a moderate adverse event (approximately 1.3 to 1.5 kg over an 8-week study), is less pronounced than risperidone's more prominent weight gain and metabolic effects (hyperglycemia, dyslipidemia, prolactin elevation), making it the better fit for the first family's priority; risperidone's described adverse-effect profile centers on weight gain/metabolic/prolactin effects and fatigue/drowsiness/dizziness/drooling, not akathisia or insomnia, which are instead listed among aripiprazole's main side effects, making risperidone the better fit for the second family's priority — exactly this profile-matched pairing. The reversed pairing (risperidone for weight-gain-averse, aripiprazole for akathisia/insomnia-averse) inverts the actual comparative side-effect profiles described. Aripiprazole does have described adverse effects (sedation, dizziness, insomnia, akathisia, nausea, vomiting, and still-present though less pronounced weight gain), contradicting a \"no adverse effects of any kind\" characterization. Risperidone likewise has a well-described adverse-effect profile (weight gain, metabolic effects, prolactin elevation, orthostatic hypotension risk), also contradicting \"no adverse effects of any kind.\" The two medications' side-effect profiles DO differ meaningfully enough to inform individualized choice, as this vignette's scenario demonstrates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Individualizing between risperidone and aripiprazole for ASD-related irritability can be guided by their differing comparative side-effect emphases — risperidone's more prominent weight gain/metabolic/prolactin profile versus aripiprazole's akathisia/insomnia profile with comparatively less pronounced weight gain. COMLEX: Olanzapine has also been studied for ASD-related irritability with a trend toward positive response, but with even more pronounced weight gain (approximately 3.5 kg) than either risperidone or aripiprazole.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — extends the antipsychotic-selection-by-side-effect-profile framework into individualizing between risperidone and aripiprazole for ASD-related irritability based on each family's specific adverse-effect priorities","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The reversed pairing (risperid","C":"Aripiprazole does have describ","D":"Risperidone likewise has a wel","E":"The two medications' side-effe"}},{"id":"sdl37_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old girl in fourth grade is evaluated because her grades have fallen. Teachers describe her as quiet and dreamy, making careless errors, losing materials, and rarely finishing assignments; her parents note the same at home since about age 7. She has 7 inattentive symptoms and 2 hyperactive-impulsive symptoms and no disruptive behavior, and earlier grades went smoothly. Which statement best describes her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Combined presentation, which is usually recognized earliest in girls","B":"Hyperactive-impulsive presentation, the subtype most typical of girls","C":"Inattentive presentation, often recognized later as schoolwork rises","D":"Not ADHD, since her symptoms first drew attention after the age of 9","E":"Not ADHD, since girls rarely meet ADHD criteria before adolescence"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The course ADHD review notes that more boys have ADHD overall but the inattentive subtype is more common in girls, and that patients with the primarily inattentive type may not present until academic requirements become greater, for example by the fourth grade, whereas hyperactive-impulsive and combined types come to attention earlier because their behavior is disruptive. With 7 inattentive and only 2 hyperactive-impulsive symptoms in two settings since age 7, she fits the predominantly inattentive presentation. The combined presentation requires the symptom threshold in both domains. The hyperactive-impulsive presentation does not fit her symptom count. DSM-5 requires several symptoms before age 12, not first recognition before 9. Girls are not excluded before adolescence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADHD needs 6 or more symptoms in a domain (5 or more at age 17 and older), several present before age 12, in 2 or more settings. COMLEX: consider ADHD, inattentive presentation, in quiet girls whose school performance slips as work becomes more demanding, and screen for coexisting learning disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 5 — first-line pharmacotherapy and monitoring once ADHD is confirmed","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The combined presentation requ","E":"Girls are not excluded before "}},{"id":"sdl37_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 7 symptoms of inattention and 6 symptoms of hyperactivity/impulsivity, both present for over a year, causing impairment at school and at home, with several symptoms present before age 12. Which DSM-5 ADHD presentation specifier applies, and how many total settings and symptom domains does this satisfy?","choices":{"A":"Combined presentation, meeting the 6-symptom threshold in both domains","B":"Predominantly inattentive presentation, meeting threshold in one domain","C":"Predominantly hyperactive presentation, meeting threshold in one domain","D":"No specifier, since DSM-5 no longer uses ADHD presentation specifiers","E":"Combined presentation, requiring onset of symptoms before age 7"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This boy meets the 6-symptom threshold in BOTH the inattentive domain (7 symptoms) and the hyperactive/impulsive domain (6 symptoms), which qualifies him for DSM-5's combined presentation specifier, with impairment documented across 2 settings (school and home) — exactly this specifier and count. Predominantly inattentive presentation would apply if only the inattentive domain met threshold while hyperactive/impulsive symptoms did not, which is not the case here since both domains independently meet the 6-symptom threshold. Predominantly hyperactive/impulsive presentation would similarly apply only if hyperactive/impulsive symptoms met threshold while inattentive symptoms did not, also not the case here. DSM-5 DOES use three presentation specifiers (combined, predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive/impulsive), replacing the DSM-IV subtypes; these specifiers were not eliminated. The combined specifier is correct, but DSM-5 requires several symptoms before age 12, not age 7 (the DSM-IV rule).","boardPrep":"Step 1: DSM-5's combined presentation specifier requires meeting the symptom-count threshold in BOTH the inattentive and hyperactive/impulsive domains simultaneously, replacing DSM-IV's separate subtype system with three specifiers that largely denote the same groups. COMLEX: This same 9-year-old's presentation additionally requires exclusion of another mental disorder better explaining the symptoms, per DSM-5 criterion E.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — extends the stimulant-related diagnostic-precision framework into correctly applying DSM-5's combined-presentation specifier when a child meets threshold in both symptom domains across the required number of settings","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Predominantly inattentive pres","C":"Predominantly hyperactive/impu","D":"DSM-5 DOES use three presentat"}},{"id":"sdl37_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Differentiate the clinical presentations, age-dependent diagnostic criteria, and underlying neurobiology of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by comparing symptom thresholds across the lifespan (>6 inattentive or hyperactive/impulsive symptoms in children vs. >5 in adolescents and adults), verifying cross-setting pervasiveness (≥ 2 settings) with onset prior to age 12, and mapping executive dysfunction to prefrontal cortical disinhibition and dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation.","batch":2,"stem":"Neuroimaging and neurochemical studies of ADHD implicate the prefrontal cortex's high dopamine utilization and its reciprocal connections with other brain regions. Which specific cognitive/behavioral functions are mediated by these prefrontal-cortex reciprocal connections, most directly explaining ADHD's core executive dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Episodic memory consolidation, spatial navigation, and contextual recall","B":"Primary visual processing, auditory processing, and pattern recognition","C":"Basic motor reflex arcs, proprioception, and postural control","D":"Attention, inhibition, decision-making, working memory, and vigilance","E":"Olfactory discrimination, taste perception, and appetite regulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The prefrontal cortex's high utilization of dopamine and its reciprocal connections with other brain regions are involved in attention, inhibition, decision-making, response inhibition, working memory, and vigilance — exactly these functions, directly explaining why prefrontal dopaminergic/noradrenergic dysregulation produces ADHD's hallmark executive dysfunction symptoms (inattention, impulsivity, poor self-regulation). Long-term episodic memory consolidation and spatial navigation are more centrally hippocampal functions, not the specifically described prefrontal-cortex reciprocal-connection functions implicated in ADHD. Primary visual and auditory sensory processing are occipital and temporal lobe functions respectively, not the prefrontal executive functions described here. Basic motor reflex arcs and proprioception are brainstem/spinal cord and somatosensory cortex functions, not prefrontal executive functions. Olfactory discrimination and taste perception are olfactory bulb/insular cortex functions, unrelated to the prefrontal executive-function domains described in ADHD neurobiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The prefrontal cortex's role in attention, inhibition, decision-making, response inhibition, working memory, and vigilance directly maps onto ADHD's core symptom clusters, providing the neuroanatomic rationale for why dopamine/norepinephrine-targeting stimulants improve these specific executive functions. COMLEX: This same executive-function framework explains why atomoxetine's prefrontal-selective norepinephrine-transporter-inhibition mechanism can improve ADHD symptoms despite minimal action in the mesolimbic reward pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — extends the stimulant dopamine/serotonin neurochemistry framework into specifying the exact prefrontal-cortex-mediated executive functions disrupted by ADHD's dopaminergic/noradrenergic circuit dysregulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old is newly diagnosed with ADHD. His family history includes a maternal uncle who died suddenly at age 30 of an undiagnosed cardiac arrhythmia. His personal history and physical exam are otherwise unremarkable. What is the recommended next step before initiating a stimulant, and what should be evaluated at follow-up once treatment begins?","choices":{"A":"Start the stimulant with no cardiac workup, then monitor academic performance","B":"Consider ECG and cardiology referral, then begin treatment with no further monitoring","C":"Avoid all pharmacotherapy permanently regardless of any workup results","D":"Start a non-stimulant, any family cardiac history ruling out stimulants","E":"Consider ECG and cardiology referral, then monitor vital signs at follow-up"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This child's family history of sudden cardiac death in a close relative is specifically one of the red-flag features for which national guidelines recommend considering ECG and/or cardiology referral before initiating a psychostimulant; once treatment begins, height, weight, heart rate, blood pressure, symptoms, mood, and treatment adherence should be monitored at follow-up visits — exactly this two-part recommended approach. Initiating immediately with no cardiac workup, then monitoring only academic performance, omits both the recommended pre-treatment cardiac evaluation and the recommended ongoing vitals monitoring. Considering ECG/cardiology referral is correct, but stopping all monitoring once treatment begins contradicts the specifically recommended ongoing follow-up vitals monitoring. Avoiding all pharmacotherapy permanently regardless of workup results is not the recommended approach; the guideline is to evaluate further (ECG/cardiology), not to categorically avoid treatment regardless of findings. A family cardiac history does not mandate avoiding all stimulants outright; it specifically triggers a recommendation for further cardiac evaluation before the treatment decision, not an automatic stimulant contraindication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A family history of sudden cardiac death is one of the specific red-flag indications for pre-treatment ECG/cardiology referral before ADHD stimulant initiation, with ongoing vitals monitoring (height, weight, heart rate, blood pressure) continuing throughout treatment regardless of the pre-treatment workup outcome. COMLEX: Monthly follow-up visits may be needed until dosing is optimized, then at least every 3 months during the first year of stimulant treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 4 — extends the mechanism-matched-pharmacotherapy-with-appropriate-baseline-evaluation framework into ADHD's specific cardiac-risk-based pre-treatment screening and ongoing vitals-monitoring protocol","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Considering ECG/cardiology ref","D":"A family cardiac history does "}},{"id":"sdl37_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has marked hyperactivity, impulsivity, and inattention at home and in preschool that exceed what is expected for his age, confirmed by validated parent and teacher rating scales. His symptoms are moderate, and his parents ask what the first step in treatment should be. Which approach is recommended?","choices":{"A":"Methylphenidate as first-line therapy, as for school-age children","B":"Behavioral therapy such as parent training before any medication","C":"Atomoxetine as first-line, since stimulants are not used before 6","D":"No treatment until age 6, since ADHD cannot be diagnosed earlier","E":"Guanfacine as first-line, given its long half-life and low sedation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The course ADHD review states that treatment of children younger than 6 years should begin with behavioral therapy, such as parent training, classroom management, and peer interventions, with medication considered only if symptoms are moderate to severe and do not respond; from age 6 onward, therapy should start with medication. Methylphenidate first is the approach for children 6 and older, though it is the best-supported medication if a preschooler later needs one. Atomoxetine is a second-line agent, and stimulants are not barred before age 6. ADHD cannot be reliably diagnosed before age 4, but at 4 years with validated rating scales the diagnosis can be made. Guanfacine is a second-line alpha-2 agonist used as an alternative or adjunct, not first-line in preschoolers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: psychostimulants are first-line for ADHD from age 6; about 70 percent respond to the first stimulant and 90 to 95 percent to a second. COMLEX: start preschoolers with parent training and behavioral management, and record height, weight, heart rate, and blood pressure at every medication follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 4 — age-dependent ADHD diagnostic criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Formulate an evidence-based treatment and monitoring strategy for Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) by conducting baseline pre-treatment screening (vitals and cardiac risk evaluation) and selecting mechanism-matched pharmacotherapy (first-line dopamine/norepinephrine stimulants vs. non-stimulants like atomoxetine or alpha2-adrenergic agonists).","batch":2,"stem":"A child with ADHD and comorbid Tourette syndrome needs pharmacotherapy. The family is concerned that stimulants will worsen his tics. Based on current evidence, what is the most accurate counseling point, and what treatment options exist if tics do worsen?","choices":{"A":"Small stimulant doses do not clearly worsen tics, and augmentation can help","B":"Stimulants are contraindicated in all patients with comorbid tic disorders","C":"Stimulants definitively cure comorbid tic disorders in all patients","D":"Small stimulant doses do not clearly worsen tics, and no augmentation options exist","E":"Non-stimulants are the agents that have been studied in comorbid tic disorders"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The previous dictum that sympathomimetics worsen tics and should therefore be avoided in comorbid ADHD and tic disorders has been questioned; small dosages of sympathomimetics do not appear to cause an increase in tic frequency/severity, though methylphenidate may worsen tics in about one-third of persons in some reports, and in severe cases, augmentation with risperidone, clonidine, or guanfacine is available — exactly this nuanced, evidence-based counseling point. Stimulants are not absolutely contraindicated in all patients with comorbid tic disorders; the current evidence specifically supports an individualized, cautious-but-not-prohibitive approach rather than an absolute contraindication. Stimulants do not definitively cure comorbid tic disorders; they are ADHD treatments, and their effect on tics specifically requires individualized monitoring, not a curative claim. Small stimulant doses not clearly worsening tics is accurate, but augmentation options DO exist (risperidone, clonidine, guanfacine) if tics do worsen, contradicting a no-options characterization. Stimulants specifically HAVE been studied, with data (including the one-third-of-persons tic-worsening figure) directly informing this counseling point, not merely non-stimulants alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The historical stimulant-avoidance dictum in comorbid ADHD/tic disorders has been revised; small doses do not clearly worsen tics, though methylphenidate-induced tics occur in about a third of cases (usually self-limited, occasionally augmentation-requiring), with risperidone, clonidine, or guanfacine available for augmentation if needed. COMLEX: Methylphenidate-induced tics fall into two patterns — those resolving immediately as the dose metabolizes, and a smaller group in whom tics persist for months but eventually resolve spontaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 34 Obj 4 — extends the Tourette-disorder mechanism-matched-pharmacotherapy framework (alpha2-adrenergic agonists, D2 antagonists) into the specific evidence-based counseling and augmentation approach for comorbid ADHD stimulant treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Small stimulant doses not clea"}},{"id":"sdl37_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"Atomoxetine's presynaptic NET inhibition leads to increased norepinephrine extracellular levels occurring mainly in one brain region, but not in the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways up to the nucleus accumbens. Which brain region is this, and what clinical property results from this regional selectivity?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus accumbens itself, resulting in significant abuse potential through mesolimbic reward pathway activation","B":"The prefrontal cortex, sparing the nucleus accumbens and resulting in minimal abuse or dependence potential","C":"The cerebellum, resulting in motor coordination side effects as the primary clinical concern","D":"The hippocampus, resulting in memory enhancement as the primary clinical effect of this mechanism","E":"The brainstem reticular activating system, resulting in significant sedation as the primary effect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Atomoxetine's presynaptic NET inhibition leads to increased norepinephrine extracellular levels occurring mainly in the prefrontal cortex, but not in the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways up to the nucleus accumbens, and therefore atomoxetine presents minimal abuse or dependence potential — exactly this region and resulting clinical property. The nucleus accumbens is specifically the region where atomoxetine's effect is described as NOT occurring, the opposite of where its action is concentrated, and reward-pathway-sparing is precisely why atomoxetine has minimal, not significant, abuse potential. The cerebellum is not the described region of atomoxetine's selective norepinephrine effect, and motor coordination side effects are not the described primary clinical property resulting from this mechanism. The hippocampus is not the described region either, and memory enhancement is not the primary clinical effect described as resulting from this prefrontal-selective mechanism. The brainstem reticular activating system is not the described region, and significant sedation is not the primary clinical property described resulting from this mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atomoxetine's prefrontal-selective norepinephrine-transporter inhibition, sparing the mesolimbic/mesocortical reward pathway and nucleus accumbens, explains both its minimal abuse potential and its distinct clinical role compared with stimulants, which do act more broadly across dopaminergic reward circuitry. COMLEX: This same prefrontal selectivity is consistent with atomoxetine also promoting increased dopamine concentrations specifically in the prefrontal cortex, likely via nonspecific dopamine reuptake modulation through the highly expressed NET in this region.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — extends the sympathomimetic mechanism/reward-pathway framework into explaining why atomoxetine's prefrontal-selective, nucleus-accumbens-sparing mechanism confers minimal abuse potential compared with classic stimulants","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"Beyond its effects on dopamine and norepinephrine transporters and presynaptic release, which additional pharmacologic action does dextroamphetamine have?","choices":{"A":"Weak inhibition of monoamine oxidase","B":"Potent inhibition of hepatic CYP2D6","C":"Direct alpha2-adrenergic receptor agonism","D":"Selective inhibition of the serotonin transporter","E":"Antagonism at presynaptic dopamine D2 autoreceptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amphetamines block the dopamine and norepinephrine transporters and drive presynaptic catecholamine efflux, and amphetamine is also a weak inhibitor of monoamine oxidase. Methylphenidate, by contrast, acts mainly by blocking the transporters. Dextroamphetamine is not a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor. Direct alpha2-adrenergic agonism is the mechanism of guanfacine and clonidine. Dextroamphetamine does not selectively inhibit the serotonin transporter; its effects center on dopamine and norepinephrine. It does not block presynaptic D2 autoreceptors; it raises synaptic dopamine through transporter effects and release instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Amphetamines block DAT/NET, promote presynaptic dopamine efflux, and weakly inhibit MAO; methylphenidate acts mainly by DAT/NET blockade. COMLEX: Amphetamine's weak MAO inhibition is one reason stimulants require caution when combined with MAO inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — extends the indirectly-acting-sympathomimetic mechanism framework into the additional weak catecholamine-reuptake-inhibition and MAO-inhibition actions shared by methylphenidate and dextroamphetamine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Compare the primary mechanisms of action and neurochemical targets of indirectly acting sympathomimetics (methylphenidate, amphetamines), modafinil, and atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old with ADHD is switched from immediate-release dextroamphetamine to lisdexamfetamine. His mother asks why the new drug lasts longer and is considered less prone to misuse even though it delivers the same active stimulant. Which property of lisdexamfetamine best explains this?","choices":{"A":"It blocks dopamine transporters without causing presynaptic release","B":"It acts mainly on alpha-1 receptors in place of dopamine pathways","C":"It is a 3-to-1 mix of dextro- and levo-isomers that offset each other","D":"It inhibits the norepinephrine transporter mainly in prefrontal cortex","E":"Red cell enzymes cleave off lysine to release dextroamphetamine slowly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock describe lisdexamfetamine as L-lysine coupled to dextroamphetamine, an amphetamine prodrug that becomes active when enzymes in red blood cells cleave off the lysine; the gradual release of dextroamphetamine extends its duration and reduces its abuse potential, and it is the only prodrug of its kind. Transporter blockade without release describes methylphenidate, whereas amphetamines also promote presynaptic release. Alpha-1 involvement is linked to modafinil's wake-promoting effect, not to lisdexamfetamine. The roughly 75 percent dextro- and 25 percent levoamphetamine mixture is Adderall; lisdexamfetamine is a single dextro-enantiomer. Selective norepinephrine transporter inhibition with prefrontal effects describes atomoxetine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: methylphenidate blocks DAT and NET; amphetamines block DAT and NET and also release monoamines; atomoxetine is a selective NET inhibitor; modafinil's wakefulness is blocked by the alpha-1 antagonist prazosin. COMLEX: in adolescents at risk of diversion, a long-acting or prodrug stimulant or a nonstimulant lowers misuse risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — sympathomimetic stimulant intoxication and toxidromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The roughly 75 percent dextro-"}},{"id":"sdl37_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old on short-acting methylphenidate three times daily develops a consistent pattern: irritability and increased hyperactivity in the late afternoon, worsening further in the evening, most pronounced right before his next scheduled dose or bedtime. His parents ask what is happening and how to address it. What should the clinician explain and recommend?","choices":{"A":"Stimulant-induced anorexia, managed by giving the dose with meals and supplements","B":"End-of-dose rebound, managed by overlapping doses or a long-acting form","C":"Stimulant-induced insomnia, managed by dosing earlier and using short-acting forms","D":"A paradoxical stimulant reaction, managed by immediate permanent discontinuation","E":"A tachyphylactic tolerance effect, managed by escalating the dose each month"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This pattern of worsening irritability and hyperactivity in the late afternoon and evening, most pronounced right before the next dose, is the classic timing pattern of rebound phenomena as a short-acting stimulant's effect wears off, managed by overlapping stimulant dosing or switching to a long-acting or combined long-/short-acting regimen — exactly this explanation and recommended management. This is not anorexia, which involves decreased appetite and weight loss, not this irritability/hyperactivity timing pattern, and the with-meals/caloric-supplement management would not address the described symptoms. This is not primarily insomnia, though poorly timed dosing can contribute to sleep difficulty; the specifically described late-afternoon/evening irritability/hyperactivity timing pattern is the hallmark of rebound, and the short-acting-only recommendation would actually worsen, not improve, this rebound pattern. This is not a paradoxical reaction requiring permanent discontinuation; rebound phenomena is a well-recognized, manageable adverse effect with established formulation-based management strategies, not a reason for abandoning stimulant treatment altogether. Tachyphylaxis would produce a gradual loss of effect across weeks rather than this fixed daily timing, and escalating the dose each month would not address a wear-off pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rebound phenomena's characteristic timing (symptom worsening as a short-acting dose wears off, before the next scheduled dose) distinguishes it from insomnia (sleep-onset difficulty) and anorexia (appetite/weight effects), each requiring distinct, sometimes opposite, formulation-based management strategies. COMLEX: Assessing exactly when in the dosing cycle symptoms worsen (peak-effect period versus withdrawal/wearing-off period) is the key diagnostic step separating rebound phenomena from other stimulant-related adverse effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — extends the side-effect-profile-to-mechanism-linking framework into correctly identifying rebound phenomena's characteristic dosing-cycle timing pattern and its specific long-acting/dose-overlap management approach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old started on short-acting methylphenidate 3 weeks ago reports feeling lightheaded when he stands up quickly at school. His attention has improved markedly. He drinks little during the day, his examination is otherwise normal, and his blood pressure is at his usual baseline in the office. According to standard guidance for managing common stimulant adverse effects, which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Give the stimulant with meals and add caloric-enhanced supplements","B":"Overlap the doses to cover the late-afternoon wearing-off period","C":"Assess whether the symptom peaks with the dose and reduce the dose","D":"Monitor blood pressure, encourage fluids, use a long-acting form","E":"Stop the stimulant and switch permanently to an antipsychotic agent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock's table for managing stimulant-induced adverse effects lists, for dizziness, monitoring blood pressure, encouraging fluid intake, and changing to a long-acting form, which fits this boy who drinks little and has a good clinical response. Giving the dose with meals and adding supplements is the strategy for anorexia, nausea, and weight loss. Overlapping doses is the strategy for rebound as the dose wears off. Assessing timing and reducing the dose is the approach to irritability. Stopping an effective stimulant for a manageable adverse effect is unnecessary, and antipsychotics have no role in stimulant-related dizziness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant adverse effects include appetite loss, abdominal pain, headache, insomnia, irritability, small rises in heart rate and blood pressure, and a small reduction in height velocity. COMLEX: most stimulant adverse effects are managed by adjusting timing, dose, or formulation rather than stopping treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 5 — baseline cardiovascular screening and vital-sign monitoring for stimulants","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Assessing timing and reducing ","E":"Stopping an effective stimulan"}},{"id":"sdl37_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Create appropriate medical management strategies (e.g., timing adjustments, formulation changes, or adjunctive medications) for common stimulant-induced adverse effects such as insomnia, anorexia, and rebound phenomena.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old on short-acting methylphenidate twice daily has become irritable and tearful, and his parents are unsure whether this happens about an hour after each dose or late in the afternoon. He has no history of a mood disorder, and his ADHD symptoms are well controlled. According to standard guidance for managing stimulant-induced irritability, what is the most appropriate first step?","choices":{"A":"Give each dose with meals and add calorie-enhanced supplements","B":"Stop the afternoon dose and give the morning dose even earlier","C":"Determine whether it peaks with a dose or as the dose wears off","D":"Add lithium at once as an adjunctive mood-stabilizing medication","E":"Switch to a transdermal patch applied at bedtime each night"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock's table for stimulant-induced irritability lists first assessing the timing of the symptom, during the peak or the withdrawal phase, then evaluating comorbid symptoms, reducing the dose, and considering adjunctive or alternative treatment. Irritability about an hour after a dose points to a peak effect that may call for a lower dose, whereas late-afternoon irritability suggests rebound managed by overlapping doses or a long-acting preparation, so timing determines the next step. Meals and supplements address anorexia and weight loss. Moving doses earlier and dropping afternoon doses is the approach to insomnia. Lithium is listed as a later adjunct, not a first step for a child without a mood disorder. A patch applied at bedtime would worsen sleep and does not address the question of timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kaplan's management table: anorexia → dose with meals; insomnia → dose earlier; dizziness → fluids and long-acting form; rebound → overlap doses or long-acting form; irritability → assess timing, then reduce dose. COMLEX: ask parents to record when symptoms occur relative to each dose before changing a stimulant regimen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 6 — methylphenidate's mechanism and short duration of action","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old has taken atomoxetine for ADHD for 5 months with good effect and no other medications. He now has dark urine, generalized itching, and yellow sclerae. ALT is 10 times the upper limit of normal, bilirubin is elevated, and viral hepatitis serologies are negative. His CYP2D6 genotype has never been tested. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Stop atomoxetine, since it can cause severe liver injury","B":"Halve the atomoxetine dose and recheck enzymes in 3 months","C":"Continue atomoxetine and order CYP2D6 genotyping first","D":"Switch to a once-daily dose of atomoxetine taken at night","E":"Continue atomoxetine and add ursodiol for the itching"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock report cases of severe liver injury in a small number of patients taking atomoxetine and state that the drug should be discontinued in patients with jaundice, such as yellowing of the skin or eyes or itching, or with laboratory evidence of liver injury. This boy has jaundice, pruritus, and a marked transaminase rise with no viral cause, so atomoxetine must be stopped. Lowering the dose and waiting months leaves him exposed to a drug that is injuring the liver. CYP2D6 genotyping explains differences in atomoxetine exposure, about fivefold higher in poor metabolizers, but it does not change the need to stop the drug once liver injury appears. Changing dose timing does not address hepatotoxicity. Treating the itch while continuing the drug ignores the cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: atomoxetine is a selective NET inhibitor metabolized by CYP2D6; adverse effects include appetite loss, abdominal discomfort, mild rises in heart rate and blood pressure and rare severe liver injury; it is contraindicated with or within 2 weeks of an MAOI and in narrow-angle glaucoma. COMLEX: teach families to report dark urine, jaundice, or itching on atomoxetine right away.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 6 — atomoxetine's mechanism as a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lowering the dose and waiting ","C":"CYP2D6 genotyping explains dif","D":"Changing dose timing does not ","E":"Treating the itch while contin"}},{"id":"sdl37_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"Genetic testing reveals a patient starting atomoxetine is a documented CYP2D6 poor metabolizer, with no other medications on board. How should this genetic finding, on its own, most appropriately influence initial atomoxetine dosing and monitoring, given that poor metabolizers reach a fivefold higher AUC and Cmax than extensive metabolizers?","choices":{"A":"Anticipate unchanged plasma concentrations, supporting the usual monitoring","B":"Avoid atomoxetine entirely in poor metabolizers, whatever the clinical response","C":"Anticipate substantially higher plasma concentrations, supporting closer monitoring","D":"Anticipate substantially lower plasma concentrations, supporting a dose increase","E":"Anticipate an effect on concentrations when a second inhibitor is later added"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because atomoxetine is metabolized principally through CYP2D6, a documented poor metabolizer will reach approximately fivefold higher AUC and Cmax than an extensive metabolizer at any given dose, so this genetic finding alone should prompt anticipating substantially higher plasma concentrations at standard doses and closer monitoring for dose-related adverse effects — exactly this appropriate clinical response. CYP2D6 genotype DOES have an established, substantial (fivefold) effect on atomoxetine levels; no dosing/monitoring adjustment being warranted directly contradicts this well-established pharmacokinetic relationship. Atomoxetine is not described as requiring complete avoidance in all poor metabolizers regardless of clinical response; rather, closer monitoring and dose consideration, not blanket avoidance, is the appropriate approach. Poor metabolizer status predicts HIGHER, not lower, plasma concentrations; a dose-increase recommendation based on a lower-concentration premise is the opposite of the correct clinical implication. This genetic finding is directly significant even in atomoxetine monotherapy, since the patient's own poor-metabolizer genotype alone (independent of any added CYP2D6 inhibitor) already produces the fivefold elevated-concentration effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A documented CYP2D6 poor-metabolizer genotype is directly clinically significant for atomoxetine dosing even without any interacting CYP2D6-inhibitor medication, since the genotype alone produces the same fivefold AUC/Cmax elevation that drug-induced phenocopying produces in a genetically normal patient. COMLEX: Approximately 7 to 10 percent of white patients and 3 to 8 percent of African American patients are poor metabolizers, making pre-treatment awareness of this phenotype clinically relevant in a meaningful minority of patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — extends the CYP450 drug-drug-interaction framework into recognizing that genetically determined CYP2D6 poor-metabolizer status alone, independent of any interacting drug, carries the same clinical significance for atomoxetine dosing","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CYP2D6 genotype DOES have an e","B":"Atomoxetine is not described a"}},{"id":"sdl37_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Predict how CYP2D6 genetic variations and concurrent CYP2D6 inhibitor therapy (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) alter the plasma concentrations and toxicity risk of atomoxetine.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient stable on atomoxetine 80 mg/day is about to start bupropion for smoking cessation. Bupropion is also a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor. What is the most appropriate proactive management step regarding the atomoxetine dose?","choices":{"A":"Take no proactive step, since bupropion does not inhibit CYP2D6","B":"Preemptively increase the atomoxetine dose to counteract a drop in levels","C":"Discontinue atomoxetine permanently before starting bupropion","D":"Anticipate a rise in atomoxetine levels and consider a preemptive dose reduction","E":"Expect the interaction to involve other SSRIs but not atomoxetine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bupropion is a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor, sharing this property with fluoxetine and paroxetine, and would be expected to raise this patient's atomoxetine plasma concentrations toward poor-metabolizer-range levels through the same phenocopying mechanism, so the appropriate proactive step is anticipating this rise and considering a preemptive atomoxetine dose reduction with close monitoring for dose-related adverse effects — exactly this management approach. Bupropion DOES have a significant, potent effect on CYP2D6 and, by extension, atomoxetine levels; no proactive step being needed directly contradicts this established interaction. Bupropion's CYP2D6 inhibition would raise, not lower, atomoxetine levels, so preemptively increasing the atomoxetine dose to counteract an anticipated drop is based on the opposite pharmacokinetic direction from what actually occurs. Permanent atomoxetine discontinuation is not described as mandatory before starting bupropion; careful dose adjustment and monitoring, not automatic discontinuation, is the appropriate management approach for this interaction. Bupropion's CYP2D6-inhibiting effect is not limited to SSRIs; it is specifically named as a drug that can raise atomoxetine levels through this same mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bupropion, alongside fluoxetine and paroxetine, is a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor capable of raising atomoxetine plasma concentrations through the same phenocopying mechanism, requiring proactive dose consideration when starting bupropion in a patient already stable on atomoxetine. COMLEX: This same principle underlies why atomoxetine's antidepressant-like pharmacology has led some clinicians to combine it with SSRIs or bupropion, making awareness of this specific pharmacokinetic interaction clinically important in that combined-use context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — extends the CYP450 drug-drug-interaction framework into proactively managing bupropion's potent CYP2D6-inhibition effect on atomoxetine levels before starting a new interacting agent in an already-stable patient","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bupropion DOES have a signific","E":"Bupropion's CYP2D6-inhibiting "}}]};
